00:06:05.497 --> 00:06:08.667 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we have in
America or in Europe?

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:11.003
I mean, is this something
that, you said in built?

00:06:12.037 --> 00:06:13.439
Is it really?

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:14.507
- I'm not aware of
all the studies,

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.642
I think red for
sure is something

00:06:16.675 --> 00:06:20.780 align:start size:97% position:3%
for which we have a relatively
innate attentional bias,

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:24.483 align:start size:88% position:13%
red processeses salient
in the environment.

00:06:24.516 --> 00:06:26.986
- This is so much of it
depends on the person,

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:30.589
like there could be a
person here in America

00:06:30.622 --> 00:06:32.091
for whom red is really calming

00:06:32.124 --> 00:06:34.693 align:start size:91% position:9%
because of their
particular circumstances.

00:06:34.726 --> 00:06:35.560
- Yeah, it's actually funny,

00:06:35.594 --> 00:06:37.463 align:start size:81% position:19%
I just got back from
China yesterday.

00:06:37.496 --> 00:06:38.597
So I flew in.

00:06:38.631 --> 00:06:39.799
And one of the things you
notice even from the sky,

00:06:39.832 --> 00:06:41.200
looking down over Chinese cities

00:06:41.233 --> 00:06:45.104
is how much red lighting
there is, big red LEDs.

00:06:45.137 --> 00:06:47.773
And so I associate red
with stop, like if red,

00:06:47.806 --> 00:06:50.543
it's meant to catch
my attention, It's
sort of alarming.

00:06:50.576 --> 00:06:51.811 align:start size:84% position:16%
But in China actually,
it's ubiquitous.

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:54.947
And it's more just like
a color of good luck.

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.983
Most restaurants
have their title

00:06:58.016 --> 00:06:59.985
of the restaurant in red.

00:07:00.018 --> 00:07:01.954
So it's also meant to
catch your attention,

00:07:01.987 --> 00:07:04.723
to simulate arousal but in
less of like a negative way

00:07:04.756 --> 00:07:07.626
that it can sometimes be
here in the United States,

00:07:07.659 --> 00:07:09.528 align:start size:88% position:13%
so red street sign isn't
necessarily gonna be.

00:07:09.561 --> 00:07:11.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So probably the emotional
valence is conditioned

00:07:11.997 --> 00:07:14.633 align:start size:91% position:9%
and culturally
contingent on some level.

00:07:14.666 --> 00:07:16.535
But that red is
gonna be salient,

00:07:16.568 --> 00:07:19.038
is gonna be something that we
can automatically attend to.

00:07:19.071 --> 00:07:20.239
That's fairly universal.

00:07:20.272 --> 00:07:23.108
- And once we attend to it,
how we feel may depend a lot

00:07:23.141 --> 00:07:24.543
on the culture that we're from.

00:07:24.576 --> 00:07:25.978
- Right.

00:07:26.011 --> 00:07:28.647 align:start position:0%
- But I think the point is
that even things like the color

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:29.748
and size and shape of a pill

00:07:29.781 --> 00:07:32.852
and who gives it to you
will affect how it works.

00:07:32.885 --> 00:07:35.688
It's not just the chemical
properties of the medicine,

00:07:35.721 --> 00:07:38.691
there's so much more, so
much in the mental world.

00:07:38.724 --> 00:07:40.826
- Well, I mean, but that also
is chemical ultimately, right?

00:07:40.859 --> 00:07:43.529 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because if it's a
blue pill or red pill,

00:07:43.562 --> 00:07:45.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's gonna affect your
neuro chemistry in some way.

00:07:45.664 --> 00:07:47.633
- And you know what,
why is it red or blue?

00:07:47.666 --> 00:07:50.569
Because of the physical
shape of the molecules,

00:07:50.602 --> 00:07:51.604
The shape?
- Right.

00:07:51.637 --> 00:07:52.572
- Wow!

00:07:52.605 --> 00:07:55.608
So, right, I guess this
is gonna be a question

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:56.442
I wanna keep coming back to

00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:58.544
how do we really
define a placebo?

00:07:58.577 --> 00:08:01.146
Because I think in the episode,

00:08:01.179 --> 00:08:04.550 align:start size:94% position:6%
I say something like
there's no active ingredient

00:08:04.583 --> 00:08:06.552
that should cause that effect.

00:08:06.585 --> 00:08:08.787
But yet, if a pill is calming,

00:08:08.820 --> 00:08:09.955
because it happens to be blue,

00:08:09.988 --> 00:08:11.290
because its molecular structure

00:08:11.323 --> 00:08:12.992
reflects blue light the best,

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:16.095
then there is something
chemically in that pill

00:08:16.128 --> 00:08:17.796
that works in calming you.

00:08:17.829 --> 00:08:20.833
And it's not because
the ingestion helps,

00:08:20.866 --> 00:08:22.535
it's just the color alone.

00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:23.636
- Right, right.

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:26.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
I guess it's easier to
define what's not a placebo,

00:08:26.171 --> 00:08:28.641
as something that works
better than a placebo?

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:31.143
- Well, that's why another
term for placebo effects

00:08:31.176 --> 00:08:34.246
are nonspecific effects,
or nonspecific factors.

00:08:34.279 --> 00:08:36.549
So whatever factors
involved in healing

00:08:36.582 --> 00:08:39.552
that we cannot attribute
to the chemical substance

00:08:39.585 --> 00:08:41.954
that is targeted
in the treatment.

00:08:41.987 --> 00:08:44.657 align:start position:0%
And there are also non specific
effects in psychotherapy

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:48.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
even that are, so tone of
voice setting, waiting times.

00:08:49.795 --> 00:08:51.830 align:start size:81% position:19%
So placebo is
really a filler term

00:08:51.863 --> 00:08:56.001 align:start size:88% position:13%
for all these different
psychosocial symbolic
ritual factors

00:08:56.034 --> 00:08:57.570
that we don't fully understand,

00:08:57.603 --> 00:09:00.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
but that we know contribute
to a cure somehow.

00:09:00.205 --> 00:09:01.306
- Right?

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:05.244 align:start size:94% position:6%
So those are called non
specific, the specific ones.

00:09:05.277 --> 00:09:07.580
How are they defined they are.

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:08.881
- So the specific one would be

00:09:08.914 --> 00:09:11.150
the actual analgesic
property of a pill,

00:09:11.183 --> 00:09:13.152
for example, has been
well studied in RCT,

00:09:13.185 --> 00:09:15.888
we know it produces that effect.

00:09:15.921 --> 00:09:18.691
But then we know that there
are other effects around

00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:21.827 align:start size:91% position:9%
and beyond that, that also
contribute to healing.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.630 align:start size:91% position:9%
And in the case of an
actual placebo procedure,

00:09:24.663 --> 00:09:27.166
where we know that
specific molecule

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:28.400 align:start size:81% position:19%
is not actually
present in the pill,

00:09:28.434 --> 00:09:32.705 align:start size:97% position:3%
but healing will still happen,
then we need to investigate

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:34.907
these different effects,
these different factors.

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:36.342
That are nonspecific.

00:09:36.375 --> 00:09:39.678 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Maybe this works,
a nonspecific,

00:09:39.711 --> 00:09:43.816 align:start size:97% position:3%
non placebo effect of a
medicine would be what works,

00:09:43.849 --> 00:09:46.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
even when it's administered
to someone in a coma,

00:09:46.284 --> 00:09:47.653
who doesn't know
what's happening,

00:09:47.686 --> 00:09:49.788
doesn't know who's
administering it.

00:09:49.821 --> 00:09:53.158
But we know that that
molecule in the blood

00:09:53.191 --> 00:09:55.995 align:start size:81% position:19%
causes blood
pressure to go down,

00:09:56.028 --> 00:09:59.898
or whatever, causes pain to
be felt less, I don't know.

00:09:59.931 --> 00:10:02.368
- But be careful
because there are,

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:04.370 align:start size:88% position:13%
placebo effects have
been found, for example,

00:10:04.403 --> 00:10:06.972
in nonverbal autistic children,

00:10:07.005 --> 00:10:10.376
who for sure cannot understand
the nature of the suggestion,

00:10:10.409 --> 00:10:13.379
because, of course, if you
have some kind of an idea

00:10:13.412 --> 00:10:17.249
about a therapeutic target
and a mechanism of action,

00:10:17.282 --> 00:10:18.417
so I have a headache,

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:19.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
and this one pill
removes the headache,

00:10:19.551 --> 00:10:22.121 align:start size:94% position:6%
then that will greatly
help in the placebo effect.

00:10:22.154 --> 00:10:25.691
But what about the case of
autistic nonverbal children

00:10:25.724 --> 00:10:28.293
who have no idea, who
don't expect anything,

00:10:28.326 --> 00:10:29.494
and yet there's an effect,

00:10:29.527 --> 00:10:32.364
there's a social cognitive
component to placebo effects,

00:10:32.397 --> 00:10:33.365
you have to be able to expect

00:10:33.398 --> 00:10:36.301
what other people expect
of you even implicitly.

00:10:36.334 --> 00:10:38.704
So probably in the case of the
nonverbal autistic children,

00:10:38.737 --> 00:10:41.440
it's also say, the reassuring
tone of voice of the parent,

00:10:41.473 --> 00:10:43.409 align:start size:81% position:19%
the parents shifted
expectations,

00:10:43.442 --> 00:10:46.045
the sort of contagious
hope that might work.

00:10:46.078 --> 00:10:49.782
And the same mechanism,
same social mechanism

00:10:49.815 --> 00:10:52.985
