Because how can it be safe?

00:20:19.517 --> 00:20:21.519
We're in the basement
of a neuroscience lab,

00:20:21.552 --> 00:20:24.522
and this machine is really big
and noises are being played.

00:20:24.555 --> 00:20:25.991
- Not even that they would ask,

00:20:26.024 --> 00:20:28.827
so did you find out what's
wrong with my child's brain?

00:20:28.860 --> 00:20:31.563
They wanted us to comment
on neuro imaging results.

00:20:31.596 --> 00:20:33.598
And we would have to
pull them aside again

00:20:33.631 --> 00:20:34.799
and say do to remember?

00:20:34.832 --> 00:20:36.601 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're not doing
actual neuro imaging,

00:20:36.634 --> 00:20:39.537 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're not, you've competent
to comment on findings

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:41.706 align:start size:88% position:13%
that are not even there,
they would forget.

00:20:41.739 --> 00:20:43.475
- They would forget,

00:20:43.508 --> 00:20:44.576
- They would forget because
all the queues are in place.

00:20:44.609 --> 00:20:49.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think it would demand
so much mental effort

00:20:49.314 --> 00:20:51.349
to remember that this is sham,

00:20:51.382 --> 00:20:54.653
that people revert to a kind
of adaptive self deception.

00:20:54.686 --> 00:20:56.655 align:start size:81% position:19%
So this is also what
we're aiming for.

00:20:56.688 --> 00:20:58.556
We're aiming to tap into
the children's ability

00:20:58.589 --> 00:21:01.793
to self deceive, which we
know can be very effective,

00:21:01.826 --> 00:21:03.628 align:start size:84% position:16%
like say, if you're
really, really nervous

00:21:03.661 --> 00:21:05.664
before giving a talk,
and then you managed

00:21:05.697 --> 00:21:07.465 align:start size:88% position:13%
to convince yourself
that you're not nervous,

00:21:07.498 --> 00:21:10.302
then lo and behold, you
start feeling confident.

00:21:10.335 --> 00:21:13.004 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now initially, there might
have been some self deception.

00:21:13.037 --> 00:21:14.906
But then if the
results are good,

00:21:14.939 --> 00:21:16.675
then it's no longer deception.

00:21:16.708 --> 00:21:19.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
- This is what I really
love about all the episodes

00:21:19.444 --> 00:21:21.680
and all the work that
we've done together.

00:21:21.713 --> 00:21:25.550
Because we also worked
on the reverse exorcism

00:21:25.583 --> 00:21:28.687
putting a spirit in
someone that, okay.

00:21:30.621 --> 00:21:33.692 align:start size:84% position:16%
In a way, we're almost
finding some truth

00:21:33.725 --> 00:21:35.894
to pseudo scientific practices.

00:21:35.927 --> 00:21:40.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
If I have some mental block
or some behavior I don't want.

00:21:42.133 --> 00:21:46.371
And my mother takes me
to a person who prays

00:21:46.404 --> 00:21:49.774
to crystals and whatever,

00:21:49.807 --> 00:21:51.943
that crystal has no
chemical mechanism

00:21:51.976 --> 00:21:53.678
with which to help me.

00:21:53.711 --> 00:21:58.450
However, if I truly believe
that this is significant,

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:00.585
and I noticed that
I'm being cared for

00:22:00.618 --> 00:22:01.853
that people care so much,

00:22:01.886 --> 00:22:03.688 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I think so much
more about my behavior,

00:22:03.721 --> 00:22:07.659
because of all of that, I
can actually get better.

00:22:07.692 --> 00:22:08.593
And that's what
we're doing here.

00:22:08.627 --> 00:22:11.596
But instead of using
crystals, and whatever,

00:22:11.629 --> 00:22:16.635
we're using science looking
stuff, lab coats, and an MRI,

00:22:17.835 --> 00:22:22.040
and imaging of brains on
the computer screens around

00:22:22.073 --> 00:22:24.676
and it just feels like this
must be serious and real.

00:22:24.709 --> 00:22:27.545
But in reality, they may
as well be laying on a bed

00:22:27.578 --> 00:22:30.515 align:start size:88% position:13%
while someone sings
nonsense words to them.

00:22:30.548 --> 00:22:32.650 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And I like the
parallel that you draw.

00:22:32.683 --> 00:22:35.086 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's true that a lot of
spiritual healing practices

00:22:35.119 --> 00:22:38.890
they draw on these
transitional objects,

00:22:38.923 --> 00:22:40.992
you could call them so
things like amulets,

00:22:41.025 --> 00:22:43.728 align:start size:81% position:19%
or stones or rocks,
or even magic,

00:22:43.761 --> 00:22:47.732
crosses that people can sort
of attribute healing power to

00:22:47.765 --> 00:22:49.534
without having to worry,

00:22:49.567 --> 00:22:52.837
it becomes a convenient way
to instill hope in people.

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:55.473 align:start size:84% position:16%
- All right, let's get
right back into it.

00:22:55.506 --> 00:22:57.575
- I hope he heals me
with my concussions

00:22:57.608 --> 00:22:59.678
and now I hope my
headaches go away.

00:23:01.179 --> 00:23:03.982
- I was about to take part
in something remarkable.

00:23:04.015 --> 00:23:07.685 align:start size:97% position:3%
The very first use of
accessory assisted suggestion

00:23:07.718 --> 00:23:10.522 align:start size:84% position:16%
on children with these
kinds of symptoms.

00:23:10.555 --> 00:23:13.892
Mind Field would play an
integral role in the study,

00:23:13.925 --> 00:23:17.896
and the results could be new
and significant for science.

00:23:17.929 --> 00:23:20.732
(upbeat music)

00:23:21.666 --> 00:23:23.601
On the day of the first session,

00:23:23.634 --> 00:23:25.637
several measures
were put in place

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:28.873 align:start size:91% position:9%
to heighten the children's
neuro enchantment.

00:23:28.906 --> 00:23:29.741
- Nathan.

00:23:30.942 --> 00:23:32.076
- Nathan!

00:23:32.109 --> 00:23:33.712 align:start size:81% position:19%
- [Michael] A
friendly fake nurse,

00:23:34.879 --> 00:23:38.550
a walk down a long,
impressive hallway

00:23:38.583 --> 00:23:39.784
(Malaya exhaling)

00:23:39.818 --> 00:23:43.722
And a 10 minute anticipation
building wait outside the lab,

00:23:44.689 --> 00:23:47.125 align:start size:81% position:19%
then it was time for
their first scan.

00:23:47.158 --> 00:23:48.560
- [Doctor] Shall we?

00:23:48.593 --> 00:23:49.461
- [Michael] While the hypothesis

00:23:49.494 --> 00:23:51.729 align:start size:81% position:19%
of this study was
grounded in science.

00:23:51.762 --> 00:23:53.932 align:start size:84% position:16%
This was Dr. Veissière
and Jay Olsen's

00:23:53.965 --> 00:23:56.067
first time testing their theory

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:58.970 align:start size:81% position:19%
on real children
with real disorders.

00:23:59.003 --> 00:24:01.172 align:start size:81% position:19%
And they didn't know
if it would work.

00:24:01.205 --> 00:24:02.340 align:start size:78% position:22%
- You may lay down
if you'd like.

00:24:02.373 --> 00:24:04.742 align:start position:0% line:0%
- I have a question, so it
seems to me that placebos work,

00:24:04.775 --> 00:24:06.678 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
in part, because
people these subjects

00:24:06.711 --> 00:24:08.847 line:0%
have a lot of faith
in medical science.

00:24:10.081 --> 00:24:10.982 line:0%
Did you study that at all?

00:24:11.015 --> 00:24:12.584 line:0%
Or ask them at all about that?

00:24:12.617 --> 00:24:15.587 align:start size:81% position:19%
I mean, and I guess
my question is,

00:24:16.754 --> 00:24:18.623 align:start size:97% position:3%
for somebody who's very
skeptical of medical science,

00:24:18.656 --> 00:24:21.960
who doesn't feel like doctors
are working to their benefit,

00:24:21.993 --> 00:24:24.629
would they still be as
susceptible to a placebo?

00:24:24.662 --> 00:24:26.564
- Well, a lot of
people might say

00:24:26.597 --> 00:24:28.066
they're critical
of medical science.

00:24:28.099 --> 00:24:30.535
But for example, if they
have acute appendicitis,

00:24:30.568 --> 00:24:34.205 align:start size:91% position:9%
their first intuition
will be to rush to the ER.

00:24:34.238 --> 00:24:37.675 align:start size:81% position:19%
And for belief to
be really effective,

00:24:37.708 --> 00:24:39.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
it needs to be
culturally widespread.

00:24:39.777 --> 00:24:41.846
And we happen to live
in a historical moment

00:24:41.879 --> 00:24:46.651
where most of the solutions
to our everyday ailments

00:24:47.818 --> 00:24:49.554
are deemed to be
found in medicine,

00:24:49.587 --> 00:24:51.856 align:start size:91% position:9%
and children are receptive
to these kinds of cues,

00:24:51.889 --> 00:24:53.992
even implicitly, even if they
don't know that they know.

00:24:54.025 --> 00:24:56.828
So I think, try it out
at home for example,

00:24:56.861 --> 00:24:58.530
if you have like a
five year old niece,

00:24:58.563 --> 00:25:00.999
show her a picture of a builder,

00:25:01.032 --> 00:25:03.167
and then a doctor
with a stethoscope

00:25:03.200 --> 00:25:06.070 align:start size:91% position:9%
and ask your niece, which
one is the smartest?

00:25:06.103 --> 00:25:08.072
For sure they're gonna
point to the doctor,

00:25:08.105 --> 00:25:10.208
because they've picked up
on a cultural consensus

00:25:10.241 --> 00:25:14.045 align:start size:91% position:9%
that this is where,
elaborate knowledge lies.

00:25:14.078 --> 00:25:17.048 align:start size:91% position:9%
At the same time, I'm
not aware of any studies,

00:25:17.081 --> 00:25:19.284 align:start size:78% position:22%
testing medical
placebos on people

00:25:19.317 --> 00:25:21.286 align:start size:84% position:16%
who are really
skeptical of medicine

00:25:21.319 --> 00:25:23.288
who are completely into
alternative medicine,

00:25:23.321 --> 00:25:25.990 align:start size:88% position:13%
it may be that it
wouldn't work with them.

00:25:26.023 --> 00:25:28.159
I think it's a good hypothesis.

00:25:28.192 --> 00:25:30.762
- Also, is there such a
thing as an anti placebo?

00:25:30.795 --> 00:25:32.697
Where (laughs) and
what I mean by that?

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:35.133
- For sure.
- Yeah okay.

00:25:35.166 --> 00:25:38.736 align:start size:88% position:13%
So that's like somebody
who's skeptical,

00:25:38.769 --> 00:25:40.271
so even something that's potent,

00:25:40.304 --> 00:25:43.141
even if you give them a potent
drug, they don't react to it,

00:25:43.174 --> 00:25:44.275
because they're so
skeptical about it--

00:25:44.308 --> 00:25:45.577 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, right,
that's another thing,

00:25:45.610 --> 00:25:47.312
nocebo is something different.

00:25:47.345 --> 00:25:48.513
What is that, though?

00:25:48.546 --> 00:25:53.618 align:start size:97% position:3%
Where, because of all the
done specific crap surrounding

00:25:53.651 --> 00:25:57.689 align:start position:0%
a chemical mechanism that
should work, it works less well,

00:25:58.389 --> 00:26:01.092
because of the context.

00:26:01.125 --> 00:26:02.360
- That's really interesting.

00:26:02.393 --> 00:26:04.762 align:start size:84% position:16%
Yeah, that might very,
very interesting.

00:26:04.795 --> 00:26:06.264
And it may be that people

00:26:06.297 --> 00:26:08.666
who are a little too high
in analytical reasoning,

00:26:08.699 --> 00:26:10.368
people who are not
very suggestible,

00:26:10.401 --> 00:26:13.371 align:start size:91% position:9%
people who are not very
prone to social influence,

00:26:13.404 --> 00:26:17.375
may also reverse placebo
themselves inadvertently,

00:26:17.408 --> 00:26:18.576
because they're too critical

00:26:18.609 --> 00:26:22.680
because they can't let the
self regulatory magic happen.

00:26:22.713 --> 00:26:24.849
But it could be that
at the social level,

00:26:24.882 --> 00:26:26.951
or naive beliefs in
some things like say,

00:26:26.984 --> 00:26:28.319
some categories of illness

00:26:28.352 --> 00:26:30.722 align:start size:91% position:9%
that may not be
scientifically validated.

00:26:30.755 --> 00:26:32.657 align:start position:0%
But just because people believe
they have these symptoms,

00:26:32.690 --> 00:26:34.626
like say, certain
food intolerances,

00:26:34.659 --> 00:26:37.662
or certain allergies that
appear to be on the rise.

00:26:37.695 --> 00:26:39.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, there's some
psychogenic factors involved

00:26:39.664 --> 00:26:40.965
in that and then might
have something to do

00:26:40.998 --> 00:26:43.668
with collective nocebo effects.

00:26:43.701 --> 00:26:46.838
And then the next problem
is also letting people know

00:26:46.871 --> 00:26:49.841 align:start position:0%
that they're genetically at
risk for a particular condition,

00:26:49.874 --> 00:26:52.377 align:start size:84% position:16%
sometimes may
trigger the symptoms,

00:26:52.410 --> 00:26:54.379 align:start size:81% position:19%
or may even trigger
the condition

00:26:54.412 --> 00:26:58.049
in a way that not knowing
might have been better.

00:26:58.082 --> 00:26:59.417
- Ignorance is bliss--

00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:00.351
- Ignorance is often bliss.

00:27:00.385 --> 00:27:02.420 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I think it's very
ethically tricky, then.

00:27:02.453 --> 00:27:03.688
- It's very ethically tricky,

00:27:03.721 --> 00:27:05.757
because you have to tell people,

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:08.993 align:start size:97% position:3%
but then there are also risks
associated with telling.

00:27:09.026 --> 00:27:09.994
- That's why you
might not wanna do

00:27:10.027 --> 00:27:11.396
a personal genetics test,

00:27:11.429 --> 00:27:13.097
- Right?

00:27:13.130 --> 00:27:14.999
Well, we're about to test
that in the lab as well,

00:27:15.032 --> 00:27:16.334
because we think
there's interesting

00:27:16.367 --> 00:27:17.869
neuro enchantment effects

00:27:17.902 --> 00:27:20.138 align:start size:81% position:19%
associated with
belief in genetics.

00:27:20.171 --> 00:27:22.306
So we're about to do a study

00:27:22.339 --> 00:27:24.809
with Sham personalized feedback.

00:27:24.842 --> 00:27:27.912
So Sham, swab and genetic.

00:27:32.950 --> 00:27:35.219 align:start size:81% position:19%
it's a placebo
analgesia condition.

00:27:35.252 --> 00:27:37.455
So we give people
a shock and pain.

00:27:37.488 --> 00:27:40.692 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we tell them that
in one condition,

00:27:40.725 --> 00:27:42.427
we've tailored the machine

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:45.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
to one particular genetic
polymorphous that they have,

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:47.765
and we fully expect
that is gonna work

00:27:47.798 --> 00:27:50.702 align:start size:97% position:3%
better in that condition,
but we haven't tested it yet.

00:27:50.735 --> 00:27:52.370 align:start size:81% position:19%
And then eventually,
one of our dreams

00:27:52.403 --> 00:27:54.439
is to also test in a kind of,

00:27:54.472 --> 00:27:57.275 align:start size:81% position:19%
three on trial to
do Sham neuroimaging

00:27:57.308 --> 00:27:59.110
versus Sham genetic testing

00:27:59.143 --> 00:28:01.079
to see which one has
the strongest effect.

00:28:01.112 --> 00:28:01.646
- Ah!

00:28:01.679 --> 00:28:03.347
What's your hypothesis?

00:28:03.380 --> 00:28:04.816
Which do you think we as
a society believe more in?

00:28:04.849 --> 00:28:08.286
Neuroimaging or genetic testing?

00:28:08.319 --> 00:28:09.420
- I'm really not sure,

00:28:09.453 --> 00:28:11.856
I think we're moving towards

00:28:11.889 --> 00:28:13.791
believing more in
genetic testing,

00:28:13.824 --> 00:28:16.861 align:start size:88% position:13%
but the belief might
still be culturally new.

00:28:16.894 --> 00:28:19.330
Whereas belief in the
power of neuroscience

00:28:19.363 --> 00:28:21.265 align:start size:84% position:16%
are very well
installed culturally,

00:28:21.298 --> 00:28:25.236
we're more than, two decades
after the decade of the brain.

00:28:25.269 --> 00:28:26.471
So I would say in 10 years,

00:28:26.504 --> 00:28:29.273
it'll probably be
genetic testing.

00:28:29.306 --> 00:28:32.977 align:start size:84% position:16%
For now, it depends
on levels of exposure.

00:28:33.010 --> 00:28:34.812
But for sure, like
say for children,

00:28:34.845 --> 00:28:37.248 align:start size:88% position:13%
the idea of a gene is
still very, very opaque,

00:28:37.281 --> 00:28:38.883
very nebulous, very esoteric,

00:28:38.916 --> 00:28:40.752
whereas putting them in
this enormous machine

00:28:40.785 --> 00:28:43.387 align:start size:84% position:16%
and this authoritative
hospital might--

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:45.923 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Might always be better than
just swabbing their mouth

00:28:45.956 --> 00:28:47.325
and telling them something.

00:28:47.358 --> 00:28:49.393
Let's get back into the
episode, you guys ready?

00:28:49.426 --> 00:28:50.895
Okay, here we go.

00:28:50.928 --> 00:28:52.864
One of my roles was
to help build up

00:28:52.897 --> 00:28:56.067
the children's belief
that it would work.

00:28:56.100 --> 00:29:00.004 line:0%
As you go into the machine,
you'll relax more and more.

00:29:00.037 --> 00:29:03.841 line:0%
Would you like to relax
slowly or quickly today?

00:29:03.874 --> 00:29:05.309 line:0%
- I can go in quickly,

00:29:05.342 --> 00:29:07.211 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Quickly?
- Oh, Wow, nice.

00:29:07.244 --> 00:29:08.345
- Very good.

00:29:08.379 --> 00:29:12.049
Although the MRI scanner
was completely deactivated,

00:29:12.082 --> 00:29:14.786 align:start size:97% position:3%
we needed the kids to believe
it was fully functional.

00:29:17.421 --> 00:29:19.924
So Jay played a series of
realistic sound effects

00:29:19.957 --> 00:29:22.426
to signify that the
machine was working.

00:29:22.459 --> 00:29:24.796 align:start size:84% position:16%
While Dr. Veissière
planted the suggestion

00:29:24.829 --> 00:29:26.497
that it would help
the patient heal.

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:31.502
- You may notice some unusual
feelings, perhaps a tingling,

00:29:31.535 --> 00:29:35.373 align:start size:88% position:13%
your brain is continuing
to learn and to heal.

00:29:35.406 --> 00:29:40.311 align:start position:0%
And to help you find this
constant feeling of confidence.

00:29:41.445 --> 00:29:42.413
- [Michael] If all went well,

00:29:42.446 --> 00:29:45.082
the placebo effect of
the Sham brain scanner

00:29:45.115 --> 00:29:47.819 align:start size:91% position:9%
would convince the
children's conscious minds

00:29:47.852 --> 00:29:49.854 align:start size:84% position:16%
that real neuroscience
was at work.

00:29:49.887 --> 00:29:51.989
- A deep, deep breath.

00:29:52.022 --> 00:29:54.458 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- [Michael] This would allow
their subconscious minds

00:29:54.491 --> 00:29:58.229 line:0%
to harness the power of their
brains to heal themselves.

00:29:58.262 --> 00:30:00.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
- For people with migraines,
it's often in the front

00:30:00.531 --> 00:30:03.034
or the back part of the
head but it could also be

00:30:03.067 --> 00:30:04.936
like a feeling like
outside the head.

00:30:04.969 --> 00:30:06.604
So I'm not sure what it's
gonna be like for you.

00:30:06.637 --> 00:30:08.139
We'll find out afterwards.

00:30:08.172 --> 00:30:09.474
So we'll now slide you in.

00:30:10.641 --> 00:30:12.176 line:0%
- Cool, it's like
a roller coaster.

00:30:12.209 --> 00:30:13.911 line:0%
I love roller coasters.

00:30:13.944 --> 00:30:16.113 line:0%
- The deeper you go in,
the more you'll relax.

00:30:16.146 --> 00:30:17.882
- Okay, I wanna go
all the way deep

00:30:21.051 --> 00:30:23.221 line:0%
I feel more stronger beeps.

00:30:23.254 --> 00:30:24.455 line:0%
- I know

00:30:24.488 --> 00:30:26.424
this is because you're
already at level three.

00:30:26.457 --> 00:30:30.528
You might notice a deeper
feeling of relaxation.

00:30:31.595 --> 00:30:34.065 line:0%
- [Nathan] Yes, I can feel it.

00:30:34.098 --> 00:30:36.367 line:0%
Can we go a little
bit deeper please?

00:30:36.400 --> 00:30:37.202 line:0%
- [Michael] Yes.

00:30:39.403 --> 00:30:41.005 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
- [Nathan] I want
my whole entire feet

00:30:41.939 --> 00:30:43.140 line:0%
Nice.

00:30:43.173 --> 00:30:44.374 align:start size:81% position:19%
- [Michael] The
scientists at McGill

00:30:44.408 --> 00:30:47.545
believe the children's brains
have always had the ability

00:30:47.578 --> 00:30:49.580
to control their symptoms.

00:30:49.613 --> 00:30:52.450
The children just needed
to believe it themselves.

00:30:52.483 --> 00:30:54.285
(Nathan sneezing)

00:30:54.318 --> 00:30:55.253
- [Dr. Veissière]
Yeah, so the beeps

00:30:55.286 --> 00:30:57.421
can sometimes make people sneeze

00:30:57.454 --> 00:31:00.992 align:start size:91% position:9%
as a sign that they're
relaxing just very deeply.

00:31:03.060 --> 00:31:04.195
Right?

00:31:04.228 --> 00:31:06.464 align:start size:88% position:13%
Very well, you did
very, very well Nathan.

00:31:06.497 --> 00:31:09.267
- [Michael] The children all
appear to enjoy the procedure

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:11.068
and the researchers
and I did our best

00:31:11.101 --> 00:31:13.404
to reinforce their
neuro enchantment.

00:31:13.437 --> 00:31:15.539
- So when you heard the
the first set of beeps

00:31:15.572 --> 00:31:17.441
and the second set of beeps

00:31:17.474 --> 00:31:20.111
Did you notice feeling them in
different parts of your body?

00:31:20.144 --> 00:31:22.046
- Well, the first time
I went in the machine,

00:31:22.079 --> 00:31:23.547
I kind of felt the hair.

00:31:23.580 --> 00:31:24.414
- Okay, good.

00:31:24.448 --> 00:31:27.318
- What level was
she taken to today?

00:31:27.351 --> 00:31:28.586
- Level two.

00:31:28.619 --> 00:31:29.720
- Level two!

00:31:29.754 --> 00:31:31.289
- [Dr. Veissière] Yeah,
level two is great, yeah.

00:31:31.322 --> 00:31:32.556
- [Michael] Yeah.

00:31:32.589 --> 00:31:33.423
- When I was in the
machine, I felt like

00:31:33.457 --> 00:31:35.559 align:start size:84% position:16%
I was going backwards
and forwards.

00:31:35.592 --> 00:31:38.529
- Okay, that's very good,
that's very good sign.

00:31:38.562 --> 00:31:40.464 line:0%
You might have noticed
how you were yawning.

00:31:40.497 --> 00:31:41.900 line:0%
- I wanna go to sleep.

00:31:43.200 --> 00:31:44.969
- That's great.

00:31:45.002 --> 00:31:46.504
- [Michael] For a kid
with hyperactivity,

00:31:46.537 --> 00:31:49.507
Nathan was already
appearing calmer.

00:31:49.540 --> 00:31:52.109
But before our young
subjects left the lab,

00:31:52.142 --> 00:31:55.246 align:start position:0%
Dr. Veissière bolstered the
suggestion that today's session

00:31:55.279 --> 00:31:58.582
would help them continue
their healing process.

00:31:58.615 --> 00:32:01.652 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So I know for sure that
you're gonna be more relaxed,

00:32:01.685 --> 00:32:05.589 align:start size:97% position:3%
much, much less anxious, I
would not be surprised at all.

00:32:05.622 --> 00:32:09.493 align:start size:84% position:16%
If the scratching
really diminished to
no scratching at all.

00:32:09.526 --> 00:32:11.495
The amazing thing
about the brain

00:32:11.528 --> 00:32:14.966 align:start size:97% position:3%
is that it has this fantastic
power to heal itself.

00:32:14.999 --> 00:32:16.634
But now what we've
been able to do here

00:32:16.667 --> 00:32:19.470 align:start size:94% position:6%
with the power of suggestion
is to get your brain

00:32:19.503 --> 00:32:22.606
to work faster and
better all the time.

00:32:22.639 --> 00:32:24.075
So how do you feel?

00:32:24.108 --> 00:32:25.042
- Amazing.

00:32:25.075 --> 00:32:26.644
- [Dr. Veissière] Oh, nice.

00:32:26.677 --> 00:32:28.579
- [Michael] Mind Field
provided the Raz Lab

00:32:28.612 --> 00:32:32.016 align:start size:78% position:22%
with Octopus by
Joy, Smartwatches.

00:32:32.049 --> 00:32:33.250
- [Nathan] Lefty rules.

00:32:33.283 --> 00:32:34.552
- [Michael] They were
specially programmed

00:32:34.585 --> 00:32:37.054 align:start size:84% position:16%
to remind the children
in between visits,

00:32:37.087 --> 00:32:40.024
that their brain
was healing itself.

00:32:40.057 --> 00:32:43.060
- Just having the watch with
you will make you feel better.

00:32:43.093 --> 00:32:45.463
But it's not the watch,
or it's not the machine

00:32:45.496 --> 00:32:48.499 align:start size:91% position:9%
that's making you feel
good, it's your own brain.

00:32:48.532 --> 00:32:51.235
- Okay, I just stopped
it, because I wanna say,

00:32:51.268 --> 00:32:53.304
at this time when
this was filmed,

00:32:53.337 --> 00:32:57.074
smart watches were like a
whole cool brand new thing.

00:32:57.107 --> 00:32:59.377
Everyone was excited,
the kids were like Oh!

00:32:59.410 --> 00:33:02.713
And we had to be like, it's
not like an Apple Watch,

00:33:02.746 --> 00:33:05.616
it's just for your brain
health, which is better.

00:33:05.649 --> 00:33:06.517
Tell me about the watches,

00:33:06.550 --> 00:33:07.451 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I don't
think in the episode,

00:33:07.484 --> 00:33:10.354
we focused enough on
what their role was.

00:33:10.387 --> 00:33:12.356
- The idea was to work
on placebo conditioning.

00:33:12.389 --> 00:33:15.326
So first we enlisted a
response in the scanner,

00:33:15.359 --> 00:33:17.561 align:start size:84% position:16%
with a mixture of
hypnotic suggestions,

00:33:17.594 --> 00:33:19.463
anticipation and all the
effects that you showed.

00:33:19.496 --> 00:33:23.634
Now once we've found a target
response, like say relaxation,

00:33:23.667 --> 00:33:26.170 align:start size:78% position:22%
or self regulation
some other way,

00:33:26.203 --> 00:33:27.605 align:start size:81% position:19%
then we get the
kids to expect that

00:33:27.638 --> 00:33:29.473 align:start size:91% position:9%
when they receive a little
buzz from the watch.

00:33:29.506 --> 00:33:31.575
They're going to
keep experiencing

00:33:31.608 --> 00:33:33.310 align:start size:78% position:22%
whatever effect
we were listening.

00:33:33.343 --> 00:33:38.349 align:start position:0%
So usually respect, relaxation,
focus, and so forth.

00:33:39.516 --> 00:33:41.385 align:start size:94% position:6%
So then we programmed
different delivery schedules

00:33:41.418 --> 00:33:43.421 align:start size:94% position:6%
for the children where they
would get a little buzz

00:33:43.454 --> 00:33:44.655
and a little friendly icon

00:33:44.689 --> 00:33:47.591
and then it would remind them
Oh, I guess I'm doing great.

00:33:47.624 --> 00:33:48.526
And then we had them come back

00:33:48.559 --> 00:33:50.127
after two weeks in the library,

00:33:50.160 --> 00:33:52.596 align:start size:97% position:3%
I would decrease the delivery
schedule of the buzzes

00:33:52.629 --> 00:33:55.499
because I didn't want them to
become too addicted to them.

00:33:55.532 --> 00:33:57.134 align:start size:81% position:19%
And eventually
after a third week,

00:33:57.167 --> 00:33:58.402
there would be no more buzzes.

00:33:58.435 --> 00:34:00.371 align:start size:84% position:16%
So they could play
around with the icons,

00:34:00.404 --> 00:34:03.508 align:start size:91% position:9%
because I didn't want them
to rely on the buzzes.

00:34:04.675 --> 00:34:06.177
But then yeah, as
you pointed out,

00:34:06.210 --> 00:34:08.379
a lot of the kids still
wanted to have the watch

00:34:08.412 --> 00:34:12.149
as a kind of a transitional
object like a little blanky,

00:34:12.182 --> 00:34:14.085 align:start size:78% position:22%
that helps you
sleep or an amulet

00:34:14.118 --> 00:34:16.287
or a special ring
that people have.

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:19.223
- You may have heard
us say a few times.

00:34:19.256 --> 00:34:20.357
Oh, you're at level three.

00:34:20.391 --> 00:34:23.394 align:start size:94% position:6%
Today, you got to level
two, is completely made up.

00:34:23.427 --> 00:34:24.762
There were no levels.

00:34:24.795 --> 00:34:29.166
But yet the kids really
read a lot into that.

00:34:29.199 --> 00:34:31.135 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And then they became
more and more proud

00:34:31.168 --> 00:34:32.803
each time they know
they went deeper.

00:34:32.836 --> 00:34:34.705
And they surrender
themselves to the--

00:34:34.738 --> 00:34:37.808 align:start position:0%
- Well, of course they did
because no matter what happened

00:34:37.841 --> 00:34:40.644
or how they felt they
were told that's good.

00:34:40.677 --> 00:34:43.481
Oh, wonderful, oh, really?

00:34:43.514 --> 00:34:45.616
- We were validating
everything they felt.

00:34:45.649 --> 00:34:46.584
- Every thing.

00:34:46.617 --> 00:34:48.119
- Was the level thing planned?

00:34:48.152 --> 00:34:49.653 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or what that
improvised on camera?

00:34:49.686 --> 00:34:52.556
- No, we kind of I think
we improvised it on camera.

00:34:52.589 --> 00:34:53.524
And then we stuck to it.

00:34:53.557 --> 00:34:54.892 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because we saw that
it worked really well.

00:34:54.925 --> 00:34:56.327
- Well, yeah,

00:34:56.360 --> 00:34:58.896
I just allowed Samuel to
guide the whole thing.

00:34:58.929 --> 00:35:00.564 align:start size:88% position:13%
I sat there and I
would just back him up.

00:35:00.597 --> 00:35:01.832
I'm wearing a lab coat as well.

00:35:01.865 --> 00:35:04.568
So I go, uh, oh wow.

00:35:04.601 --> 00:35:07.638
And it was almost hard
sometimes to not laugh

00:35:07.671 --> 00:35:08.873
because he'd be like,

00:35:08.906 --> 00:35:12.443
Ah, yes. Well, oh,
you feel sleepy?

00:35:12.476 --> 00:35:14.311 align:start size:84% position:16%
Oh, that means
you're very advanced.

00:35:14.344 --> 00:35:16.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I'd be like he
said about everything.

00:35:16.580 --> 00:35:17.581
(laughing)

00:35:17.614 --> 00:35:18.449
It was so funny!

00:35:19.349 --> 00:35:20.417 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I was always excited
to see what level

00:35:20.451 --> 00:35:22.553 align:start size:91% position:9%
he would say they reach
because it gave me a sense

00:35:22.586 --> 00:35:24.755
of how much further you
wanted to go with them.

00:35:24.788 --> 00:35:25.623
- Right, right.

00:35:27.191 --> 00:35:29.627 align:start size:94% position:6%
And because as you remember,
we had many more kids

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:31.195
than we what ended
up showing on camera.

00:35:31.228 --> 00:35:34.632 align:start size:88% position:13%
And we had great results
with all of them.

00:35:34.665 --> 00:35:35.833
- Right, so let's
talk about that.

00:35:35.866 --> 00:35:40.204 align:start size:97% position:3%
So you see three children
featured here in this episode.

00:35:40.237 --> 00:35:42.907 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I believe there were
seven, eight or nine

00:35:42.940 --> 00:35:44.542
that we actually worked with.

00:35:44.575 --> 00:35:45.609
- There were 12,

00:35:45.642 --> 00:35:47.511
- there were 12, Okay,

00:35:47.544 --> 00:35:49.480
not all 12 could fit

00:35:49.513 --> 00:35:51.549 align:start size:91% position:9%
into the time constraints
in that episode.

00:35:51.582 --> 00:35:54.585 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's partly why I
wanna have this marathon

00:35:54.618 --> 00:35:55.586
so we can talk more.

00:35:55.619 --> 00:35:57.188
- You remember Mikey?

00:35:57.221 --> 00:35:58.556
Really, really hyper,

00:35:58.589 --> 00:35:59.824 align:start size:88% position:13%
really difficult to
get him in the machine.

00:35:59.857 --> 00:36:02.593
And still, there were lots and
lots of improvements at home,

00:36:02.626 --> 00:36:04.428 align:start size:91% position:9%
he was able to take a
shower for the first time

00:36:04.461 --> 00:36:06.430
he made a friend at camp.

00:36:06.463 --> 00:36:09.366
And the watch in particular
worked really well with him

00:36:09.399 --> 00:36:10.467 align:start size:81% position:19%
because he was
very focused on it.

00:36:10.500 --> 00:36:13.971
And I made him work really
hard to earn that watch.

00:36:14.004 --> 00:36:15.639
So by the time he got the watch,

00:36:15.672 --> 00:36:17.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
he was already going in
the machine, he was calmer,

00:36:17.474 --> 00:36:19.610 align:start size:84% position:16%
he was more
respectful to his dad.

00:36:19.643 --> 00:36:23.447 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I think I remember
the parents,

00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:27.618 align:start position:0%
the dad saying, okay, so we
reached a point where your goal

00:36:27.651 --> 00:36:30.621
was to get the watch off the kid

00:36:30.654 --> 00:36:33.857
that you don't need to watch,
after all, your own brain,

00:36:33.890 --> 00:36:38.329
your own self can control this.

00:36:39.730 --> 00:36:41.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
But people really want
those accessories.

00:36:41.865 --> 00:36:43.300
And the parents were like,

00:36:43.333 --> 00:36:45.002
Well, I mean, could you
tell them to keep it on?

00:36:45.035 --> 00:36:46.570
Because it keeps the kid calm,

00:36:46.603 --> 00:36:48.472
and it makes them
more thoughtful.

00:36:48.505 --> 00:36:50.608
And it makes them more
aware of their behavior.

00:36:50.641 --> 00:36:51.742 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You have kids, right?
- Mm-hmm.

00:36:51.776 --> 00:36:54.311
- Do you use any of these
tactics on your own kids?

00:36:54.344 --> 00:36:57.948
- Absolutely, our very first
subject was my son Mathias

00:36:57.981 --> 00:37:00.017
who suffered from
chronic migraines.

00:37:00.050 --> 00:37:02.419
And we did a lot of work on him.

00:37:02.452 --> 00:37:06.757
And we had amazing results
on his chronic migraines,

00:37:06.790 --> 00:37:08.525
Mathias, he couldn't make it

00:37:08.558 --> 00:37:09.693
in the final cut of the episode.

00:37:09.726 --> 00:37:13.697 align:start position:0%
But he also helped in the
initial study as a child mentor,

00:37:13.730 --> 00:37:16.300
because we found that a lot
of the kids were terrified

00:37:16.333 --> 00:37:17.534
of getting into the machine.

00:37:17.567 --> 00:37:19.570
But then if they could
speak to another child

00:37:19.603 --> 00:37:20.571 align:start size:81% position:19%
who had gone through
the procedure,

00:37:20.604 --> 00:37:22.940
and who could talk about
how fun and safe it was

00:37:22.973 --> 00:37:24.642
and how he'd gotten better,

00:37:24.675 --> 00:37:26.877 align:start size:91% position:9%
then we were able to
have much better results.

00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:29.346
So we use this kind of
peer mentoring strategy

00:37:29.379 --> 00:37:32.683
that we're still using,
actually, in the studies

00:37:32.716 --> 00:37:33.751
that we're running now.

00:37:33.784 --> 00:37:35.619
But yeah, for sure, I use
these kinds of tricks.

00:37:35.652 --> 00:37:40.591
And I give placebos to my
children, all the time.

00:37:40.624 --> 00:37:43.527
- And then you have your kids
peddle the placebos to others,

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:45.629
because you're right, we
don't show Matia, Mathias?

00:37:45.662 --> 00:37:46.830
- Mathias, yeah.

00:37:46.863 --> 00:37:49.700 align:start size:97% position:3%
- We don't show Mathias in
the episode, but he was there.

00:37:49.733 --> 00:37:53.304
And man, the kids believed him
so much more than the adults.

00:37:53.337 --> 00:37:56.040
If he said, Yeah, I get
in their like every day,

00:37:56.073 --> 00:37:57.508
or whatever his schedule was,

00:37:57.541 --> 00:37:58.709
he was honest about all of this,

00:37:58.742 --> 00:38:01.612
and he was honest about the
good results he was getting.

00:38:01.645 --> 00:38:03.414
Although let's be very clear,

00:38:03.447 --> 00:38:07.451 align:start size:97% position:3%
this MRI is just a piece of
plastic, it is not plugged in.

00:38:07.484 --> 00:38:09.653
It does not have
the liquid helium

00:38:09.686 --> 00:38:12.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
in their magnets in
anything, it's just plastic.

00:38:13.557 --> 00:38:16.493
- And Mathias at the time he
participated in this study,

00:38:16.526 --> 00:38:18.629
was still blind
to the condition,

00:38:18.662 --> 00:38:20.864 align:start size:88% position:13%
meaning that he was
still somewhat convinced

00:38:20.897 --> 00:38:23.634 align:start size:88% position:13%
that there was
something to the machine

00:38:23.667 --> 00:38:25.669 align:start size:88% position:13%
even though he
understood the language,

00:38:25.702 --> 00:38:27.104 align:start size:84% position:16%
he understood the
concept of suggestion,

00:38:27.137 --> 00:38:28.472
but there's a part of him

00:38:28.505 --> 00:38:30.641 align:start size:88% position:13%
that didn't wanna
think about that fully.

00:38:30.674 --> 00:38:33.377
- How can this only
be a suggestion if
I'm in the basement

00:38:33.410 --> 00:38:35.612
of a neuroscience lab
with this big machine?

00:38:35.645 --> 00:38:38.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
Clearly, they're lying
when they tell me the truth

00:38:38.648 --> 00:38:40.384
that this is a placebo.

00:38:40.417 --> 00:38:41.885
All right, let's get back
into it, you guys ready?

00:38:41.918 --> 00:38:43.354
Here we go.

00:38:43.387 --> 00:38:45.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
In six weeks, we would
return to the lab

00:38:45.622 --> 00:38:47.558
to check in on their progress.

00:38:48.725 --> 00:38:51.095
(upbeat music)

00:38:54.765 --> 00:38:56.934 align:start size:91% position:9%
This high tech
contraption is pretty much

00:38:56.967 --> 00:38:58.135
what McGill university's

00:38:58.168 --> 00:39:00.571 align:start size:81% position:19%
first Sham brain
scanner looked like.

00:39:00.604 --> 00:39:03.006 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was an old
discarded hair dryer.

00:39:03.039 --> 00:39:04.975
But the patients
didn't know that.

00:39:05.008 --> 00:39:08.412 align:start position:0%
In the original study, 56
undergraduate students were told

00:39:08.445 --> 00:39:10.948 align:start size:91% position:9%
that it was able to reduce
pain, cause amnesia,

00:39:10.981 --> 00:39:12.950
influence sexual attraction

00:39:12.983 --> 00:39:15.652 align:start size:91% position:9%
and produce various
other impressive effects.

00:39:15.685 --> 00:39:18.589 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
The labs new, more
modern sham brain scanner

00:39:18.622 --> 00:39:21.959 line:0%
shows even more promise, and
I was invited to participate

00:39:21.992 --> 00:39:25.129
in its test on a whole new
group of adult subjects.

00:39:26.563 --> 00:39:29.566
(soft music)

00:39:29.599 --> 00:39:32.603
- Okay, so you can come in
here and just grab a seat.

00:39:32.636 --> 00:39:34.671
- [Michael] These college
students are fully aware

00:39:34.704 --> 00:39:37.574 align:start size:88% position:13%
of what's possible
today with neuroscience.

00:39:37.607 --> 00:39:40.010 align:start size:88% position:13%
Could even they be
neuro and chanted enough

00:39:40.043 --> 00:39:42.079
to believe in the impossible,

00:39:42.112 --> 00:39:45.549 align:start size:91% position:9%
that an MRI scanner could
read their thoughts.

00:39:45.582 --> 00:39:47.951 line:0%
Now, we're looking at cutting
edge psychological research.

00:39:47.984 --> 00:39:49.620 line:0%
- Okay.
- Yes.

00:39:49.653 --> 00:39:51.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's part of the neural
activation mapping project.

00:39:51.721 --> 00:39:52.689 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're gonna be
putting you in an MRI.

00:39:52.722 --> 00:39:55.692
So it's a modified one,
it's called a CTMS FMRI.

00:39:55.725 --> 00:39:57.161
- Okay.

00:39:57.194 --> 00:39:58.562 align:start size:91% position:9%
- So Combined Transcranial
Magnetic Stimulation,

00:39:58.595 --> 00:40:00.698
Functional Magnetic
Resonance Imaging,

00:40:01.631 --> 00:40:02.532
which is a big word just meaning

00:40:02.566 --> 00:40:04.134
it can both read and
influence thoughts.

00:40:04.167 --> 00:40:05.569
- All right, okay.

00:40:05.602 --> 00:40:07.805
- So you'll be choosing
a number from 10 to 99.

00:40:07.838 --> 00:40:09.473
- Okay.

00:40:09.506 --> 00:40:11.942 align:start position:0%
- And then from looking at your
neural activation patterns,

00:40:11.975 --> 00:40:13.544
Dr. Veissière here
will try to infer

00:40:13.577 --> 00:40:14.711
which number you're thinking.

00:40:14.744 --> 00:40:16.613 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Interesting, okay.
- Okay, great.

00:40:16.646 --> 00:40:18.449 align:start size:84% position:16%
- [Michael] Most
adults know the basics

00:40:18.482 --> 00:40:22.653 align:start size:94% position:6%
about MRIs, so we made
sure to cover every detail.

00:40:22.686 --> 00:40:25.155 align:start size:88% position:13%
- In the scanner, the
magnetism is a lot less.

00:40:25.188 --> 00:40:26.723 align:start size:81% position:19%
this is good for the
documentary team,

00:40:26.756 --> 00:40:27.891
because it means they
can bring cameras,

00:40:27.924 --> 00:40:30.828
so that said, we do have to
remove the metal gadgets.

00:40:30.861 --> 00:40:32.062
- Got it, all these.

00:40:32.095 --> 00:40:32.996
- [Michael] After
this realistic,

00:40:33.029 --> 00:40:35.566
but completely unnecessary step,

00:40:35.599 --> 00:40:39.136 align:start size:97% position:3%
it was time to begin our
fake mind reading experiment.

00:40:40.170 --> 00:40:43.540
(eerie sound)

00:40:43.573 --> 00:40:45.075 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So we're gonna
start the calibration,

00:40:45.108 --> 00:40:46.844
so try to stay very still.

00:40:46.877 --> 00:40:48.045
- [Michael] Of course,

00:40:48.078 --> 00:40:49.546
there was actually
nothing to calibrate,

00:40:49.579 --> 00:40:51.748 align:start size:88% position:13%
but we were conditioning
our subjects

00:40:51.781 --> 00:40:53.517
with the expected procedures,

00:40:53.550 --> 00:40:56.053 align:start size:81% position:19%
sights and sounds
of a real MRI study.

00:40:57.654 --> 00:40:59.556
(beeping sound)

00:40:59.589 --> 00:41:01.024
Okay, think of the number one.

00:41:01.057 --> 00:41:02.493 align:start size:81% position:19%
- [Michael] The
subjects were asked

00:41:02.526 --> 00:41:05.662
to concentrate on the
numbers zero to nine,

00:41:05.695 --> 00:41:09.166
while the machine supposedly
mapped parts of their brains.

00:41:09.199 --> 00:41:10.901
- Think of the number nine.

00:41:10.934 --> 00:41:12.035
- [Michael] Dr. Veissière and I

00:41:12.068 --> 00:41:14.505 align:start size:84% position:16%
remained in character
at all times,

00:41:14.538 --> 00:41:17.207
pretending to analyze the
subjects brain activity,

00:41:17.240 --> 00:41:19.910
but the images on our
screens were actually

00:41:19.943 --> 00:41:22.679 align:start size:78% position:22%
old MRI scans from
former patients

00:41:22.712 --> 00:41:23.981
- [Jay] Is the calibration good?

00:41:24.014 --> 00:41:25.716
- It looks pretty good to me.

00:41:25.749 --> 00:41:28.752 align:start size:97% position:3%
- With our subject now primed,
it was time to convince her

00:41:28.785 --> 00:41:30.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
that the scanner could
identify a number

00:41:30.554 --> 00:41:33.690
she was thinking of,
by reading her mind,

00:41:33.723 --> 00:41:35.559
- You're gonna choose
a number from 10 to 99.

00:41:35.592 --> 00:41:37.027
- Okay.

00:41:37.060 --> 00:41:38.529 align:start position:0%
- You're gonna hear a beep,
then you'll hear a second beep.

00:41:38.562 --> 00:41:39.696 align:start size:81% position:19%
So you're always
making your decision

00:41:39.729 --> 00:41:40.631
between the two beeps.

00:41:40.664 --> 00:41:41.598
- Okay.

00:41:41.631 --> 00:41:42.599
- Good?
- Got it.

00:41:42.632 --> 00:41:43.634
- So I'll slide you in.

00:41:43.667 --> 00:41:46.203
(soft music)

00:41:46.236 --> 00:41:48.138
(beep)

00:41:53.577 --> 00:41:55.145 align:start size:91% position:9%
So hold on to that
number for just a second.

00:41:55.178 --> 00:41:56.680
- Okay.

00:41:56.713 --> 00:41:57.814
- They're gonna print it
out, and then we'll see.

00:41:57.847 --> 00:41:58.649
- Okay.

00:41:59.816 --> 00:42:01.251
- It was pretty clear.

00:42:01.284 --> 00:42:03.253
- [Michael] we pretended
to give Jay the results

00:42:03.286 --> 00:42:05.989 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the MRIs analysis,
but actually,

00:42:06.022 --> 00:42:09.293 align:start position:0%
Jay was about to add the
subjects number to the document

00:42:09.326 --> 00:42:11.728
with a little sleight of hand.

00:42:11.761 --> 00:42:13.330 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Okay, so you can say lying
down just for a second.

00:42:13.363 --> 00:42:14.631
What was the number?

00:42:14.664 --> 00:42:15.566
- 31.

00:42:15.599 --> 00:42:16.667
- 31, okay, cool.

00:42:16.700 --> 00:42:18.001
So if you can set up here.

00:42:18.034 --> 00:42:18.969
- What?

00:42:19.002 --> 00:42:20.203
(laughing)

00:42:20.236 --> 00:42:22.139
- So it's pretty close,
but it's swapped, uh.

00:42:22.172 --> 00:42:24.708
- Yeah, okay.

00:42:24.741 --> 00:42:26.977 align:start size:91% position:9%
- [Michael] That miss
was actually intentional.

00:42:27.010 --> 00:42:29.580
So the results didn't
appear to be perfect.

00:42:29.613 --> 00:42:30.781
Adding to the realism.

00:42:30.814 --> 00:42:33.216 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So you're doing well, we'll
do another trial of this.

00:42:33.249 --> 00:42:35.185
The same thing, okay,
I'll slide you in.

00:42:36.653 --> 00:42:38.755
(upbeat music)

00:42:38.788 --> 00:42:40.857
- [Michael] Michelle
thought of a new number

00:42:40.890 --> 00:42:42.893
will the scanner get
it right this time?

00:42:43.727 --> 00:42:44.795
- Super clear.

00:42:44.828 --> 00:42:46.997 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It's clear.
- Really good this time.

00:42:47.030 --> 00:42:48.932 align:start size:91% position:9%
- [Jay] All right, they
think the signal is clear.

00:42:48.965 --> 00:42:50.634
What was your number?

00:42:50.667 --> 00:42:51.768
- 27

00:42:51.801 --> 00:42:53.604
- Uh, 27 did you say?

00:42:53.637 --> 00:42:54.838
- 27.

00:42:54.871 --> 00:42:56.740
- Okay, great, yeah so
if you can set up here.

00:42:56.773 --> 00:42:57.774
- Yes, oh, wow.

00:42:57.807 --> 00:42:58.842
What?

00:42:58.875 --> 00:43:02.112
I don't understand, crazy.

00:43:02.145 --> 00:43:04.381 align:start size:78% position:22%
- So you chose 27?
- I did.

00:43:04.414 --> 00:43:06.617
- And technician got 27 as well.

00:43:06.650 --> 00:43:08.218 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Yeah, well okay.
- Yeah.

00:43:08.251 --> 00:43:09.820
(Mitchell laughing)

00:43:09.853 --> 00:43:12.122
- Oh, wow, how does that feel?

00:43:13.757 --> 00:43:15.726
- I didn't expect it
to be so specific.

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:18.762 align:start size:97% position:3%
A lot of different areas in
the brain could be lighting up

00:43:18.795 --> 00:43:20.897 align:start size:81% position:19%
Just think of the
concept of a number.

00:43:20.930 --> 00:43:22.899
- [Michael] What do
you know, it worked,

00:43:22.932 --> 00:43:25.902
thanks to the wonders
of science, or rather,

00:43:25.935 --> 00:43:27.237
the skills of Jay,

00:43:27.270 --> 00:43:30.040 align:start size:88% position:13%
who in addition to
being a neuroscientist,

00:43:30.073 --> 00:43:33.143 align:start size:94% position:6%
happens to be a professional
magician on the side.

00:43:34.310 --> 00:43:36.647
Well, Jay won't reveal
the secret of the trick.

00:43:36.680 --> 00:43:38.882 align:start size:88% position:13%
The mind reading
illusion is very similar

00:43:38.915 --> 00:43:40.283
to The Mentalist tricks

00:43:40.316 --> 00:43:43.286 align:start size:97% position:3%
that have entertained
audiences for over a century.

00:43:43.319 --> 00:43:45.656 align:start size:84% position:16%
The only difference
is that when audiences

00:43:45.689 --> 00:43:48.158 align:start size:88% position:13%
see the stunt performed
in a magic setting,

00:43:48.191 --> 00:43:51.428
they think it's a great
trick, not real science.

00:43:51.461 --> 00:43:52.763
- The machine had 89.

00:43:52.796 --> 00:43:54.798
(laughing)

00:43:54.831 --> 00:43:56.033
- That's cool.

00:43:56.066 --> 00:43:57.300 align:start size:81% position:19%
- [Michael] However,
in the impressive

00:43:57.333 --> 00:43:59.369 align:start size:78% position:22%
scientific setting
of the Raz Lab,

00:43:59.402 --> 00:44:02.839
these subjects thought our
magic trick was real science.

00:44:02.872 --> 00:44:04.675
- Oh my god (laughing)

00:44:04.708 --> 00:44:05.909
- [Michael] They didn't realize

00:44:05.942 --> 00:44:08.412
that the real science
they were experiencing.

00:44:08.445 --> 00:44:09.346
- It's pretty cool.

00:44:09.380 --> 00:44:11.782
- [Michael] Was the
power of suggestion.

00:44:11.815 --> 00:44:13.183
- Wow.

00:44:13.216 --> 00:44:16.119 align:start size:91% position:9%
- A dose of neuro
enchantment this powerful

00:44:16.152 --> 00:44:20.157
can make for a formidable
and effective placebo.

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:22.292
I want some of the
physical sensations.

00:44:22.325 --> 00:44:23.226
- Sure.

00:44:23.259 --> 00:44:24.394
- What was your experience?

00:44:24.427 --> 00:44:25.428 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I felt like the
headache was coming on,

00:44:25.462 --> 00:44:29.032 align:start size:91% position:9%
I sort of felt like
tingling through my head--

00:44:29.065 --> 00:44:29.966
- Where?

00:44:29.999 --> 00:44:31.234
- Just through this area.

00:44:31.267 --> 00:44:33.737
Like this whole area
felt more like full

00:44:33.770 --> 00:44:38.008 align:start position:0%
- I felt like a sort of
pressure or something like that,

00:44:38.041 --> 00:44:39.843
a strange feeling.

00:44:39.876 --> 00:44:41.278
- Back here a little bit.

00:44:41.311 --> 00:44:42.279
- [Michael] In the back?

00:44:42.312 --> 00:44:43.480
- Yeah, a little
bit in the back.

00:44:43.513 --> 00:44:45.149
- Somewhere in the back of
the head, right, interesting.

00:44:46.015 --> 00:44:47.818 line:0%
- Some tingling inside my head.

00:44:47.851 --> 00:44:49.352
- Okay.

00:44:49.385 --> 00:44:52.789
- I wanna hear what it felt
like to have your mind read.

00:44:52.822 --> 00:44:54.291
- That was very strange.

00:44:54.324 --> 00:44:57.160 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think I was probably
skeptical, like going into it.

00:44:57.193 --> 00:45:00.897
And then I couldn't figure out
as I was thinking like, why,

00:45:00.930 --> 00:45:02.099
how that would happen otherwise.

00:45:02.132 --> 00:45:04.735 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so I'm just I think
in a baffled state.

00:45:04.768 --> 00:45:05.368
(laughing)

00:45:05.401 --> 00:45:06.336
- Yeah, yeah.

00:45:06.369 --> 00:45:09.272
It was time to clear up
this baffling mystery.

00:45:09.305 --> 00:45:11.475
So some deception has
been going on today.

00:45:13.143 --> 00:45:17.280
Dr. Olson is not reading your
mind, neither is this machine.

00:45:17.313 --> 00:45:19.182
This machine is deactivated.

00:45:19.215 --> 00:45:21.785 align:start size:84% position:16%
All the noises were
coming from a speaker.

00:45:21.818 --> 00:45:23.453 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It was an
illusion, oh, my God!

00:45:23.486 --> 00:45:26.289
(laughing)

00:45:26.322 --> 00:45:27.758
That's pretty cool.

00:45:27.791 --> 00:45:30.794
- Dr. Olson is not
reading your mind.

00:45:30.827 --> 00:45:31.995
- Oh, no!

00:45:32.028 --> 00:45:33.864 align:start size:88% position:13%
- The sounds you're
hearing are not magnets.

00:45:33.897 --> 00:45:35.332
It's just from a speaker.

00:45:35.365 --> 00:45:37.768
The machine in there is
actually deactivated,

00:45:37.801 --> 00:45:38.902
it's not working.

00:45:38.935 --> 00:45:40.804
- Well, it's like placebo.

00:45:40.837 --> 00:45:42.439 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah, that's
exactly what it is.

00:45:42.472 --> 00:45:45.075 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now imagine tapping into
this power for other effects,

00:45:45.108 --> 00:45:46.543
like healing, for example.

00:45:46.576 --> 00:45:47.778
- Right, wow!

00:45:48.678 --> 00:45:50.413 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I'm definitely believe
the placebo effect

00:45:50.446 --> 00:45:51.448
is alive and well.

00:45:51.481 --> 00:45:52.883
(laughing)

00:45:52.916 --> 00:45:55.418 align:start size:78% position:22%
- All right, so
we just saw adults

00:45:55.451 --> 00:45:58.188
being fooled and
certainly lied to.

00:45:59.589 --> 00:46:04.161 align:start position:0%
However, the cool thing is that,
while we punked the adults,

00:46:05.829 --> 00:46:09.032
we used that same phenomenon
for good with the children.

00:46:09.065 --> 00:46:11.468 align:start size:81% position:19%
And we don't deceive
the children,

00:46:11.501 --> 00:46:13.804
we just allow them to
deceive themselves.

00:46:16.472 --> 00:46:19.943
I still don't know how
the magic trick is done.

00:46:19.976 --> 00:46:21.545 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I never asked
because it's his trick,

00:46:21.578 --> 00:46:23.446
not mine to know.

00:46:23.479 --> 00:46:26.416 align:start size:91% position:9%
But when the participants
said the number

00:46:26.449 --> 00:46:28.518 align:start position:0%
they were thinking of he managed
to somehow get it written

00:46:28.551 --> 00:46:30.520 align:start size:91% position:9%
on that sheet and the time
it took him to be like,

00:46:30.553 --> 00:46:33.824
yep, that's what it said,
and it was phenomenal.

00:46:33.857 --> 00:46:35.392
And then to hear people say,

00:46:35.425 --> 00:46:38.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
Oh, yeah, no, I felt
tingling, my head felt full.

00:46:38.328 --> 00:46:40.597
It's like you were
just laying on a bed.

00:46:40.630 --> 00:46:42.465 align:start size:84% position:16%
Nothing was on,
nothing was happening.

00:46:42.498 --> 00:46:44.568
It's all in your imagination.

00:46:44.601 --> 00:46:46.336 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, a little
behind the scenes here,

00:46:46.369 --> 00:46:47.571 align:start size:78% position:22%
you might remember
that there was

00:46:47.604 --> 00:46:49.472
a second condition
to that experiment,

00:46:49.505 --> 00:46:53.109 align:start size:91% position:9%
where we implanted
numbers in people's heads.

00:46:53.142 --> 00:46:54.878
So once we get them to believe

00:46:54.911 --> 00:46:56.413
that the machine
reads their mind.

00:46:56.446 --> 00:46:57.381
So their minds are blown.

00:46:57.414 --> 00:47:00.116
And then we inserted
numbers in their head.

00:47:00.149 --> 00:47:02.919
So one of the guys you
might remember, say,

00:47:02.952 --> 00:47:05.522
I thought of the number
45 I hate the number 45.

00:47:05.555 --> 00:47:07.924 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would never myself,
pick that number,

00:47:07.957 --> 00:47:09.926
the number was just
flying into my face.

00:47:09.959 --> 00:47:11.595
And so it's in that
second condition

00:47:11.628 --> 00:47:13.330
that a lot of them reported,

00:47:14.530 --> 00:47:16.933 align:start size:94% position:6%
kind of weird feelings like
tingling and headaches.

00:47:16.966 --> 00:47:18.969
- That's right,
I totally forgot.

00:47:19.002 --> 00:47:20.604
It didn't even make
it into the episode.

00:47:20.637 --> 00:47:23.306
But we also said now that
we can read your mind,

00:47:23.339 --> 00:47:27.577
we are going to implant
thoughts into your mind,

00:47:27.610 --> 00:47:30.113 align:start size:91% position:9%
you are going to have a
number put into your head,

00:47:30.146 --> 00:47:31.381
you'll close your eyes

00:47:31.414 --> 00:47:33.550
and a number will appear
that we have put there.

00:47:33.583 --> 00:47:36.253
And of course, we use the
same sleight of hand trick

00:47:36.286 --> 00:47:37.454
to convince them that
that's what happened,

00:47:37.487 --> 00:47:39.223
whatever they thought they had.

00:47:40.490 --> 00:47:43.193
Whatever they had thought was
what we wanted them to think.

00:47:43.226 --> 00:47:44.527
And,

00:47:44.560 --> 00:47:46.496
obviously, that's crazy,

00:47:46.529 --> 00:47:50.066 align:start size:78% position:22%
like that could
be abused so much.

00:47:50.099 --> 00:47:54.204
But yet we convinced
neuro science postdocs

00:47:54.237 --> 00:47:56.206
that we were able to do it.

00:47:56.239 --> 00:47:57.941 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And you might remember
we were toying around

00:47:57.974 --> 00:48:00.310 align:start size:97% position:3%
with doing like just
improvising a third condition

00:48:00.343 --> 00:48:02.545 align:start size:88% position:13%
where we make people
speak or like say things

00:48:02.578 --> 00:48:04.547 align:start size:88% position:13%
or we induce the
symptoms of Tourette's.

00:48:04.580 --> 00:48:08.585
And in retrospect, if we had
done that saying the behavior

00:48:08.618 --> 00:48:11.154 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
in the reverse exorcism
episode, remember,

00:48:11.187 --> 00:48:14.224 line:0%
like, we were able to induce
all kinds of weird feelings,

00:48:14.257 --> 00:48:15.959 line:0%
like even an out
of body experience,

00:48:15.992 --> 00:48:18.261
but we couldn't really
get people to speak.

00:48:18.294 --> 00:48:21.231
We couldn't get the spirit
to speak through their mouth.

00:48:21.264 --> 00:48:22.565
But if you'd have taken them

00:48:22.598 --> 00:48:23.700
through like two
steps like this,

00:48:23.733 --> 00:48:25.135
to really convince them

00:48:25.168 --> 00:48:26.469
that this machine
is doing something,

00:48:26.502 --> 00:48:28.204
then we could have
gotten participants

00:48:28.237 --> 00:48:30.106
to do all kinds of wild things.

00:48:30.139 --> 00:48:31.641
- Yeah, that we branded at time,

00:48:31.674 --> 00:48:33.276
but I remember that
day, you were like,

00:48:33.309 --> 00:48:35.278 align:start position:0%
let's just go out on the street,
recruit people to come in.

00:48:35.311 --> 00:48:36.713
I can get them to say bad words.

00:48:36.746 --> 00:48:38.315
And think that it's being,

00:48:38.348 --> 00:48:40.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
like they're a puppet
of the machine.

00:48:41.017 --> 00:48:42.552
- Is that related to hypnosis?

00:48:42.585 --> 00:48:44.487
- Yeah, sure, absolutely.

00:48:44.520 --> 00:48:49.159 align:start position:0%
It is hypnosis, it's accessory,
ritual assisted hypnosis.

00:48:49.192 --> 00:48:52.495 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I think that
hypnotism is often seen,

00:48:52.528 --> 00:48:54.130 align:start size:81% position:19%
well, at least I
can speak for myself

00:48:54.163 --> 00:48:56.499
as a nothing but a trick, right?

00:48:56.532 --> 00:48:59.636
I know that there are cases
where it's used clinically.

00:48:59.669 --> 00:49:01.538
Is it all placebo, though?

00:49:01.571 --> 00:49:03.673
I mean, what are
you really doing

00:49:03.706 --> 00:49:05.108
when you hypnotize someone?

00:49:05.141 --> 00:49:06.576
And they need to be
susceptible to it.

00:49:06.609 --> 00:49:07.844
- Have you ever been hypnotized?

00:49:07.877 --> 00:49:11.514
- No, I think I could respond
to being hypnotized like this.

00:49:11.547 --> 00:49:13.350
Because while I don't
have a lot of faith

00:49:13.383 --> 00:49:17.554
in whoa, Hocus Pocus, hypnotizy,

00:49:17.587 --> 00:49:20.056
instead, I will be
like, this is an MRI,

00:49:20.089 --> 00:49:22.158
you're wearing a lab coat,

00:49:22.191 --> 00:49:24.527 align:start size:88% position:13%
whatever is gonna
happen is gonna happen.

00:49:24.560 --> 00:49:26.563 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, so is
hypnosis like placebo

00:49:26.596 --> 00:49:28.765 align:start size:81% position:19%
that depends what
you mean by placebo?

00:49:28.798 --> 00:49:32.535 align:start size:88% position:13%
But if what you're
asking is, is hypnosis,

00:49:32.568 --> 00:49:36.573 align:start size:88% position:13%
tapping into some, say,
autonomic resources

00:49:36.606 --> 00:49:37.841
that we typically

00:49:37.874 --> 00:49:40.643 align:start size:81% position:19%
don't have access to
volitionally effort
fully, then yes.

00:49:40.676 --> 00:49:44.514
So getting people to somehow
relax their hyper vigilance

00:49:44.547 --> 00:49:47.250
and being being able to tap into

00:49:47.283 --> 00:49:48.785 align:start size:88% position:13%
or elicits
physiological responses

00:49:48.818 --> 00:49:51.388 align:start size:91% position:9%
that they themselves could
not induce voluntarily.

00:49:51.421 --> 00:49:52.322
Yes.

00:49:52.355 --> 00:49:53.657
And I think you're correct.

00:49:55.258 --> 00:49:56.426
The induction ritual

00:49:56.459 --> 00:49:58.495 align:start size:84% position:16%
of surrendering to
the set of suggestions

00:49:58.528 --> 00:50:00.330
about falling into trance,

00:50:00.363 --> 00:50:01.564
that's the kind of a
cultural bound ritual

00:50:01.598 --> 00:50:05.635
that works for some people,
that may not work for others.

00:50:05.668 --> 00:50:10.273 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I think that anyone
can be hypnotized,

00:50:10.306 --> 00:50:11.641 align:start size:78% position:22%
as long as you
find the right set

00:50:11.674 --> 00:50:13.476 align:start size:81% position:19%
of what we would
call epistemic cues.

00:50:13.509 --> 00:50:16.079
So you tap into the
kind of knowledge,

00:50:16.112 --> 00:50:18.148 align:start size:94% position:6%
the kinds of things that
they're likely to believe in

00:50:18.181 --> 00:50:20.650
as being authoritative, then
you can definitely get people

00:50:20.683 --> 00:50:22.319 align:start size:78% position:22%
to relax their
critical thinking.

00:50:23.519 --> 00:50:26.122 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So, I'll say I've actually
been hypnotized before.

00:50:26.155 --> 00:50:28.491
And I was very, very
skeptical going in.

00:50:28.524 --> 00:50:31.127 align:start size:81% position:19%
And as the hypnotist
was telling me,

00:50:31.160 --> 00:50:32.362
oh, when you open your eyes,

00:50:32.395 --> 00:50:33.630
you're gonna forget
the number three.

00:50:33.663 --> 00:50:35.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
I remember thinking
to myself consciously,

00:50:35.164 --> 00:50:37.667
there's no way I'm
gonna fall for this.

00:50:37.700 --> 00:50:40.136 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then he say,
Okay, now count to 10.

00:50:40.169 --> 00:50:43.440 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then I would get to
three, and then I would pause,

00:50:43.473 --> 00:50:45.575 align:start position:0%
even though I'm telling
myself, in my head, I'm saying,

00:50:45.608 --> 00:50:48.511 align:start position:0%
I can see the number three,
but I just couldn't get it out.

00:50:48.544 --> 00:50:52.749
- Wow, I need to do this,
I need to get hypnotized,

00:50:52.782 --> 00:50:54.851
because I've always seen
it as just a party trick

00:50:54.884 --> 00:50:57.520
where I'm gonna make you
bark like a dog on stage

00:50:57.553 --> 00:50:59.222
for at a kid's birthday party.

00:50:59.255 --> 00:51:00.723
And they do, and I'm like
they're all faking it.

00:51:00.756 --> 00:51:02.358
- But you may not
be susceptible.

00:51:02.391 --> 00:51:06.296
So there's a pretty Gaussian
distribution of susceptibility.

00:51:06.329 --> 00:51:09.666 align:start size:94% position:6%
So most people will fall for
some things, but not all.

00:51:09.699 --> 00:51:11.301
Some people will
fall for nothing.

00:51:11.334 --> 00:51:13.103 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Some fall for all
- Exactly.

00:51:13.136 --> 00:51:15.105 align:start size:91% position:9%
- So it's interesting
because it is a relatively

00:51:15.138 --> 00:51:18.375
stable psychological trade,
and that's well studied.

00:51:19.609 --> 00:51:21.211
It doesn't change much
throughout the lifetime

00:51:21.244 --> 00:51:22.412
except in childhood.

00:51:22.445 --> 00:51:23.780 align:start size:81% position:19%
So between the ages
of seven and 14

00:51:23.813 --> 00:51:26.216
is when people are
more hypnotizable.

00:51:26.249 --> 00:51:29.185
And some people are so we
say their highs or lows,

00:51:29.218 --> 00:51:31.221
high hypnotizables,
low hypnotizables,

00:51:31.254 --> 00:51:32.823 align:start size:81% position:19%
and there is a
normal distribution.

00:51:34.590 --> 00:51:35.792
- But it's really weird.

00:51:35.825 --> 00:51:37.494
Actually, I don't know
if I'd recommend it.

00:51:37.527 --> 00:51:39.329
It was a kind of an unpleasant
experience to be hypnotized.

00:51:39.362 --> 00:51:40.697
- I've done more
unpleasant things.

00:51:40.730 --> 00:51:43.600
I think, I wanna talk about
that after this episode,

00:51:43.633 --> 00:51:46.136
kind of talk through some
of the more uncomfortable

00:51:46.169 --> 00:51:48.238
and scary moments, but for now,

00:51:48.271 --> 00:51:49.606
let's just get right
back into the episode.

00:51:49.639 --> 00:51:51.474
All right, here we go.

00:51:51.507 --> 00:51:53.677
(soft music)

00:51:56.479 --> 00:51:58.648
There's evidence that
the power of suggestion

00:51:58.681 --> 00:52:01.317
even works on animals.

00:52:01.350 --> 00:52:03.253 align:start size:91% position:9%
A study at North
Carolina State University

00:52:03.286 --> 00:52:07.724 align:start position:0%
found that 86% of dogs receiving
real seizure medication

00:52:07.757 --> 00:52:09.626
had a reduction in seizures,

00:52:09.659 --> 00:52:13.229
but almost as many a full 79%

00:52:13.262 --> 00:52:16.599
experienced the same effect
from just a placebo pill.

00:52:16.632 --> 00:52:20.303 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now, we don't know how a
placebo effects a dog's brain.

00:52:20.336 --> 00:52:21.938 align:start size:81% position:19%
But it could be that
dogs have learned

00:52:21.971 --> 00:52:25.475
to associate vet visits
and medicine from humans

00:52:25.508 --> 00:52:26.609
with feeling better.

00:52:26.642 --> 00:52:28.344
So giving them a placebo

00:52:28.377 --> 00:52:31.314
could help a dog's
brain heal itself.

00:52:31.347 --> 00:52:32.782
(upbeat music)

00:52:32.815 --> 00:52:34.618
Quick note, we

00:52:35.818 --> 00:52:38.955
recorded the scene with
that dog so many times.

00:52:38.988 --> 00:52:40.857 align:start size:91% position:9%
There were so many takes,
were you there, Dan?

00:52:40.890 --> 00:52:41.691
- I was there.

00:52:41.724 --> 00:52:42.959
- Oh, man, first of all,

00:52:42.992 --> 00:52:44.627
I don't think a lot of
people don't know this.

00:52:44.660 --> 00:52:46.496
But all those rooftop scenes

00:52:46.529 --> 00:52:48.932 align:start size:88% position:13%
from season one and
two were shot at night.

00:52:48.965 --> 00:52:51.267
We did a whole week
of overnight shoots

00:52:51.300 --> 00:52:54.704
from 5:00 in the evening
to 5:00 in the morning.

00:52:54.737 --> 00:52:57.574
And so I don't know what
time of night that was.

00:52:57.607 --> 00:52:58.575 align:start size:88% position:13%
But we're all exhausted.
- It's like 3:00

00:52:58.608 --> 00:53:00.810
in the morning, the dog
would climb up to you--

00:53:00.843 --> 00:53:02.645
- The dog would jump on me yeah.

00:53:02.678 --> 00:53:04.347
it was hard to get
the dog to wait.

00:53:04.380 --> 00:53:05.615
And the trainer was like, look,

00:53:05.648 --> 00:53:07.617 align:start size:81% position:19%
the dog doesn't
understand its cue.

00:53:07.650 --> 00:53:09.686
It just knows that you're
gonna give him cheese.

00:53:09.719 --> 00:53:10.620 align:start size:81% position:19%
It doesn't know
what you're saying,

00:53:10.654 --> 00:53:12.655 align:start size:88% position:13%
And he doesn't know
when it's a good moment.

00:53:12.688 --> 00:53:14.391
The dog was not a great actor.

00:53:15.658 --> 00:53:17.227
Shall we continue?

00:53:17.260 --> 00:53:17.894
Here we go.

00:53:19.729 --> 00:53:21.665
- So first, I'll ask
you to lay down again.

00:53:22.832 --> 00:53:25.268 line:0%
And of course, you're
a total pro at this.

00:53:25.301 --> 00:53:26.436 line:0%
- [Michael] For several weeks,

00:53:26.469 --> 00:53:28.871 line:0%
the children had undergone
sessions in the Sham scanner

00:53:28.904 --> 00:53:30.773
at McGill University.

00:53:30.806 --> 00:53:33.409
- Your body continues to relax

00:53:33.442 --> 00:53:36.512
as we keep unleashing
this healing energy.

00:53:36.545 --> 00:53:37.981 line:0%
- [Michael] Each time
they were reminded

00:53:38.014 --> 00:53:40.617 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
that through concentration
and relaxation,

00:53:40.650 --> 00:53:43.519 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
they were helping
heal their own brains.

00:53:43.552 --> 00:53:44.720 line:0%
- There we go.

00:53:44.753 --> 00:53:48.691 align:start position:0% line:0%
Finally, after six weeks of
receiving the placebo sessions,

00:53:48.724 --> 00:53:52.328 line:0%
it was time to get a
report on the results.

00:53:52.361 --> 00:53:53.930 line:0%
Malaya suffered from anxiety

00:53:53.963 --> 00:53:56.666 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
and a compulsive skin
picking disorder.

00:53:56.699 --> 00:53:58.768
Had she experienced
any level of success?

00:54:00.069 --> 00:54:01.904
It's good to see you.
- Good to see you.

00:54:01.937 --> 00:54:04.540
- Go ahead and take a
seat, you're wearing

00:54:04.573 --> 00:54:06.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
not even, I thought maybe
they'd have short sleeves on

00:54:06.676 --> 00:54:09.012
you've just got a tank top on.

00:54:09.045 --> 00:54:10.546
Your arms look fantastic.

00:54:10.579 --> 00:54:12.548 line:0%
They've really gotten better.

00:54:12.581 --> 00:54:14.617 line:0%
And your face I mean, all of it.

00:54:14.650 --> 00:54:16.619 line:0%
Why do you think you're better?

00:54:16.652 --> 00:54:18.621
- I'm guessing it's the machine.

00:54:18.654 --> 00:54:19.856
- [Michael] Yeah, what about it?

00:54:19.889 --> 00:54:22.525
- Ah, I'm not really
sure how it works.

00:54:22.558 --> 00:54:24.560
But I'm picking a lot less,

00:54:24.593 --> 00:54:27.597
sometimes if I see
like a tiny flake,

00:54:27.630 --> 00:54:29.599
I'll just leave it there.

00:54:29.632 --> 00:54:32.402
- That's a pretty short amount
of time for such a big change

00:54:32.435 --> 00:54:35.605
in the way you think and
behave, that's awesome.

00:54:35.638 --> 00:54:37.640 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I don't really feel
the urge to do it

00:54:37.673 --> 00:54:41.644 align:start position:0%
as much as I did before, so
it's a big improvement I guess.

00:54:41.677 --> 00:54:43.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, that
definitely feels like

00:54:43.512 --> 00:54:45.915
and sounds like your
brain being powerful.

00:54:45.948 --> 00:54:47.784
- It's obviously
doing something,

00:54:47.817 --> 00:54:49.852
because my arms are better.

00:54:49.885 --> 00:54:51.587
- Well, you seem a
lot more confident

00:54:51.620 --> 00:54:54.090 line:0%
holding your head up higher,
would you agree Anne Marie?

00:54:54.123 --> 00:54:57.860 line:0%
- Yes, she seems less anxious.

00:54:57.893 --> 00:55:01.364 align:start size:97% position:3%
She's more positive, (mumbles)
I was hoping for this.

00:55:01.397 --> 00:55:03.333
It's more than nice to see her.

00:55:03.366 --> 00:55:04.868
- Yeah, good work.

00:55:06.535 --> 00:55:09.406
And ask for Nicholas and
his migraine headaches.

00:55:10.573 --> 00:55:13.443
Hey, it's good to see
you again, Nicholas.

00:55:13.476 --> 00:55:15.912
Tell me about the procedure
and how you felt afterwards,

00:55:15.945 --> 00:55:18.581 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It accomplished things
that I wanted it to.

00:55:18.614 --> 00:55:20.917
I haven't had a migraine at all.

00:55:20.950 --> 00:55:22.618
- [Michael] That's awesome.

00:55:22.651 --> 00:55:24.587
- And concentration is a
big thing that it helped.

00:55:24.620 --> 00:55:25.822
- Wow!

00:55:25.855 --> 00:55:26.723
And can I see the
symptom charts?

00:55:26.756 --> 00:55:27.657
- For sure.

00:55:27.690 --> 00:55:28.958
- So these go back to February.

00:55:28.991 --> 00:55:30.626 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah, and they're
really telling.

00:55:30.659 --> 00:55:33.663
- And you can see not
good, lots going on.

00:55:33.696 --> 00:55:36.766
But then recently,
no migraines at all.

00:55:36.799 --> 00:55:38.401
That's incredible.

00:55:38.434 --> 00:55:39.635
- Yeah.

00:55:39.668 --> 00:55:41.137 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So you said you went
to the neurologist.

00:55:41.170 --> 00:55:42.772 align:start size:84% position:16%
- They thought it
was quite remarkable.

00:55:42.805 --> 00:55:45.541 align:start size:81% position:19%
He hasn't needed any
rescue medication

00:55:45.574 --> 00:55:47.076 align:start size:81% position:19%
and he hasn't needed
the preventer.

00:55:47.109 --> 00:55:49.379
And he's not having migraines.

00:55:49.412 --> 00:55:51.715 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I'm actually really
excited for high school.

00:55:53.115 --> 00:55:54.584
- [Michael] Both
Malaya and Nicholas

00:55:54.617 --> 00:55:57.453 align:start size:94% position:6%
attribute their improvements
to the Sham scanner.

00:55:57.486 --> 00:56:00.690
They believed it was working
for them, and indeed it did.

00:56:01.690 --> 00:56:03.926
So how did Nathan who
suffered from ADHD

00:56:03.959 --> 00:56:07.797
and impulse control disorder
fair over the last six weeks?

00:56:07.830 --> 00:56:10.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well I received a home
video from Nathan's mother,

00:56:10.132 --> 00:56:12.068
with an update on his progress?

00:56:13.669 --> 00:56:16.038
- So the best part about
going through the treatment

00:56:16.071 --> 00:56:20.176
was just what it did to our
son, we noticed a difference

00:56:20.209 --> 00:56:22.712 align:start size:88% position:13%
in Nathan immediately
after the first session,

00:56:22.745 --> 00:56:23.946 align:start size:81% position:19%
and do you remember
what happened?

00:56:23.979 --> 00:56:25.081
- I slept?

00:56:25.114 --> 00:56:28.418 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, he slept
something he doesn't do

00:56:28.451 --> 00:56:31.721 align:start size:91% position:9%
very often during the day,
he slept for two hours.

00:56:31.754 --> 00:56:34.023
He was refreshed, it was
just incredible to see it.

00:56:34.056 --> 00:56:36.926
And then what happened
was the entire summer

00:56:36.959 --> 00:56:39.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
we had him off the
medicine, and he did great.

00:56:39.895 --> 00:56:42.432 align:start size:84% position:16%
He thought about stuff
before he did it,

00:56:42.465 --> 00:56:46.702 align:start size:94% position:6%
we were able to talk things
out and it's been fun.

00:56:46.735 --> 00:56:48.671
So overall, we're very pleased.

00:56:48.704 --> 00:56:51.607
And we just had such
a great experience,

00:56:51.640 --> 00:56:52.776
so thank you very much.

00:56:54.176 --> 00:56:56.579
- [Michael] The Children's
results are encouraging,

00:56:56.612 --> 00:56:59.649
and a powerful sign of
how effective suggestions

00:56:59.682 --> 00:57:02.585
and our willingness
believe them can be.

00:57:02.618 --> 00:57:04.554
In time, the kids
will understand

00:57:04.587 --> 00:57:06.923 align:start size:81% position:19%
how all of the power
was within them,

00:57:06.956 --> 00:57:08.725
and not in the scanner.

00:57:09.925 --> 00:57:13.563 align:start position:0%
This work is truly cutting edge
it hasn't been done before.

00:57:13.596 --> 00:57:14.764
- Correct.

00:57:14.797 --> 00:57:17.467
We also think of this
as a great new way

00:57:17.500 --> 00:57:19.035
to do science and
to collaborate.

00:57:19.068 --> 00:57:21.737
- So much more of the public
will see what's being done.

00:57:21.770 --> 00:57:23.573
- Yes, science isn't
just about publishing

00:57:23.606 --> 00:57:24.774
a paper that nobody reads.

00:57:24.807 --> 00:57:26.642 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's about spreading
the ideas that you find.

00:57:26.675 --> 00:57:28.177
- 100%.

00:57:28.210 --> 00:57:29.879
- As far as I'm concerned,
you're already a co author

00:57:29.912 --> 00:57:31.981
in our scientific
experimental paper.

00:57:32.014 --> 00:57:33.683
- Wow, very cool.

00:57:33.716 --> 00:57:34.550
Thank you.

00:57:34.583 --> 00:57:36.986
(upbeat music)

00:57:41.023 --> 00:57:42.592 align:start size:88% position:13%
- [Michael] The
Children's improvements

00:57:42.625 --> 00:57:44.994 align:start size:78% position:22%
were caused by the
placebo effect.

00:57:45.027 --> 00:57:48.097
And no deception was
used to mask that.

00:57:48.130 --> 00:57:50.766 align:start size:88% position:13%
The parents knew the
machine was deactivated.

00:57:50.799 --> 00:57:53.636
And the children were only
told that it had the power

00:57:53.669 --> 00:57:56.138 align:start size:81% position:19%
to put a suggestion
in their brain,

00:57:56.171 --> 00:58:00.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
a suggestion that ultimately
came from themselves.

00:58:00.209 --> 00:58:03.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
But surely, the more
people learn about placebos

00:58:03.112 --> 00:58:05.615
and their lack of
intrinsic power,

00:58:05.648 --> 00:58:07.517
the less effective
they'll be, right?

00:58:08.717 --> 00:58:11.787 align:start size:91% position:9%
No, studies show that
even when subjects learned

00:58:11.820 --> 00:58:13.756 align:start size:81% position:19%
that their treatment
was a placebo,

00:58:13.789 --> 00:58:16.159 align:start size:81% position:19%
the positive results
do not go away.

00:58:17.626 --> 00:58:20.229
What the subjects have learned
about how to heal themselves,

00:58:20.262 --> 00:58:21.164
remains with them.

00:58:22.798 --> 00:58:24.834 align:start size:91% position:9%
It was an honor to have
been a part of this study.

00:58:24.867 --> 00:58:27.737
I think this is Mind
Field at its best.

00:58:27.770 --> 00:58:30.806
Using our resources to help
researchers with their work

00:58:30.839 --> 00:58:33.676
and helping the public see
how the brain is studied.

00:58:34.743 --> 00:58:36.812
Placebos can't fix everything.

00:58:36.845 --> 00:58:41.584
But these kids always had
the power to start healing.

00:58:41.617 --> 00:58:44.654
All they needed was the
power of suggestion.

00:58:45.788 --> 00:58:47.857 align:start size:81% position:19%
And as always,
thanks for watching.

00:58:50.025 --> 00:58:52.628 align:start size:81% position:19%
All right, so
that's the episode.

00:58:52.661 --> 00:58:56.198 align:start size:97% position:3%
Dr. Veissière I have
dismissed Elisabeth and Daniel

00:58:56.231 --> 00:58:59.669
so that we can talk
one on one, honestly.

00:58:59.702 --> 00:59:00.670
- I miss them.

00:59:00.703 --> 00:59:02.305
- Do you feel the pressure?

00:59:02.338 --> 00:59:05.942
What are your thoughts now
two years after that episode?

00:59:07.610 --> 00:59:09.745
- Well, my thoughts are we
are to take this further.

00:59:09.778 --> 00:59:12.148
And so I'm thinking
lots of directions.

00:59:12.181 --> 00:59:13.649
One of them is practical.

00:59:13.682 --> 00:59:15.585
It's kind of unfortunate
that the general public

00:59:15.618 --> 00:59:17.320
is really interested in this.

00:59:18.621 --> 00:59:21.223
But it's still hard to convince

00:59:21.256 --> 00:59:24.293
the scientific community
and the clinical community,

00:59:24.326 --> 00:59:26.696 align:start size:81% position:19%
there's something
worth pursuing there

00:59:26.729 --> 00:59:28.130
because it's so weird.

00:59:28.163 --> 00:59:30.366
So it's been difficult
to get research funding,

00:59:30.399 --> 00:59:32.802
it's been difficult to get
our research papers accepted

00:59:32.835 --> 00:59:35.705 align:start size:81% position:19%
for publication when
we try to present

00:59:35.738 --> 00:59:37.106
this particular project,

00:59:37.139 --> 00:59:39.875
- How do you do a
control in a study

00:59:39.908 --> 00:59:41.611
on the effectiveness of placebo?

00:59:41.644 --> 00:59:44.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, do you test it
against a different placebo?

00:59:46.315 --> 00:59:48.117 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, and that's been one
of the comments from reviewers

00:59:48.150 --> 00:59:50.052
when we try to submit
this from publications.

00:59:50.085 --> 00:59:51.654
And I think it was a
really good comment

00:59:51.687 --> 00:59:52.555
they're asking, okay,

00:59:52.588 --> 00:59:54.390 align:start size:88% position:13%
so it seems you're
doing this really cool,

00:59:54.423 --> 00:59:55.758
really amazing procedure,

00:59:55.791 --> 00:59:57.827 align:start size:88% position:13%
you're giving these
kids a lot of attention,

00:59:57.860 --> 00:59:59.729
a lot of positive reinforcement.

00:59:59.762 --> 01:00:01.831
But what exactly works?

01:00:01.864 --> 01:00:03.065
And what doesn't work?

01:00:03.098 --> 01:00:04.300 align:start size:81% position:19%
Is there anything
in that intervention

01:00:04.333 --> 01:00:07.303 align:start size:88% position:13%
that is absolutely
necessary or essential?

01:00:07.336 --> 01:00:10.072 align:start size:91% position:9%
For example, do you
actually need the scanner?

01:00:10.105 --> 01:00:12.108
Could you just go and, give kids

01:00:12.141 --> 01:00:14.410 align:start size:94% position:6%
lots of positive suggestions
about how they can use

01:00:14.443 --> 01:00:18.714
their hyperactivity to be
really focused and strong?

01:00:18.747 --> 01:00:20.116
Or could you do us just a watch,

01:00:20.149 --> 01:00:22.718
so I can tell you something
we're doing right now is,

01:00:22.751 --> 01:00:25.421
we're beginning to test
in a randomized trial,

01:00:25.454 --> 01:00:28.324 align:start size:84% position:16%
just the watch versus
just the machine

01:00:28.357 --> 01:00:29.925
to see if one of
them works better.

01:00:29.958 --> 01:00:34.297
- Huh, okay, that sounds
really nice and precise.

01:00:35.464 --> 01:00:36.499
- Precise or not?

01:00:36.532 --> 01:00:38.234
Because even then there's
so many other factors,

01:00:38.267 --> 01:00:39.902 align:start size:78% position:22%
as you know, so
say, the charisma,

01:00:39.935 --> 01:00:42.772
or the reassuring tone
of the experimenter.

01:00:42.805 --> 01:00:43.706
Like I'm sure you're interested

01:00:43.740 --> 01:00:45.975
in the replication
crisis in psychology.

01:00:46.008 --> 01:00:48.411 align:start size:88% position:13%
So one of the hypotheses
is also that well,

01:00:48.444 --> 01:00:52.281
it could be that some labs are
trying to replicate a study.

01:00:52.314 --> 01:00:54.417
Well, the experimenters are
just not as charismatic,

01:00:54.450 --> 01:00:56.786
there's just not as good
or not as authoritative.

01:00:56.819 --> 01:00:58.754
And there's lots of other
factors that are going on.

01:00:58.787 --> 01:01:01.691 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's not just a mechanism
itself that doesn't work

01:01:01.724 --> 01:01:04.226
is that this one team is
not able to reproduce it.

01:01:04.259 --> 01:01:07.029 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Man, it's so hard to control
for all of those things,

01:01:07.062 --> 01:01:08.731 align:start size:88% position:13%
a bunch of different
people need to run this,

01:01:08.764 --> 01:01:10.833
but they all have to have
the exact same charisma.

01:01:12.000 --> 01:01:13.803 align:start size:88% position:13%
And every day, no matter
what their mood is

01:01:13.836 --> 01:01:15.404
different from day
to day, it can't be.

01:01:15.437 --> 01:01:19.275
Because we really only wanna
look at watch versus machine,

01:01:19.308 --> 01:01:23.713 align:start size:97% position:3%
when we know that the charisma
can play such a big role.

01:01:23.746 --> 01:01:25.948
- Mm hmm, or also, the
setting of the study,

01:01:25.981 --> 01:01:26.949
is it in the hospital?

01:01:26.982 --> 01:01:28.117
Or is it like right now

01:01:28.150 --> 01:01:29.819 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're gonna replicate
it in a school.

01:01:29.852 --> 01:01:31.320
Because it turned out
that logistically,

01:01:31.353 --> 01:01:32.588
now that Mind Field is not there

01:01:32.621 --> 01:01:35.858
to handle recruitment for us,
then we just could not manage

01:01:35.891 --> 01:01:38.094 align:start size:97% position:3%
to bring all the
participants in the hospital.

01:01:38.127 --> 01:01:39.862 align:start size:88% position:13%
And we have a contingent
from the school.

01:01:39.895 --> 01:01:41.430 align:start size:81% position:19%
So it's a lot easier
to do it there.

01:01:41.463 --> 01:01:44.500
And as such, we also have to
use a simpler mock scanner.

01:01:44.533 --> 01:01:46.302
- Right, you know what
that brings brings up

01:01:46.335 --> 01:01:48.804
a big limitation of how
Mind Field can help.

01:01:48.837 --> 01:01:49.738
Because if you said okay,

01:01:49.772 --> 01:01:52.742
I've got a different
experimental design

01:01:52.775 --> 01:01:55.277 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I think is gonna
work really well.

01:01:55.310 --> 01:01:56.979
But it's very similar.

01:01:57.012 --> 01:01:58.214
Could you do another episode

01:01:58.247 --> 01:02:00.249 align:start size:84% position:16%
that will look almost
exactly the same?

01:02:00.282 --> 01:02:01.317
I would love to,

01:02:01.350 --> 01:02:04.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I don't know if
YouTube would pay for that.

01:02:04.520 --> 01:02:07.022
So I hope that Mind Field

01:02:07.055 --> 01:02:10.459
can at least bring
what's being done,

01:02:10.492 --> 01:02:12.328 align:start size:78% position:22%
and its importance
to the public,

01:02:12.361 --> 01:02:17.032 align:start size:97% position:3%
so that they know what to
support and they know what to,

01:02:17.065 --> 01:02:19.468
I don't know what kind of
good things are being done

01:02:19.501 --> 01:02:23.205
and why it's important for us
to have research institutions

01:02:23.238 --> 01:02:27.409
that are investigating things
that might seem I don't know,

01:02:27.442 --> 01:02:29.211
a little still fuzzy,

01:02:29.244 --> 01:02:31.847
but yet can lead to
amazing discoveries.

01:02:31.880 --> 01:02:34.216
What are you working on
that I don't know about

01:02:34.249 --> 01:02:35.818
that might be interesting.

01:02:35.851 --> 01:02:38.788
for future episodes of
Mind Field of Vsauce,

01:02:38.821 --> 01:02:41.557 align:start size:91% position:9%
I know that you were
working with tulpamancers.

01:02:41.590 --> 01:02:42.525
That's still going on?

01:02:42.558 --> 01:02:45.861
And if so explain what that is.

01:02:45.894 --> 01:02:47.563 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Sure, so before talking
about tulpamancers,

01:02:47.596 --> 01:02:49.532 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're also doing
lots of stuff related

01:02:49.565 --> 01:02:50.733
to suggestion and
placebo studies.

01:02:50.766 --> 01:02:55.504
So we're testing like, say fake
Adderall, fake psychedelics.

01:02:55.537 --> 01:02:57.139
- [Michael] Fake psychedelics.

01:02:57.172 --> 01:02:58.340
- Fake psychedelics--

01:02:58.373 --> 01:03:00.209
- There's an episode in the
first season of Mind Field,

01:03:00.242 --> 01:03:02.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
where we give people a short
acting and psychedelic,

01:03:02.611 --> 01:03:04.814 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we have
Confederates in the room

01:03:04.847 --> 01:03:06.248
that pretend like, Whoa!

01:03:06.281 --> 01:03:07.516
Everything's echoing.

01:03:07.549 --> 01:03:09.819
And sure enough, you can make
people think they're tripping.

01:03:09.852 --> 01:03:11.453
- Absolutely, and we
also use Confederates,

01:03:11.486 --> 01:03:13.856
just like you guys, and we
had really good results.

01:03:13.889 --> 01:03:16.458
- So what are you
specifically like,

01:03:16.491 --> 01:03:20.062
what variables are you kind
of tweaking and studying?

01:03:20.095 --> 01:03:22.131 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, we're also interested
in the whole battery

01:03:22.164 --> 01:03:24.433 align:start size:81% position:19%
of non specific
psychosocial effects

01:03:24.466 --> 01:03:27.469
beyond the chemical properties
of the substance themselves,

01:03:27.502 --> 01:03:29.839 align:start size:88% position:13%
including the particular
cultural expectations

01:03:29.872 --> 01:03:31.507
that this psychedelic experience

01:03:31.540 --> 01:03:34.410
should yield this
particular effect.

01:03:34.443 --> 01:03:38.848
So we're looking at, social
proof, social conformity,

01:03:38.881 --> 01:03:40.549
emotional contagion, but also,

01:03:40.582 --> 01:03:43.419
testing particular
cultural beliefs.

01:03:43.452 --> 01:03:47.556 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're also going to be
testing, Sham genetic testing

01:03:47.589 --> 01:03:51.460
to see how convincing that
might be, and at some point,

01:03:51.493 --> 01:03:56.398 align:start position:0%
one of your dream is to compare
that to Sham neuroscience,

01:03:56.431 --> 01:03:59.068 align:start size:91% position:9%
so to see, different kinds
of neuro enchantment.

01:04:00.202 --> 01:04:01.270
- What's the point?

01:04:01.303 --> 01:04:04.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
Okay, so we learn that
people are neural enchanted,

01:04:04.573 --> 01:04:07.076
and they're, I don't
know DNA enchanted,

01:04:07.109 --> 01:04:10.479
what's the word for that, our
faith and genetic research?

01:04:11.914 --> 01:04:13.549
- We think they're really
big ethical implications,

01:04:13.582 --> 01:04:15.985
particularly when we start
thinking about potential,

01:04:16.018 --> 01:04:19.622
nocebo effects and culturally
widespread nocebo effects.

01:04:19.655 --> 01:04:23.392
So, genetic testing also
generates a lot of anxiety.

01:04:23.425 --> 01:04:24.526
A lot of the times it's not done

01:04:24.560 --> 01:04:28.030 align:start size:97% position:3%
under proper clinical settings
and clinical guidance,

01:04:28.063 --> 01:04:29.598
and people start
worrying immensely,

01:04:29.631 --> 01:04:31.500
because they have
a particular gene

01:04:31.533 --> 01:04:32.401
that is thought to correlate

01:04:32.434 --> 01:04:33.903
with a particular
condition like say,

01:04:33.936 --> 01:04:35.604
Alzheimer's, for example.

01:04:35.637 --> 01:04:39.441
And that can bring about all
kinds of negative experiences,

01:04:39.474 --> 01:04:43.345
including sometimes perhaps
precipitating early onset

01:04:43.378 --> 01:04:47.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
of diseases that may not
otherwise have been triggered.

01:04:47.716 --> 01:04:50.552
So we think there's lots
of important implications,

01:04:50.585 --> 01:04:53.022 align:start size:81% position:19%
particularly in
terms of, debunking,

01:04:53.055 --> 01:04:55.424
dangerous pseudo medical
and pseudo scientific idea.

01:04:55.457 --> 01:04:57.192
So if we're able to demonstrate

01:04:57.225 --> 01:04:59.495
that there's a lot of authority,

01:04:59.528 --> 01:05:01.563
a lot of placebo nocebo
effects associated with,

01:05:01.596 --> 01:05:04.266
say, genetic testing,
then we're better able

01:05:04.299 --> 01:05:07.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
to inform clinical practice,
for example, or policy--

01:05:07.703 --> 01:05:11.974
- And the public cannot be
quite so enchanted by it

01:05:12.007 --> 01:05:15.444
or perhaps misled,
needlessly panicked.

01:05:15.477 --> 01:05:16.278
Wow, it's important stuff.

01:05:16.311 --> 01:05:17.646 align:start size:78% position:22%
- And so to answer
your questions,

01:05:17.679 --> 01:05:19.515 align:start size:88% position:13%
yes, we're still working
with tulpamancers.

01:05:19.548 --> 01:05:22.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
As you recall, I did a
cyber ethnography years ago,

01:05:22.584 --> 01:05:24.386
with just delivered a
psychological testing

01:05:24.419 --> 01:05:27.390
on these really cool,
weird young people

01:05:28.557 --> 01:05:30.592
who conjure imaginary friends

01:05:30.625 --> 01:05:33.529 align:start size:88% position:13%
that they come to
experience as basically

01:05:33.562 --> 01:05:38.567 align:start size:84% position:16%
as auditory visual
hallucinations, except
they're not crazy.

01:05:40.002 --> 01:05:41.570
And they report an increase
in well being in their life,

01:05:41.603 --> 01:05:43.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
even in social
adjustment as a result

01:05:43.505 --> 01:05:44.406
of having picked
up the practice.

01:05:44.440 --> 01:05:46.475
So people wanna say,
well, they're crazy,

01:05:46.508 --> 01:05:48.677
they're hearing voices, and we
think we're able to show that

01:05:48.710 --> 01:05:50.546 align:start size:81% position:19%
No, in fact, there's
lots of context

01:05:50.579 --> 01:05:54.650
in which you can hear voices
and be a very healthy person.

01:05:54.683 --> 01:05:59.555 align:start size:94% position:6%
So now, under the leadership
of Dr. Mike Lifshitz,

01:05:59.588 --> 01:06:03.659
and Dr. Tanya Lerman
at Stanford University,

01:06:03.692 --> 01:06:05.627
we're in the process
of doing neuro imaging

01:06:05.660 --> 01:06:07.696
of tulpamancers and
comparing them also

01:06:07.729 --> 01:06:11.033 align:start size:91% position:9%
to evangelical Christians
who speak in tongues.

01:06:11.066 --> 01:06:14.636 align:start position:0%
And so we're interested in
seeing what happens in the brain

01:06:14.669 --> 01:06:18.040
when either spirit or tulpa

01:06:18.073 --> 01:06:20.109
sort of takes over
and starts talking.

01:06:20.142 --> 01:06:22.011
And we're interested in the

01:06:23.145 --> 01:06:25.381 align:start size:78% position:22%
motor areas of
the brain as well.

01:06:25.414 --> 01:06:28.350 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we wanna see if something
different is going on.

01:06:28.383 --> 01:06:31.520 align:start size:97% position:3%
When say, an agency other than
itself a tulpa or a spirit

01:06:31.553 --> 01:06:32.488
is sort of in control.

01:06:32.521 --> 01:06:33.722
- Yeah, what does it
look like in the brain

01:06:33.755 --> 01:06:38.594
when you aren't in control of
your thoughts and behavior?

01:06:39.728 --> 01:06:41.463 align:start size:81% position:19%
If we can tease
out the difference,

01:06:41.496 --> 01:06:44.366 align:start size:97% position:3%
then are we literally finding
like that is your agency

01:06:44.399 --> 01:06:46.802 align:start size:91% position:9%
that is your
consciousness, your will?

01:06:46.835 --> 01:06:49.638
And if it's gone, then you
feel like you're possessed.

01:06:49.671 --> 01:06:52.207
Which, by the way, brings us to

01:06:52.240 --> 01:06:55.077
our reverse exorcism episode,

01:06:55.110 --> 01:06:57.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
which you guys
should all check out.

01:06:57.446 --> 01:06:58.514
Keep me up to date
on what you're doing,

01:06:58.547 --> 01:07:02.284 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I really wanna
help in any way that I can.

01:07:02.317 --> 01:07:05.487 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, communicate all the
cool work that you're doing.

01:07:05.520 --> 01:07:07.790
So Dr. Veissière thanks.
- It's always a pleasure,

01:07:07.823 --> 01:07:09.491
- Always a pleasure.
- Thanks for having me.

01:07:09.524 --> 01:07:12.328 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Now, we're gonna
turn to a darker topic.

01:07:13.595 --> 01:07:14.663 line:0%
My own death.

01:07:15.697 --> 01:07:17.099 line:0%
Should I die?

01:07:18.533 --> 01:07:19.568 line:0%
Let's find out.

01:07:20.502 --> 01:07:22.805
(eerie music)

01:07:26.408 --> 01:07:27.609
Someday,

01:07:27.642 --> 01:07:28.644
I

01:07:28.677 --> 01:07:29.712
will die.

01:07:31.146 --> 01:07:32.648
But should I?

01:07:32.681 --> 01:07:34.316
If I was offered a longer life,

01:07:34.349 --> 01:07:36.318
I would take that in a second.

01:07:36.351 --> 01:07:39.055
But how long is too long?

01:07:40.255 --> 01:07:43.125
Is death something I
should deny forever?

01:07:44.526 --> 01:07:47.763
Or is death and the role
it plays in the universe,

01:07:47.796 --> 01:07:50.399 align:start size:84% position:16%
something I am
better off accepting?

01:07:52.534 --> 01:07:55.104
(upbeat music)

01:08:13.622 --> 01:08:15.657 align:start size:88% position:13%
I wanna start by looking
at a particular way

01:08:15.690 --> 01:08:18.661
death affects how we live
and treat one another.

01:08:20.695 --> 01:08:23.832 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
Terror Management Theory
proposes that people

01:08:23.865 --> 01:08:28.704 align:start position:0% line:0%
like you and me manage the
terror of death's inevitability

01:08:28.737 --> 01:08:31.640
by embracing cultural values.

01:08:31.673 --> 01:08:35.644
That the more aware a person
is of their own mortality,

01:08:35.677 --> 01:08:37.846
the more vehemently they will
enforce their particular views

01:08:37.879 --> 01:08:40.215
of the world on to others.

01:08:40.248 --> 01:08:43.185 align:start size:97% position:3%
Created by social
psychologists Sheldon Solomon,

01:08:43.218 --> 01:08:45.387 align:start size:81% position:19%
Jeff Greenberg,
and Tom Pyszczynski

01:08:45.420 --> 01:08:46.722
Terror Management Theory,

01:08:46.755 --> 01:08:51.627
or TMT suggests that often
we are afraid of change,

01:08:51.660 --> 01:08:53.795
because we're afraid of death.

01:08:53.828 --> 01:08:56.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
Each one of us has a
worldview, a set of beliefs,

01:08:56.631 --> 01:08:59.768 align:start size:97% position:3%
customs and norms we identify
with that can live on

01:08:59.801 --> 01:09:02.404
after our business
physical bodies die.

01:09:02.437 --> 01:09:05.707
TMT suggests that rises in
nationalism and prejudice

01:09:05.740 --> 01:09:09.611
are correlated with rises in
the salience of mortality,

01:09:09.644 --> 01:09:11.613
but is how present the
inevitability of death

01:09:11.646 --> 01:09:13.582
is in people's minds.

01:09:13.615 --> 01:09:15.817
This role that death
plays fascinates me

01:09:15.850 --> 01:09:18.954 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
and two have TMT
originators Jeff and Sheldon

01:09:18.987 --> 01:09:21.356 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
have agreed to work
with me on a pilot study

01:09:21.389 --> 01:09:22.958
of Terror Management Theory

01:09:22.991 --> 01:09:26.195 align:start size:81% position:19%
and real life
reminders of death.

01:09:26.228 --> 01:09:28.430
(soft music)

01:09:28.463 --> 01:09:30.499
What's your hypothesis today?

01:09:30.532 --> 01:09:34.636
Well, I think we're gonna
hope for the participants

01:09:34.669 --> 01:09:38.574 align:start size:97% position:3%
who are reminded of their
mortality to be more punitive,

01:09:38.607 --> 01:09:40.642
and their assessments.

01:09:40.675 --> 01:09:41.744
- Let's see what happens.

01:09:42.644 --> 01:09:44.346
- Good morning.

01:09:44.379 --> 01:09:46.582 line:0%
- [Michael] For our study, we
created a fake Research Center

01:09:46.615 --> 01:09:49.451 line:0%
staffed by actors and
invited participants

01:09:49.484 --> 01:09:51.753
to be a part of what they
were told was a focus group

01:09:51.786 --> 01:09:53.822 align:start size:78% position:22%
about the criminal
justice system.

01:09:53.855 --> 01:09:56.658 align:start size:88% position:13%
During the actual study,
each group will hear

01:09:56.691 --> 01:09:59.428 align:start position:0%
a list of several different
crimes that have been committed,

01:09:59.461 --> 01:10:01.797
and will then be asked
to propose a punishment

01:10:01.830 --> 01:10:03.899 align:start size:84% position:16%
for each offender
with a severity level

01:10:03.932 --> 01:10:07.769 align:start position:0%
ranging from one to seven, with
one being the most lenient,

01:10:07.802 --> 01:10:10.405 line:0%
and seven being the most severe.

01:10:10.438 --> 01:10:13.242 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
The control group will
simply enter the survey room

01:10:13.275 --> 01:10:15.444 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
and be asked to
answer the questions.

01:10:15.477 --> 01:10:18.480 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
The experimental group,
however, will first be exposed

01:10:18.513 --> 01:10:20.649 align:start size:81% position:19%
to reminders of
their own mortality

01:10:20.682 --> 01:10:23.552 align:start size:91% position:9%
with strategically placed
posters in the lobby.

01:10:23.585 --> 01:10:25.554 align:start size:88% position:13%
Also, the questionnaires
they fill out

01:10:25.587 --> 01:10:27.723
will include questions
about their own death.

01:10:29.357 --> 01:10:31.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
Decades of TMT research have
shown that when presented

01:10:31.793 --> 01:10:34.296
with violations of
common worldviews,

01:10:34.329 --> 01:10:36.632 align:start size:88% position:13%
those who are more aware
of their own deaths

01:10:36.665 --> 01:10:39.635 align:start size:84% position:16%
will recommend bigger
punishments for the
crimes presented.

01:10:39.668 --> 01:10:42.537 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
But will our real life
reminders of death,

01:10:42.570 --> 01:10:44.873 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
not just the survey
questions usually used

01:10:44.906 --> 01:10:46.642 line:0%
make a difference?

01:10:46.675 --> 01:10:48.711 line:0%
Well, first, let's look at
the control participants.

01:10:50.312 --> 01:10:52.314 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
- Thank you so much
for being here.

01:10:52.347 --> 01:10:53.682 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
Now, I cannot
emphasize this enough,

01:10:53.715 --> 01:10:55.751 line:0%
there are no right
or wrong answers.

01:10:55.784 --> 01:10:58.354 line:0%
This is just about your
gut level reactions.

01:10:59.054 --> 01:11:00.756
All right, let's begin.

01:11:02.390 --> 01:11:03.491 align:start size:81% position:19%
After raising
millions of dollars

01:11:03.525 --> 01:11:06.762 align:start size:97% position:3%
in grant money to fund
education for needy children,

01:11:06.795 --> 01:11:09.598 line:0%
a fundraising manager
unhappy with this life

01:11:09.631 --> 01:11:10.499 line:0%
fled with all the money

01:11:10.532 --> 01:11:12.934 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
and was arrested months
later in Tasmania

01:11:12.967 --> 01:11:15.671 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
where he was living
under a different name.

01:11:15.704 --> 01:11:18.974 line:0%
So one, his punishment
three months in prison,

01:11:19.007 --> 01:11:22.311 line:0%
seven is most severe,
10 years in prison.

01:11:22.344 --> 01:11:23.578
Please answer now.

01:11:23.611 --> 01:11:25.981 line:0%
(upbeat music)

01:11:27.782 --> 01:11:32.788 line:0%
- This is one that I think
does has views on both sides.

01:11:33.455 --> 01:11:34.323
- Yeah.

01:11:34.356 --> 01:11:35.524 line:0%
(bell ringing)

01:11:35.557 --> 01:11:36.692 line:0%
- [Michael] That
is a lot of sevens.

01:11:36.725 --> 01:11:39.661 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
If our control group is
already maxing out like that,

01:11:39.694 --> 01:11:42.030 line:0%
well then our scale has
no room in that direction

01:11:42.063 --> 01:11:44.599 line:0%
to show any effective
mortality salience.

01:11:44.632 --> 01:11:46.702
Discovering issues like this,

01:11:46.735 --> 01:11:49.671 align:start size:97% position:3%
learning how to better isolate
mortality salient's effect

01:11:49.704 --> 01:11:51.940
is exactly what a
pilot test is for.

01:11:51.973 --> 01:11:53.075
Hey!
- Hey.

01:11:53.108 --> 01:11:54.710
- Personal differences, huh?

01:11:54.743 --> 01:11:56.445
- Yeah.

01:11:56.478 --> 01:11:58.647
- An imposter with no medical
training posed as a surgeon

01:11:58.680 --> 01:12:01.850
and bungled a minor operation
to remove a child's tonsils.

01:12:01.883 --> 01:12:04.720
The patient recovered fully
after additional treatment.

01:12:04.753 --> 01:12:08.523 line:0%
One is six months on probation,
seven is 10 years in prison.

01:12:08.556 --> 01:12:09.691 line:0%
(upbeat music)

01:12:09.724 --> 01:12:13.829 line:0%
- Okay, if you are taking
on the persona of a doctor,

01:12:13.862 --> 01:12:15.698 line:0%
we expect good behavior.

01:12:16.664 --> 01:12:19.435 line:0%
