01:13:16.524 --> 01:13:18.493 line:0%
- Okay, thank you so
much for your time.

01:13:18.526 --> 01:13:19.628 line:0%
I really appreciate it.

01:13:19.661 --> 01:13:21.596 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
- All right, so
here's the results

01:13:21.629 --> 01:13:23.965
and the mathematical analysis.

01:13:23.998 --> 01:13:26.001
These are averages per question.

01:13:26.034 --> 01:13:29.104
These are the averages and
medians per participant.

01:13:29.137 --> 01:13:31.640 align:start size:84% position:16%
- The fours are great,
the three is great.

01:13:31.673 --> 01:13:34.609
- But this is grounds
for optimism, at least.

01:13:34.642 --> 01:13:36.778
- Seven was the max
sentencing value

01:13:36.811 --> 01:13:39.548
and our control group
gave an average of 4.5.

01:13:40.915 --> 01:13:43.485 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm really happy with
that as a control group.

01:13:43.518 --> 01:13:44.753
- Absolutely.

01:13:44.786 --> 01:13:46.455
- [Michael] Now, our
experimental groups.

01:13:47.622 --> 01:13:49.224
Remember, they will
be seeing posters

01:13:49.257 --> 01:13:51.693
that remind them of
their own mortality

01:13:51.726 --> 01:13:54.596 align:start position:0%
and will be asked different
questions in their questionnaire

01:13:54.629 --> 01:13:57.599
for example, please
describe the emotions

01:13:57.632 --> 01:14:01.002
that the thought of your
own death arouses in you.

01:14:01.035 --> 01:14:04.506 align:start size:88% position:13%
And write down as
specifically as you can,

01:14:04.539 --> 01:14:07.609
what you think will happen
to you physically as you die.

01:14:08.776 --> 01:14:11.513 align:start size:91% position:9%
The point is to prime
their mortality salience.

01:14:11.546 --> 01:14:12.747
Let's see if this group

01:14:12.780 --> 01:14:15.217 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
is more punitive towards
worldview violations.

01:14:17.619 --> 01:14:20.088 line:0%
- After raising millions of
dollars to fund education

01:14:20.121 --> 01:14:22.824 line:0%
for needy children,
a fundraising manager

01:14:22.857 --> 01:14:23.992 line:0%
fled with all the money

01:14:24.025 --> 01:14:26.628 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
and was arrested months
later in Tasmania.

01:14:26.661 --> 01:14:28.697 line:0%
One, three months in prison,

01:14:28.730 --> 01:14:31.933 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
seven, 10 years in
prison, please answer now.

01:14:31.966 --> 01:14:35.003 line:0%
(upbeat music)

01:14:35.036 --> 01:14:36.505
- He's thinking about it.

01:14:37.539 --> 01:14:38.841 line:0%
- Please hold up your answers.

01:14:40.775 --> 01:14:42.644 line:0%
All right, thank you so much.

01:14:42.677 --> 01:14:43.712
- Ah, okay.

01:14:44.712 --> 01:14:45.814 line:0%
(bell ringing)

01:14:45.847 --> 01:14:48.617 line:0%
(upbeat music)

01:14:48.650 --> 01:14:51.520 line:0%
- An imposter with no medical
training posed as a surgeon

01:14:51.553 --> 01:14:55.023 line:0%
and bungled a minor operation
to remove a child's tonsils.

01:14:55.056 --> 01:14:59.694 line:0%
One, six months on probation,
seven, 10 years in prison.

01:14:59.727 --> 01:15:03.098 line:0%
(upbeat music)

01:15:03.131 --> 01:15:05.300
- They are thinking a lot more.

01:15:05.333 --> 01:15:06.134 line:0%
- Yeah.

01:15:06.167 --> 01:15:08.737 line:0%
(upbeat music)

01:15:14.075 --> 01:15:15.911 line:0%
(laughing)

01:15:15.944 --> 01:15:19.147 align:start position:0% line:0%
- A 10, I'm pretty sure she
knows that seven is the highest.

01:15:19.180 --> 01:15:20.615
- We'll call it a seven yeah.

01:15:20.648 --> 01:15:22.717
- It's funny to see when
people feel bold enough,

01:15:22.750 --> 01:15:25.053
even though I'm like
breaking the bounds

01:15:25.086 --> 01:15:27.088
and the rules of the task.

01:15:27.121 --> 01:15:27.956 line:0%
(bell ringing)

01:15:27.989 --> 01:15:30.992 line:0%
(upbeat music)

01:15:31.025 --> 01:15:32.961 line:0%
- An anti government
protester was arrested

01:15:32.994 --> 01:15:34.596
for painting profanities

01:15:34.629 --> 01:15:36.798 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
at the Lincoln Memorial
in Washington DC.

01:15:38.132 --> 01:15:40.702 line:0%
(upbeat music)

01:15:40.735 --> 01:15:43.338 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- I really do appreciate
the way they clearly

01:15:43.371 --> 01:15:46.975 line:0%
seem to be taking a bit
more time to deliberate.

01:15:47.008 --> 01:15:48.276 line:0%
- Yeah.

01:15:48.309 --> 01:15:50.879 line:0%
(upbeat music)

01:15:54.115 --> 01:15:54.950 line:0%
(bell ringing)

01:15:54.983 --> 01:15:58.253 line:0%
(upbeat music)

01:15:58.286 --> 01:15:59.221
- Okay, they can
put the papers down

01:15:59.254 --> 01:16:02.090
and tell them that we
will be in shortly.

01:16:02.123 --> 01:16:03.291
- Okay, thank you so much.

01:16:03.324 --> 01:16:04.425
We finished with this
part of the study.

01:16:04.459 --> 01:16:06.795
So if you don't mind just
hanging out for a moment.

01:16:06.828 --> 01:16:08.763
And our researchers will
be in here in a moment

01:16:08.796 --> 01:16:10.765
to ask you a couple questions.

01:16:10.798 --> 01:16:13.234
- Let's find out if the
reminders of mortality,

01:16:13.267 --> 01:16:16.605
we showed our experimental
group were salient enough.

01:16:16.638 --> 01:16:17.639
- Let me ask you
about one thing,

01:16:17.672 --> 01:16:19.207
out in the waiting room,

01:16:19.240 --> 01:16:21.710
did you all notice
the posters at all?

01:16:21.743 --> 01:16:22.778
- [All] Yes.

01:16:24.112 --> 01:16:25.780
- Okay.

01:16:25.813 --> 01:16:26.948 line:0%
- I was surprised as soon
as they walked in the door,

01:16:26.981 --> 01:16:28.283 line:0%
and I saw the gravestones.

01:16:29.150 --> 01:16:30.852 line:0%
- Yeah, that's right.

01:16:30.885 --> 01:16:33.888 line:0%
- I wondered what
did I get into?

01:16:33.921 --> 01:16:35.857 align:start size:81% position:19%
- So we are looking
into something

01:16:35.890 --> 01:16:39.194
that's called Terror
Management Theory.

01:16:39.227 --> 01:16:42.831 align:start position:0%
And it's the idea that your
own awareness of your mortality

01:16:42.864 --> 01:16:46.635 align:start size:88% position:13%
can affect the behaviors
that you exhibit,

01:16:46.668 --> 01:16:49.137 align:start size:84% position:16%
that we all manage the
terror that we feel

01:16:49.170 --> 01:16:52.674
knowing that we are mortal,
by behaving in certain ways,

01:16:52.707 --> 01:16:55.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
especially in ways that
reinforce our own worldviews

01:16:55.777 --> 01:16:58.680
because we can kind of live
on through the societies

01:16:58.713 --> 01:17:01.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
and cultures and identities
that we have today.

01:17:01.349 --> 01:17:03.018 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Did any of you feel like
you were still thinking

01:17:03.051 --> 01:17:04.219
a little bit about death

01:17:04.252 --> 01:17:06.121
when you were came in here?

01:17:06.154 --> 01:17:08.289
- I was definitely
going after people

01:17:08.322 --> 01:17:11.393
who transgressed against my
worldview to use your terms.

01:17:11.426 --> 01:17:12.694
Yes, I noticed that.

01:17:12.727 --> 01:17:14.195 align:start size:84% position:16%
Yes, I was definitely
doing that.

01:17:14.228 --> 01:17:16.364 align:start size:81% position:19%
- So this was
incredibly helpful.

01:17:16.397 --> 01:17:18.734 align:start size:88% position:13%
Thank you very much
for your participation.

01:17:19.901 --> 01:17:22.671 align:start size:97% position:3%
It looks like our experimental
stimuli were successful.

01:17:22.704 --> 01:17:25.006
They were salient, but they
didn't cause the participants

01:17:25.039 --> 01:17:27.442
to think they were
related to the study.

01:17:27.475 --> 01:17:32.047 align:start size:91% position:9%
- The control participants
averaged about 4.5.

01:17:32.080 --> 01:17:35.984 align:start size:97% position:3%
The experimental participants
were close to 4.7.

01:17:37.452 --> 01:17:41.723
So there's a slight tendency
for the experimental people

01:17:41.756 --> 01:17:44.325 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be leaning in the
direction that we predicted.

01:17:44.358 --> 01:17:47.295
But we're talking
about relatively

01:17:47.328 --> 01:17:48.697
inconsequential differences.

01:17:48.730 --> 01:17:49.898
- That's right.

01:17:49.931 --> 01:17:51.800 align:start size:88% position:13%
It just makes me hungry
to run more people.

01:17:51.833 --> 01:17:52.634
And with the number that we had,

01:17:52.667 --> 01:17:55.070 align:start size:81% position:19%
That's statistically
insignificant.

01:17:55.103 --> 01:17:58.106
Do you think that we
did see any effects

01:17:58.139 --> 01:18:00.141
of mortality salience today?

01:18:00.174 --> 01:18:02.711 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I feel like the
mortality salient groups

01:18:02.744 --> 01:18:05.180
tended to take a little
longer before responding

01:18:05.213 --> 01:18:06.114
- Yeah me, too.

01:18:06.147 --> 01:18:07.482
- And they seem more thoughtful.

01:18:07.515 --> 01:18:10.118
they really put more
effort into trying

01:18:10.151 --> 01:18:11.319
to do the right thing.

01:18:11.352 --> 01:18:12.454
- The difference
was dramatic enough

01:18:12.487 --> 01:18:13.488
that we picked up on it.

01:18:13.521 --> 01:18:14.622
- Absolutely.

01:18:14.656 --> 01:18:16.324 align:start position:0%
- [Michael] Although our
stimuli might need to go through

01:18:16.357 --> 01:18:18.159 line:0%
a couple more passes
and some more vetting,

01:18:18.192 --> 01:18:20.762 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
we did find an interesting
difference in the time

01:18:20.795 --> 01:18:22.430 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
it took for our
groups to respond.

01:18:22.463 --> 01:18:25.033 line:0%
Our control group took an
average of four minutes

01:18:25.066 --> 01:18:27.936 align:start size:88% position:13%
and 46 seconds to decide
on their punishments.

01:18:27.969 --> 01:18:30.271
But our experimental
group took an average

01:18:30.304 --> 01:18:33.041
of seven minutes, 18 seconds.

01:18:33.074 --> 01:18:36.077 align:start size:88% position:13%
- In a sense that really
is the prediction,

01:18:36.110 --> 01:18:37.779
the right thing by
their own worldview.

01:18:37.812 --> 01:18:40.115 align:start size:91% position:9%
But by the same token,
when we think about death,

01:18:40.148 --> 01:18:41.449
we wanna do what's right.

01:18:41.482 --> 01:18:42.984
And if we're acting like jurors,

01:18:43.017 --> 01:18:45.787
we wanna make the
right decisions.

01:18:45.820 --> 01:18:47.756
- As we very much learned today,

01:18:47.789 --> 01:18:51.359
the goal isn't to prove one
thing one way or the other.

01:18:51.392 --> 01:18:54.095
It's just to reduce uncertainty.

01:18:54.128 --> 01:18:56.097 align:start size:81% position:19%
In the most careful
way possible.

01:18:56.130 --> 01:18:57.799
- Absolutely.

01:18:57.832 --> 01:18:59.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
To know a little bit more
today than we did yesterday.

01:19:01.836 --> 01:19:04.439
- Whoa, okay, we have
a lot to talk about.

01:19:04.472 --> 01:19:06.441 align:start size:91% position:9%
And luckily, I've got just
the person here with me

01:19:06.474 --> 01:19:09.344 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
to talk about all
of this with Judy Ho

01:19:09.377 --> 01:19:11.513 line:0%
a clinical neuropsychologist,

01:19:11.546 --> 01:19:15.517 align:start position:0% line:0%
tenured professor at Pepperdine,
you run your own practice,

01:19:15.550 --> 01:19:17.852
you work with ethics a lot.

01:19:17.885 --> 01:19:19.487
What is your actual position?

01:19:19.520 --> 01:19:22.123
- So I'm the chair of the
institutional review board

01:19:22.156 --> 01:19:23.825
at Pepperdine University,

01:19:23.858 --> 01:19:25.126 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is how we first
started working together

01:19:25.159 --> 01:19:27.529
because you were doing
the trolley experiment.

01:19:27.562 --> 01:19:29.197 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that was an interesting
thing to talk about.

01:19:29.230 --> 01:19:31.800 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- Yes, if you have seen
the trolley problem episode

01:19:31.833 --> 01:19:34.836 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
of Mind Field you
have seen Judy there

01:19:34.869 --> 01:19:36.004 line:0%
helping us with the ethics

01:19:36.037 --> 01:19:38.506 line:0%
of can we make people think
they've committed murder?

01:19:38.539 --> 01:19:39.474 line:0%
Is that okay?

01:19:39.507 --> 01:19:41.543
Maybe it's not okay for
the university to do,

01:19:41.576 --> 01:19:43.545
but can a TV show do it?

01:19:43.578 --> 01:19:45.346
Bottom line is we learned a lot.

01:19:45.379 --> 01:19:48.516
And so, Judy, I wanted you
here today to talk with me

01:19:48.549 --> 01:19:53.221 align:start size:91% position:9%
about running experiments
on people, okay.

01:19:53.254 --> 01:19:57.993 align:start size:94% position:6%
But also running experiments
on people for a TV show.

01:19:59.160 --> 01:20:01.262
Because there are all
kinds of limitations

01:20:01.295 --> 01:20:02.396
and issues that come up.

01:20:02.430 --> 01:20:07.101
And I think that what we
just saw is a great example

01:20:07.134 --> 01:20:10.271
of all the different
things that happen,

01:20:10.304 --> 01:20:11.840
that are both good and bad,

01:20:11.873 --> 01:20:13.942 align:start size:94% position:6%
challenges and
opportunities for Mind Field

01:20:13.975 --> 01:20:15.977 align:start size:91% position:9%
when it comes to
psychological experiments.

01:20:16.010 --> 01:20:17.178
- Sure.

01:20:17.211 --> 01:20:20.014
- So the biggest
problem we have is,

01:20:20.047 --> 01:20:22.283
it's not possible for us to run

01:20:22.316 --> 01:20:25.386
enormous numbers
of participants.

01:20:25.419 --> 01:20:27.555
We have a very tight schedule,

01:20:27.588 --> 01:20:29.858
and we have a whole
crew working with us,

01:20:29.891 --> 01:20:32.460
we cannot run hundreds
or thousands of people,

01:20:32.493 --> 01:20:34.896
we can run like a couple dozen.

01:20:36.097 --> 01:20:39.634 align:start size:88% position:13%
And sometimes that's
not enough to really get

01:20:39.667 --> 01:20:43.204 align:start size:81% position:19%
a statistically
significant result.

01:20:43.237 --> 01:20:44.439
- Right.

01:20:44.472 --> 01:20:47.576 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Also, I think it's
very clear here.

01:20:49.277 --> 01:20:53.514
The kinds of participants
who come on to the show,

01:20:53.547 --> 01:20:55.483
they don't even know
they're on a TV show,

01:20:55.516 --> 01:20:57.218 align:start size:88% position:13%
by the way until the end
when we debrief them,

01:20:57.251 --> 01:21:01.623
but the kinds of people who
are available here in LA,

01:21:01.656 --> 01:21:03.191
in the middle of the day.

01:21:03.224 --> 01:21:04.059
- Right.

01:21:05.660 --> 01:21:07.562
- Is a both a very narrow

01:21:07.595 --> 01:21:09.497
and a very wide group of people.

01:21:09.530 --> 01:21:10.465
- Right.

01:21:10.498 --> 01:21:11.666
- If we were running
this at a University,

01:21:11.699 --> 01:21:14.335 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we needed people to be
free in the middle of the day,

01:21:14.368 --> 01:21:16.471
we would get a very
homogenous group.

01:21:16.504 --> 01:21:18.039
- Yeah.

01:21:18.072 --> 01:21:19.507 align:start size:91% position:9%
- They would be
predominantly young people

01:21:19.540 --> 01:21:21.342
between like 19 and 22,

01:21:21.375 --> 01:21:23.444 align:start size:84% position:16%
their worldviews would
be pretty similar.

01:21:23.477 --> 01:21:26.981
I mean, they're all at
the same institution,

01:21:27.014 --> 01:21:29.617 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're probably socio
economically pretty well off,

01:21:29.650 --> 01:21:31.619
because they're at
this university.

01:21:31.652 --> 01:21:36.391
Their cultural backgrounds
might be much more similar

01:21:36.424 --> 01:21:39.027
than what we find working in TV,

01:21:39.060 --> 01:21:43.398 align:start size:91% position:9%
where we might get retired
people and immigrants

01:21:43.431 --> 01:21:47.235
and really young people and
actors, a lot of actors.

01:21:47.268 --> 01:21:48.436
- Right, yes.

01:21:48.469 --> 01:21:50.204 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Oh, my gosh, the
number of people in LA,

01:21:50.237 --> 01:21:52.573
who were free to do studies
in the middle of the day,

01:21:52.606 --> 01:21:56.311
who part time actors is a huge,

01:21:56.344 --> 01:21:59.547
which that always leads to the
issue of the audience at home

01:21:59.580 --> 01:22:01.649
goes, wait a second, and
they Google up something

01:22:01.682 --> 01:22:04.585
and they go, that person
was on a TV show, right?

01:22:04.618 --> 01:22:06.454
Is this all just fake?

01:22:06.487 --> 01:22:08.556 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's like,
that's what it's like

01:22:08.589 --> 01:22:10.525
to just grab who's
available in LA.

01:22:11.659 --> 01:22:13.995 align:start size:84% position:16%
But because of the
diversity that we get,

01:22:15.563 --> 01:22:19.600 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think our populations
represent the country better.

01:22:19.633 --> 01:22:22.236
But also, we have
way more noise.

01:22:22.269 --> 01:22:23.538
- Yes.

01:22:23.571 --> 01:22:26.507
- Questions about how
bad is it to deface

01:22:26.540 --> 01:22:29.544 align:start size:81% position:19%
an American monument
are going to have

01:22:29.577 --> 01:22:33.514
very different answers
from different people.

01:22:33.547 --> 01:22:35.183
- Absolutely.

01:22:35.216 --> 01:22:38.286
- And in this particular
study, we had that problem.

01:22:38.319 --> 01:22:40.388 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're trying to see if you'll
enforce your worldview,

01:22:40.421 --> 01:22:41.723
more or less if you're reminded

01:22:41.756 --> 01:22:43.691
that you're going
to die someday,

01:22:43.724 --> 01:22:45.660 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the issue was that
everyone showing up

01:22:45.693 --> 01:22:47.495
had very different worldviews.

01:22:47.528 --> 01:22:48.629
- Yes.

01:22:48.662 --> 01:22:49.564
All right, so I've just thrown

01:22:49.597 --> 01:22:50.498
a whole bunch of stuff at you,

01:22:50.531 --> 01:22:52.000
- A lot of stuff to talk about.

01:22:52.033 --> 01:22:53.134 align:start size:91% position:9%
Well, first of all, I
thought it was fascinating

01:22:53.167 --> 01:22:54.602
the way that it was set up.

01:22:54.635 --> 01:22:56.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was a really, really
good episode to watch.

01:22:56.704 --> 01:22:59.474 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, just to see the
reactions of the proponents

01:22:59.507 --> 01:23:00.541
of the theory that was so cool

01:23:00.574 --> 01:23:01.408
that you got them to be in it.

01:23:01.442 --> 01:23:03.678 align:start size:88% position:13%
'Cause they are
invested in the theory.

01:23:05.179 --> 01:23:06.381
- That was so cool.

01:23:06.414 --> 01:23:08.483
And both of them wrote
all of those scenarios.

01:23:08.516 --> 01:23:13.421
And it was truly
science in action.

01:23:13.454 --> 01:23:15.356
They came up with scenarios,

01:23:15.389 --> 01:23:18.292
we found out Wow, some of
these are just so extreme,

01:23:18.325 --> 01:23:20.695 align:start size:91% position:9%
even to a control group
that we don't see any room

01:23:20.728 --> 01:23:24.365
for any other variables to
change in that direction.

01:23:24.398 --> 01:23:28.036
So we had to rewrite
them in between days,

01:23:28.069 --> 01:23:31.439
and gosh, it was true science.

01:23:31.472 --> 01:23:33.408
- Yeah, no, it was really
cool to see it in action.

01:23:33.441 --> 01:23:35.676
And it was really cool
to see how different

01:23:35.709 --> 01:23:37.612
the control group and the
experimental group was

01:23:37.645 --> 01:23:40.681
in terms of the latency and
even their facial responses,

01:23:40.714 --> 01:23:42.683 align:start size:88% position:13%
as they were
considering all of this.

01:23:42.716 --> 01:23:45.219 align:start size:91% position:9%
I certainly saw that
being a significant effect

01:23:45.252 --> 01:23:46.621
that they just seemed
like they were taking

01:23:46.654 --> 01:23:49.123 align:start size:84% position:16%
their roles so much
more seriously, right.

01:23:49.156 --> 01:23:50.491
Whereas sometimes when
you get these people

01:23:50.524 --> 01:23:52.560
to come in for an
experimental study,

01:23:52.593 --> 01:23:53.694
they're just here
for their whatever,

01:23:53.728 --> 01:23:56.597 align:start size:97% position:3%
$10 or whatever you're
offering them for their time.

01:23:56.630 --> 01:23:58.733
And, they're more
haphazard about it.

01:23:58.766 --> 01:24:00.701 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I certainly saw that
with a second group,

01:24:00.734 --> 01:24:05.406
there was just this real
pronounced sense of gravity

01:24:05.439 --> 01:24:07.108
to their considerations.

01:24:07.141 --> 01:24:08.810
- Yeah, we thought,

01:24:08.843 --> 01:24:13.048 align:start position:0%
what the severity of punishment
dealt out, that will be

01:24:14.415 --> 01:24:17.819 align:start size:97% position:3%
the variable that will change
based on mortality sale.

01:24:17.852 --> 01:24:21.289 align:start position:0%
But it turned out to be
disposition and thoughtfulness.

01:24:21.322 --> 01:24:22.457
- Totally.

01:24:22.490 --> 01:24:24.459 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Luckily, we recorded
the whole thing.

01:24:24.492 --> 01:24:27.195 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we could go back and
say, how long did they take?

01:24:27.228 --> 01:24:28.463
That wasn't originally measured,

01:24:28.496 --> 01:24:30.498 align:start size:88% position:13%
but because of the
footage, we could time.

01:24:30.531 --> 01:24:32.867
And we found out that it
took almost twice as long

01:24:32.900 --> 01:24:34.602
for the group that
came in thinking,

01:24:34.635 --> 01:24:35.837
I've just written an essay

01:24:35.870 --> 01:24:38.573
about what I think
death will be like.

01:24:38.606 --> 01:24:41.609 align:start size:91% position:9%
Punishing people for
violating cultural norms.

01:24:41.642 --> 01:24:44.679
This is a heavy thing, too,
and they reacted differently.

01:24:44.712 --> 01:24:47.348
- Yeah, and I think
that it may not be

01:24:47.381 --> 01:24:48.850
I mean, I understand
the original tenants

01:24:48.883 --> 01:24:50.284
of Terror Management Theory.

01:24:50.317 --> 01:24:52.620
But I can also imagine
when somebody is really,

01:24:52.653 --> 01:24:54.789
really very cognizant
of their mortality,

01:24:54.822 --> 01:24:57.191 align:start size:88% position:13%
that they might actually
be more lenient,

01:24:57.224 --> 01:24:59.427
like life is short,
I want this person

01:24:59.460 --> 01:25:00.661
to have a second chance.

01:25:00.694 --> 01:25:02.230
And so I think part of
what wasn't measured

01:25:02.263 --> 01:25:03.631
that might have been helpful

01:25:03.664 --> 01:25:06.634 align:start size:97% position:3%
is actually trying to
counterbalance the two groups

01:25:06.667 --> 01:25:10.471
in terms of do you have the
same types of belief systems,

01:25:10.504 --> 01:25:11.739
and ideologies, right?

01:25:11.772 --> 01:25:13.474
Because if we don't have that,

01:25:13.507 --> 01:25:15.643
then they're kind of just
going at their own values.

01:25:15.676 --> 01:25:17.178
And if we don't know
very much about it,

01:25:17.211 --> 01:25:19.180
you can see why that
noise could contribute

01:25:19.213 --> 01:25:21.682
to the average being 4.5
and 4.7 in the two groups.

01:25:21.715 --> 01:25:23.851
- Exactly, which is basically
no difference at all right?

01:25:23.884 --> 01:25:25.453
- Right, yeah.

01:25:25.486 --> 01:25:27.421 align:start size:97% position:3%
- But yes, I think in
hindsight, and this is, again,

01:25:27.454 --> 01:25:29.490 align:start size:84% position:16%
the whole point of
science is to be like,

01:25:29.523 --> 01:25:31.225
all right, what can we learn?

01:25:31.258 --> 01:25:33.594
And how can we keep at all times

01:25:33.627 --> 01:25:37.798
the uncertainty on this
track down and down?

01:25:37.831 --> 01:25:40.234
And right, if we kind of knew

01:25:40.267 --> 01:25:42.270 align:start size:88% position:13%
what people's worldviews
were ahead of time,

01:25:42.303 --> 01:25:45.473 align:start size:97% position:3%
like if we had interviewed
them a couple of weeks before,

01:25:45.506 --> 01:25:48.876 align:start position:0%
long enough ago, that they don't
remember it anymore, right?

01:25:48.909 --> 01:25:51.445
Make them somehow not
think that it matters

01:25:51.478 --> 01:25:53.614 align:start size:84% position:16%
too much that the
questions are related,

01:25:53.647 --> 01:25:57.285
then the test, I think, could
have given us better results

01:25:57.318 --> 01:26:00.555
in terms of the punishments
dealt out and all of that.

01:26:00.588 --> 01:26:02.623
And it can be frustrating
when you wanna do a show,

01:26:02.656 --> 01:26:04.659
and you wanna show
results and teach,

01:26:04.692 --> 01:26:06.294
but you also want to experiment.

01:26:07.661 --> 01:26:09.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
And sometimes the experiment
won't always leave you

01:26:09.630 --> 01:26:11.632
with everything nicely tied up.

01:26:11.665 --> 01:26:12.600
- Right.

01:26:12.633 --> 01:26:14.502
- And that's a good
lesson to give people

01:26:14.535 --> 01:26:18.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
but it has been frustrating
to do experiments

01:26:18.305 --> 01:26:19.941
where you need a big
population of people,

01:26:19.974 --> 01:26:22.343
it's easier on the Mind Field,

01:26:22.376 --> 01:26:24.345
to make myself the guinea pig.

01:26:24.378 --> 01:26:25.613
Or focus on a topic where

01:26:25.646 --> 01:26:27.281
we just need to see
it happen one time.

01:26:27.314 --> 01:26:28.515
- Right, right.

01:26:28.549 --> 01:26:31.419
- Like can I get someone to
falsely confessed to a crime?

01:26:31.452 --> 01:26:33.588
If I get one person to
do it, then we're done.

01:26:33.621 --> 01:26:35.990
But if I wanna see a
tendency for people

01:26:36.023 --> 01:26:38.693 align:start size:81% position:19%
to be more punitive
to worldview,

01:26:38.726 --> 01:26:43.598
their worldview violations,
if they're reminded of death,

01:26:43.631 --> 01:26:44.966
I really need to run
hundreds of people.

01:26:44.999 --> 01:26:47.235 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I mean, what do
you what do you think?

01:26:48.769 --> 01:26:51.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do we need to remind the
audience of that every time?

01:26:51.739 --> 01:26:52.673
- Honestly, I don't think so.

01:26:52.706 --> 01:26:54.442
And I would say, first of all,

01:26:54.475 --> 01:26:57.311 align:start size:84% position:16%
that social
psychology experiences
(mumbles) especially,

01:26:57.344 --> 01:26:58.646
are built on the backbone

01:26:58.679 --> 01:27:00.481
of very homogenous populations,

01:27:01.682 --> 01:27:04.485 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's tended to always involve
undergraduate students

01:27:04.518 --> 01:27:07.421 align:start size:84% position:16%
who are given the
option of either doing

01:27:07.454 --> 01:27:10.324
this 10 minute experiment
or writing a 15 page paper.

01:27:10.357 --> 01:27:12.660
It's not really very
ethical, actually.

01:27:12.693 --> 01:27:15.563
Because you're kind of coercing
them towards the experiment.

01:27:15.596 --> 01:27:16.764
And so you're gonna
get lots of first

01:27:16.797 --> 01:27:18.933 align:start size:81% position:19%
and second year
psychology students.

01:27:18.966 --> 01:27:21.302
And, again, it's
still for a class.

01:27:21.335 --> 01:27:22.970
So how honest are
they really being?

01:27:23.003 --> 01:27:25.573
Because what if the
professor discovers

01:27:25.606 --> 01:27:26.407
what their answers are?

01:27:26.440 --> 01:27:27.808 align:start size:78% position:22%
And so there's all
kinds of things

01:27:27.841 --> 01:27:30.678 align:start size:81% position:19%
that are already
inherently an issue,

01:27:30.711 --> 01:27:33.648 align:start size:97% position:3%
and even the published
studies and social psychology.

01:27:33.681 --> 01:27:36.651
And I think what a TV
experiment really does

01:27:36.684 --> 01:27:39.553
that I don't think
any more kind of,

01:27:39.586 --> 01:27:41.555
planned out research
study with hundreds

01:27:41.588 --> 01:27:43.591 align:start size:84% position:16%
and thousands of
participants will do,

01:27:43.624 --> 01:27:47.728
is a really lively and
visual demonstration.

01:27:47.761 --> 01:27:49.363
And that in itself
is really useful.

01:27:49.396 --> 01:27:50.598 align:start size:81% position:19%
Because if somebody
actually says,

01:27:50.631 --> 01:27:52.466 align:start size:81% position:19%
Now, I wanna take
that study protocol,

01:27:52.499 --> 01:27:53.601
and apply it to 500 people,

01:27:53.634 --> 01:27:54.869 align:start size:84% position:16%
they now have a
template to do that--

01:27:54.902 --> 01:27:56.570 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Which they should
do, by the way,

01:27:56.603 --> 01:27:58.839
please do that, that would be,

01:27:58.872 --> 01:28:00.708
Mind Field would
have done its job.

01:28:00.741 --> 01:28:04.011
But yeah, I think
the responsibility

01:28:04.044 --> 01:28:09.050
I have with Mind Field is to
document how science is done,

01:28:09.917 --> 01:28:10.818
and what results might look like

01:28:10.851 --> 01:28:12.353
and what the protocols can be.

01:28:12.386 --> 01:28:13.487
And then the comments

01:28:13.521 --> 01:28:15.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
might have been full of
people with different ideas,

01:28:15.456 --> 01:28:17.558 align:start size:84% position:16%
how it would have
been a differently
and I'm like, perfect.

01:28:17.591 --> 01:28:19.393
- Awesome that people wanna
weigh in and be so engaged--

01:28:19.426 --> 01:28:21.462
- Exactly go off and do that,

01:28:22.730 --> 01:28:25.366
we really didn't know how
that experiment would go.

01:28:25.399 --> 01:28:27.368 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I still don't
know how it would go

01:28:27.401 --> 01:28:29.837 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you change one little
thing in one way or the other.

01:28:29.870 --> 01:28:31.072 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Well, and I
think it also shows

01:28:31.105 --> 01:28:33.741
the beauty of what an
ad hoc analysis can do.

01:28:33.774 --> 01:28:35.509
Like oftentimes you
have your hypotheses,

01:28:35.542 --> 01:28:38.112
like, for example,
your hypothesis was
that you would go in

01:28:38.145 --> 01:28:40.715
and you would see that there
was gonna be more severity

01:28:40.748 --> 01:28:42.883
in terms of ratings
of punishment in the
experimental group.

01:28:42.916 --> 01:28:45.586
But actually, you
didn't see that.

01:28:45.619 --> 01:28:47.555 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then you thought that
something else was happening,

01:28:47.588 --> 01:28:48.956
because we saw the videotape.

01:28:48.989 --> 01:28:51.392
And, oh, it's how
long they took.

01:28:51.425 --> 01:28:53.094
And you were able to go
back and call that data

01:28:53.127 --> 01:28:54.495 align:start size:84% position:16%
and to make a
statement about that.

01:28:54.528 --> 01:28:56.664
And that difference
was significant.

01:28:56.697 --> 01:28:58.566
It was almost doubled
the amount of time

01:28:58.599 --> 01:29:01.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the experimental group,
it was cool to see that.

01:29:01.502 --> 01:29:02.703
- It was cool.

01:29:02.736 --> 01:29:04.372
And you could feel a
difference in the mood

01:29:04.405 --> 01:29:06.006
which it's hard to measure.

01:29:06.039 --> 01:29:08.008 align:start size:84% position:16%
But you know what,
when you are recording

01:29:08.041 --> 01:29:12.546 align:start position:0%
everything from multiple
angles, you have that evidence,

01:29:12.579 --> 01:29:14.014 align:start size:81% position:19%
right, you have
that documentation.

01:29:14.047 --> 01:29:15.750
- They seem stressed.

01:29:15.783 --> 01:29:17.017
Like, when I was watching
them, I felt bad for them.

01:29:17.050 --> 01:29:19.687
They they just looked so upset.

01:29:19.720 --> 01:29:20.621
- They weren't fun scenario,

01:29:20.655 --> 01:29:22.957 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's like hey, this
person botched a surgery

01:29:22.990 --> 01:29:25.526
because they lied about
their qualifications.

01:29:25.559 --> 01:29:27.595 align:start size:88% position:13%
Or this doctor got
drunk and hurt someone.

01:29:27.628 --> 01:29:29.530 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are pretty bad things
to think about, anyway,

01:29:29.563 --> 01:29:32.166
- This really just brings up
though, kind of the spirit

01:29:32.199 --> 01:29:34.435 align:start size:88% position:13%
of why we do experiments
in the first place,

01:29:34.468 --> 01:29:36.704
because it's always a
cost benefit analysis.

01:29:36.737 --> 01:29:37.638
And I think we talked about this

01:29:37.671 --> 01:29:39.407
also on the trolley episode.

01:29:39.440 --> 01:29:42.476 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's really about what kind
of information you're getting.

01:29:42.509 --> 01:29:43.711 align:start size:84% position:16%
Are you just torturing
people for fun

01:29:43.744 --> 01:29:45.780 align:start size:84% position:16%
and using your hidden
cameras for fun?

01:29:45.813 --> 01:29:48.582
Or are you providing some kind
of educational value or a way

01:29:48.615 --> 01:29:51.619 align:start position:0%
that people can process
important issues like mortality?

01:29:51.652 --> 01:29:53.921
When I first saw
you in the casket,

01:29:53.954 --> 01:29:56.190
I gotta tell you, that's
one of my worst fears.

01:29:56.223 --> 01:29:58.626 align:start position:0%
We talked about this on the
most recent episode I worked on

01:29:58.659 --> 01:30:01.529
with you that, that is my
biggest fear, is death.

01:30:01.562 --> 01:30:04.765
So that you are providing
some value and insight

01:30:04.798 --> 01:30:06.934
to people who are thinking
about how do I make the most

01:30:06.967 --> 01:30:08.169
of my time on this earth?

01:30:08.202 --> 01:30:10.771
And does it mean if I
take my moral decisions

01:30:10.804 --> 01:30:11.872
a little bit more carefully,

01:30:11.905 --> 01:30:13.607 align:start size:91% position:9%
that I'm gonna feel better
about myself in my life

01:30:13.640 --> 01:30:14.741
at the end of the day?

01:30:14.775 --> 01:30:16.911 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because that could be
providing some kind of insight

01:30:16.944 --> 01:30:18.579 align:start size:81% position:19%
for people to
better their lives.

01:30:18.612 --> 01:30:22.616 align:start position:0%
- Yeah, that's my favorite part
of every experiment we run,

01:30:22.649 --> 01:30:23.851 align:start size:81% position:19%
the debriefing
where I get to say,

01:30:23.884 --> 01:30:25.586
here's what we're studying.

01:30:25.619 --> 01:30:28.489
And people they've never gone,

01:30:28.522 --> 01:30:30.491
"Okay, cool, can I
get my money now?"

01:30:30.524 --> 01:30:31.859 align:start size:91% position:9%
They're always like,
well, they want the money.

01:30:31.892 --> 01:30:35.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
But also, of course,
they're like, wow, oh, yeah.

01:30:35.195 --> 01:30:38.566 align:start size:91% position:9%
'cause I was feeling this,
and, they're excited,

01:30:38.599 --> 01:30:40.634
to think about it on
the on the car home,

01:30:40.667 --> 01:30:42.937
and they're gonna tell their
friends about it that night.

01:30:42.970 --> 01:30:45.473
And I hope it does a
lot of good for them,

01:30:45.506 --> 01:30:46.674
even if sometimes,

01:30:46.707 --> 01:30:48.943
they may have been a little
bit scared halfway through.

01:30:48.976 --> 01:30:50.511
- Yeah, yeah, exactly.

01:30:50.544 --> 01:30:52.813
And I think that that
is really the core

01:30:52.846 --> 01:30:55.649
of why the experiments
are important on TV,

01:30:55.682 --> 01:30:58.052
because, you are gonna get
a more engaged audience.

01:30:58.085 --> 01:31:00.888
And if it encourages people
to think about themselves,

01:31:00.921 --> 01:31:02.923 align:start size:84% position:16%
and to encourage
personal development
to tell their friends

01:31:02.956 --> 01:31:05.192
and family, then I think
you've done a great job.

01:31:05.225 --> 01:31:07.027
So I don't think that you
have to necessarily say,

01:31:07.060 --> 01:31:08.696 align:start size:84% position:16%
and by the way, maybe
this experiment,

01:31:08.729 --> 01:31:10.664 align:start size:97% position:3%
will turn out different if
you had 500 people running it,

01:31:10.697 --> 01:31:13.100 align:start size:91% position:9%
because the visual
aspects of the experiment,

01:31:13.133 --> 01:31:15.636
as it is built is what's
triggering these people

01:31:15.669 --> 01:31:17.171
to really do that
self evaluation

01:31:17.204 --> 01:31:18.272
versus reading it on a page.

01:31:18.305 --> 01:31:20.908
I mean, that's part of the
problem with experiments

01:31:20.941 --> 01:31:23.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that they're in these
journals that you have to be

01:31:23.810 --> 01:31:24.978
a psychologist to subscribe

01:31:25.011 --> 01:31:28.148 align:start position:0%
or just a really interested
person who's not a psychologist,

01:31:28.181 --> 01:31:30.585
that you wanna pay
your $500 a year.

01:31:31.952 --> 01:31:34.121
- Because you're getting like
a weekly thing, and it's huge.

01:31:34.154 --> 01:31:35.055
Oh, my gosh!

01:31:35.088 --> 01:31:36.690
- No one's gonna read that.

01:31:36.723 --> 01:31:37.791 align:start position:0%
So this is a great way for
people to understand experiments

01:31:37.824 --> 01:31:40.294 align:start size:91% position:9%
and not feel afraid, a lot
of people will tell me,

01:31:40.327 --> 01:31:41.762
I'm afraid to run experiments.

01:31:41.795 --> 01:31:42.930
That seems like a
lot of work to do.

01:31:42.963 --> 01:31:45.032
Well, the way that it was
broken down in the episode,

01:31:45.065 --> 01:31:47.635
it doesn't really
look like people

01:31:47.668 --> 01:31:49.670 align:start size:88% position:13%
can't do a version of
it themselves sometimes.

01:31:49.703 --> 01:31:50.804
- It did take a lot of work.

01:31:50.838 --> 01:31:52.840 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have the best crew ever--
- You made it look easy.

01:31:52.873 --> 01:31:55.976
- Yeah, we made it look
easy, love to hear that.

01:31:56.009 --> 01:31:59.113
I also think what we
just saw is a good way

01:31:59.146 --> 01:32:02.716 align:start size:81% position:19%
to talk about
hidden camera shows.

01:32:02.749 --> 01:32:03.951
- Yeah.

01:32:03.984 --> 01:32:05.819
Because a common
question I get is,

01:32:05.852 --> 01:32:08.756
how do the people not
know they're on TV?

01:32:08.789 --> 01:32:10.725
How do they not see the cameras?

01:32:12.759 --> 01:32:16.297
And they don't, we
do a really good job.

01:32:16.330 --> 01:32:18.732
I mean, we're in LA,

01:32:18.765 --> 01:32:21.835
and I'm working with the
best hidden camera people.

01:32:21.868 --> 01:32:23.771
I mean, I go into the
room, and I'm like,

01:32:23.804 --> 01:32:25.005
but aren't we gonna film this?

01:32:25.038 --> 01:32:26.941
And they're like, we're
filming it right now.

01:32:26.974 --> 01:32:28.609
- From five cameras.

01:32:28.642 --> 01:32:30.110 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Yeah, the ways they
have the disguise cameras,

01:32:30.143 --> 01:32:33.113
I've always said we need
to cover that on the show.

01:32:33.146 --> 01:32:36.350 align:start size:94% position:6%
So that people know how well
hidden the cameras were.

01:32:36.383 --> 01:32:37.751
- Yeah, 'cause they were
excellent angles too--

01:32:37.784 --> 01:32:39.620 align:start size:88% position:13%
you got really good--
- I know, but guess what

01:32:39.653 --> 01:32:41.355
the producers are like, no,

01:32:41.388 --> 01:32:43.257 align:start size:84% position:16%
because then people
will know the secrets,

01:32:43.290 --> 01:32:45.059
and they'll know
what to look at.

01:32:45.092 --> 01:32:46.760
Trade secrets, but also,

01:32:46.793 --> 01:32:48.662 align:start size:84% position:16%
they don't want future
participants to go.

01:32:48.695 --> 01:32:49.863
You know what?

01:32:49.896 --> 01:32:52.032
If I see that pattern,

01:32:52.065 --> 01:32:55.269
there's probably a
camera behind it.

01:32:55.302 --> 01:32:56.637
It works because
people don't know

01:32:56.670 --> 01:32:58.372
how we're hiding them so well.

01:32:58.405 --> 01:33:01.809
If you look at the
projectors screen,

01:33:01.842 --> 01:33:03.744 align:start size:88% position:13%
you can see that
there's a band across it

01:33:03.777 --> 01:33:04.878
of a different texture.

01:33:04.912 --> 01:33:07.114
And that is something that
a camera can see through.

01:33:07.147 --> 01:33:09.650 align:start size:91% position:9%
But in the room, you don't
think anything of it.

01:33:09.683 --> 01:33:12.052 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's just this boring
feature, and no one notices.

01:33:12.085 --> 01:33:14.054
And then when we tell
them, they're on TV,

01:33:14.087 --> 01:33:16.056
they're all like, what?

01:33:16.089 --> 01:33:19.727
So there's a whole
other permission form
they have to sign

01:33:19.760 --> 01:33:20.594
after they learn that,

01:33:20.628 --> 01:33:22.229 align:start size:81% position:19%
'cause we can't tell
them beforehand,

01:33:22.262 --> 01:33:24.999
or else people will act
different if they know

01:33:25.032 --> 01:33:26.734
that there's going
to be an audience

01:33:26.767 --> 01:33:27.768
out there watching later.

01:33:27.801 --> 01:33:28.869
- Yes, absolutely.

01:33:28.902 --> 01:33:31.138
And we see that a lot in
social psychology studies,

01:33:31.171 --> 01:33:33.907 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, even just the presence
of more people in the room

01:33:33.940 --> 01:33:35.676
can already impact
how they respond.

01:33:35.709 --> 01:33:38.846 align:start position:0%
So in this particular scenario,
there was one experimenter,

01:33:38.879 --> 01:33:40.381
it feels less like an audience.

01:33:40.414 --> 01:33:42.349
But sometimes you'll have
three or four experimenters

01:33:42.382 --> 01:33:44.785
and it does like really,

01:33:45.786 --> 01:33:48.856
like in a big way affect
how people start to deal

01:33:48.889 --> 01:33:50.691 align:start size:84% position:16%
with themselves,
deal with each other,

01:33:50.724 --> 01:33:52.660
you can sometimes see
them being more polite,

01:33:52.693 --> 01:33:54.728
a little bit more
ginger with things.

01:33:54.761 --> 01:33:56.263 align:start size:91% position:9%
And it's only because they
think that other people

01:33:56.296 --> 01:33:58.232 align:start size:88% position:13%
are watching and taking
notes about them.

01:33:58.265 --> 01:33:59.100
- You know what?

01:34:00.233 --> 01:34:01.735 align:start size:88% position:13%
It makes me wanna bring
up something else,

01:34:01.768 --> 01:34:02.836
we really need to get
back into the episode,

01:34:02.869 --> 01:34:05.339
but like, come on, I can
do whatever I want, right?

01:34:05.372 --> 01:34:09.710
So Mind Field began,

01:34:09.743 --> 01:34:11.211
as this idea I had for a show

01:34:11.244 --> 01:34:13.848
that at the time I
called Prankology,

01:34:14.881 --> 01:34:16.684
because when I was a
student in high school

01:34:16.717 --> 01:34:18.452 align:start size:84% position:16%
and college
psychology professors

01:34:18.485 --> 01:34:20.788 align:start size:81% position:19%
would often show
Candid Camera clips,

01:34:20.821 --> 01:34:22.856
and then talk about
the social psychology

01:34:22.889 --> 01:34:24.792
that was evident in those clips.

01:34:24.825 --> 01:34:27.027 align:start size:94% position:6%
The clips were entertaining,
the science was cool.

01:34:27.060 --> 01:34:28.729
I'm like, let's do that.

01:34:28.762 --> 01:34:32.132
and pranks were kind of a
thing because of Punked.

01:34:32.165 --> 01:34:34.468
This was a while back, right?

01:34:34.501 --> 01:34:38.772
But all of the pranks that
like networks wanted to do

01:34:38.805 --> 01:34:40.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
essentially only taught
one thing, fight or flight.

01:34:40.474 --> 01:34:42.176
They just wanted people
to get really scared

01:34:42.209 --> 01:34:43.911
and freaked out and
pee in their pants.

01:34:43.944 --> 01:34:46.780
Luckily, YouTube saw
that there was value

01:34:46.813 --> 01:34:48.248
in something like this.

01:34:48.281 --> 01:34:50.217 align:start size:81% position:19%
We're like, so is
anyone gonna scream?

01:34:50.250 --> 01:34:52.786
Is anyone gonna think
their car was totaled?

01:34:52.819 --> 01:34:55.489 align:start size:91% position:9%
And I'm like, no, they're
just gonna respond

01:34:55.522 --> 01:34:57.224
to a question with a number.

01:34:59.259 --> 01:35:02.730
Interestingly, you have
to make that interesting.

01:35:02.763 --> 01:35:04.364 align:start size:88% position:13%
How do you have someone
fill out a survey

01:35:04.397 --> 01:35:06.266
and have it be good visually?

01:35:06.299 --> 01:35:08.769
Well, we came up with the
idea of having them write

01:35:08.802 --> 01:35:12.740
their number and then show it
to the proctor of the study,

01:35:12.773 --> 01:35:14.475
so that the camera could
see what number they wrote.

01:35:14.508 --> 01:35:16.176
That's the whole reason
they did it that way,

01:35:16.209 --> 01:35:18.445 align:start size:84% position:16%
rather than fill it
in and turn the paper.

01:35:18.478 --> 01:35:19.980 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Which of course
changes results too.

01:35:20.013 --> 01:35:21.482
Because if it's a
private opinion,

01:35:21.515 --> 01:35:23.150 align:start position:0%
versus one that they're
showing their showing somebody.

01:35:23.183 --> 01:35:25.953
- They're showing, then their
face is associated with it.

01:35:25.986 --> 01:35:27.488
But last at the last minute,

01:35:27.521 --> 01:35:29.490
we had to put those blinders up.

01:35:29.523 --> 01:35:31.125 align:start size:81% position:19%
So they couldn't see
what other people

01:35:31.158 --> 01:35:32.359
next to them were doing.

01:35:32.392 --> 01:35:33.827
Because we knew that
that would affect it.

01:35:33.860 --> 01:35:35.996
So we had to walk
the line of, okay,

01:35:36.029 --> 01:35:37.798
we want them to
put the number up

01:35:37.831 --> 01:35:39.533 align:start size:78% position:22%
next to their face
for the camera,

01:35:39.566 --> 01:35:41.435
but we don't want that to affect

01:35:41.468 --> 01:35:42.870
how they answer the question.

01:35:42.903 --> 01:35:45.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
So how do we make sure
that it still feels

01:35:45.505 --> 01:35:46.840
as private as possible?

01:35:46.873 --> 01:35:48.008
- Right.

01:35:48.041 --> 01:35:49.343 align:start size:81% position:19%
- A lot of thinking
goes in this.

01:35:49.376 --> 01:35:51.078
- A lot of thinking,
a lot of thinking.

01:35:51.111 --> 01:35:54.314
And I also wonder to just
even the characteristics

01:35:54.347 --> 01:35:55.449
of the experimenter in the room,

01:35:55.482 --> 01:35:57.084
the actor that you guys hired.

01:35:57.117 --> 01:35:59.186
Again, with certain
of those questions,

01:35:59.219 --> 01:36:00.821
there could also be a response,

01:36:00.854 --> 01:36:04.525 align:start position:0%
of well, this person might
judge me if I rate this a seven,

01:36:04.558 --> 01:36:06.794 align:start size:81% position:19%
just based on their
own perception

01:36:06.827 --> 01:36:07.995
of what that person
might be thinking--

01:36:08.028 --> 01:36:10.564
- Exactly, that is something
that is worth investigating

01:36:10.597 --> 01:36:12.566
more experimenting with more.

01:36:12.599 --> 01:36:17.805
What I can say is that
during those trials,

01:36:19.272 --> 01:36:21.074
the direction we gave Trin,
who's our wonderful actress,

01:36:21.107 --> 01:36:23.477 align:start size:97% position:3%
she's just perfect at this
kind of role, and many others,

01:36:23.510 --> 01:36:27.080 align:start size:91% position:9%
but we told her, you have
to kind of act bored.

01:36:27.113 --> 01:36:29.049
Like you've been
doing this all day,

01:36:29.082 --> 01:36:30.484
and you don't care
what they write down.

01:36:30.517 --> 01:36:32.386
- Yeah, no gasps of surprise.

01:36:32.419 --> 01:36:35.289
- Yeah, you're not gonna be
like, oh, you gave it a one?

01:36:35.322 --> 01:36:38.926
Also, she just read a script,
she read each word off a page.

01:36:38.959 --> 01:36:41.094
So it was always
the same language.

01:36:41.127 --> 01:36:44.331
And it was just monotone, which
is a funny direction to give

01:36:44.364 --> 01:36:47.067
someone who is in a like
a really talented actress.

01:36:47.100 --> 01:36:49.069 align:start size:94% position:6%
To be like, we need you to--
- As boring as possible.

01:36:49.102 --> 01:36:50.304
- Be as boring as possible.

01:36:50.337 --> 01:36:52.306
And it's the least
acting as possible.

01:36:52.339 --> 01:36:53.473
- Right, right.

01:36:53.506 --> 01:36:54.975 align:start size:81% position:19%
- As a little
acting as possible.

01:36:55.008 --> 01:36:56.476 align:start size:88% position:13%
Judy, thank you so much.
- Thank you so fun.

01:36:56.509 --> 01:36:59.012 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Always a pleasure.
- Awesome.

01:36:59.045 --> 01:37:01.315 align:start position:0%
- All right, we're gonna
continue watching this episode,

01:37:01.348 --> 01:37:03.951
when we come back, I
will have a new guest,

01:37:03.984 --> 01:37:05.385
but not new to you or me.

01:37:05.418 --> 01:37:06.587
So actually not a
new guests at all,

01:37:06.620 --> 01:37:08.589
but I'll have a different guest.

01:37:08.622 --> 01:37:09.957
Let's get ready to learn more

01:37:09.990 --> 01:37:12.192 align:start size:81% position:19%
about whether
Michael should die.

01:37:12.225 --> 01:37:14.628
(upbeat music)

01:37:14.661 --> 01:37:17.497 align:start position:0%
Our pilot test shows that
there's still a lot to discover

01:37:17.530 --> 01:37:21.869
about terror management, and
many promising ways to do it.

01:37:21.902 --> 01:37:24.404 align:start size:91% position:9%
I'm particularly intrigued
by our observation

01:37:24.437 --> 01:37:26.607
that for all the
closed mindedness,

01:37:26.640 --> 01:37:30.043 align:start size:91% position:9%
mortality salience appears
to cause it also led

01:37:30.076 --> 01:37:34.581
to what looked like increased
consideration, and thought,

01:37:34.614 --> 01:37:36.950
I'd love to see more
research on that idea.

01:37:36.983 --> 01:37:38.952
But the point is this.

01:37:38.985 --> 01:37:43.590 align:start size:88% position:13%
If death's effects
aren't all entirely bad,

01:37:43.623 --> 01:37:47.494 align:start size:91% position:9%
what if, instead of, or
at least, at the same time

01:37:47.527 --> 01:37:50.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we hope for the
abolition of natural death,

01:37:50.931 --> 01:37:55.002
we also find a way to accept it.

01:37:55.035 --> 01:37:59.172
Now, obviously, I don't want
to die, at least not soon.

01:37:59.205 --> 01:38:01.909 align:start size:97% position:3%
But accepting the
inevitability of my own death

01:38:01.942 --> 01:38:06.947
and being less afraid of it
feels powerful, and honest.

01:38:08.081 --> 01:38:09.216 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'd like to learn
what that looks like.

01:38:09.249 --> 01:38:11.551 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I have a friend
who can help.

01:38:11.584 --> 01:38:14.188
(soft music)

01:38:16.456 --> 01:38:19.693 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm paying a visit to
Caitlin Doughty, a mortician,

01:38:19.726 --> 01:38:22.596
author and death
positivity activist

01:38:22.629 --> 01:38:24.631
who has made an entire
career out of discussing

01:38:24.664 --> 01:38:28.535 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
the aspects of death that
most of us prefer to ignore.

01:38:28.568 --> 01:38:31.638 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
What do you say to
someone who comes to you?

01:38:31.671 --> 01:38:35.142 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
and says, I think
death is terrifying.

01:38:35.175 --> 01:38:36.010
It's,

01:38:37.043 --> 01:38:39.546
so scary and sad that

01:38:40.613 --> 01:38:42.115
I'm just here now,

01:38:42.148 --> 01:38:43.050
- Is this person dying?

01:38:43.083 --> 01:38:44.284
Or is this person--

01:38:44.317 --> 01:38:45.452
- This person is me in
front of you right now.

01:38:45.485 --> 01:38:47.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
- This person is
you, okay so (laughs)

01:38:47.620 --> 01:38:49.222
I would tell you
a couple things.

01:38:49.255 --> 01:38:50.423
First, you're dealing

01:38:50.456 --> 01:38:55.362
with the primal existential
quandary of human existence.

01:38:55.395 --> 01:38:56.196
- Yes.

01:38:56.229 --> 01:38:57.531
- And you are one of,

01:38:57.564 --> 01:39:00.567 align:start size:91% position:9%
the many billions of
people who have felt this,

01:39:00.600 --> 01:39:03.236
so you're not alone
in feeling this way.

01:39:03.269 --> 01:39:05.505
So we go through life,
we reach a certain age,

01:39:05.538 --> 01:39:09.176 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we begin to
understand that someday,

01:39:09.209 --> 01:39:12.412 align:start size:88% position:13%
ourselves, and everyone
we love will die.

01:39:12.445 --> 01:39:15.749
And that's powerful,
painful knowledge.

01:39:15.782 --> 01:39:17.517
And I think from that moment,

01:39:17.550 --> 01:39:19.319
we have to start developing

01:39:19.352 --> 01:39:22.089 align:start size:78% position:22%
defense mechanisms
to handle that,

01:39:22.122 --> 01:39:24.324 align:start size:81% position:19%
and to integrate
that into our lives.

01:39:24.357 --> 01:39:26.226
- So what are those
defense mechanisms?

01:39:26.259 --> 01:39:30.630
- I think that the more obvious
ones would be having a child

01:39:30.663 --> 01:39:32.766
writing a book,
making a TV show,

01:39:32.799 --> 01:39:35.035
creating a legacy of some kind,

01:39:35.068 --> 01:39:39.373 align:start position:0%
but there's also a more
insidious version, which is war,

01:39:39.406 --> 01:39:42.175 align:start size:84% position:16%
taking other
countries, being rich

01:39:42.208 --> 01:39:44.277 align:start size:88% position:13%
and being okay with
other people being poor.

01:39:44.310 --> 01:39:46.613
I think those are all
signs of death denial,

01:39:46.646 --> 01:39:48.348
they're all saying, but I'm okay

01:39:48.381 --> 01:39:50.584 align:start size:91% position:9%
because I have this money,
or I have this power,

01:39:50.617 --> 01:39:55.355
or I have these kind of dark
impulses that allow me to say,

01:39:55.388 --> 01:39:58.358
at least I can outrun
death in that way.

01:39:58.391 --> 01:40:01.695 align:start size:97% position:3%
And of course, that's not
true, no one can outrun death.

01:40:01.728 --> 01:40:03.663 align:start size:91% position:9%
But you can trick yourself
into believing that.

01:40:03.696 --> 01:40:06.566
- So how would you characterize

01:40:06.599 --> 01:40:09.102 align:start size:84% position:16%
the western
relationship to death?

01:40:09.135 --> 01:40:12.305
- Take America 150 years ago,

01:40:12.338 --> 01:40:15.442 align:start size:84% position:16%
if you were my
husband, and you died,

01:40:15.475 --> 01:40:17.477
I would be entirely
in charge of you,

01:40:17.510 --> 01:40:20.147
I would wash your body,
I would get the neighbor

01:40:20.180 --> 01:40:22.215
to make a wooden coffin for you,

01:40:22.248 --> 01:40:24.317
we would put you in the
coffin and carry you

01:40:24.350 --> 01:40:26.453
on our shoulders to the grave

01:40:26.486 --> 01:40:28.155
which someone had
dug themselves.

01:40:28.188 --> 01:40:31.691 align:start size:97% position:3%
It would have been an entirely
self sufficient process.

01:40:31.724 --> 01:40:34.761 align:start size:94% position:6%
But what happened around
the turn of the 20th century

01:40:34.794 --> 01:40:37.597
is really three big
things in my mind.

01:40:37.630 --> 01:40:39.533
One, you had the
rise of hospitals,

01:40:39.566 --> 01:40:41.768 align:start size:84% position:16%
so people were no
longer dying at home,

01:40:41.801 --> 01:40:43.570
you had the rise
of funeral homes,

01:40:43.603 --> 01:40:47.307 align:start size:91% position:9%
which means that we are
now outsourcing our death.

01:40:47.340 --> 01:40:49.409
Third one is slaughterhouses,

01:40:49.442 --> 01:40:52.345
so all of a sudden,
all food production,

01:40:52.378 --> 01:40:55.582 align:start size:91% position:9%
and the killing of animals
is also hidden as well.

01:40:55.615 --> 01:40:58.085 align:start size:78% position:22%
And we live in our
suburban houses

01:40:58.118 --> 01:40:59.753 align:start size:84% position:16%
where all those
things are outsourced.

01:40:59.786 --> 01:41:01.555 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it's just these
little layers and layers

01:41:01.588 --> 01:41:04.458 align:start size:81% position:19%
and layers of
denial around death.

01:41:04.491 --> 01:41:08.628
- But what does it
mean to accept death?

01:41:08.661 --> 01:41:12.165
- I don't think that you
ever truly accept death.

01:41:12.198 --> 01:41:16.436 align:start position:0%
But I believe that the
movement toward accepting death

01:41:16.469 --> 01:41:18.839 align:start size:81% position:19%
involves really
true self awareness

01:41:18.872 --> 01:41:22.609 align:start size:91% position:9%
about where you're hiding
your fears of death.

01:41:22.642 --> 01:41:26.113
That's where real awareness
and acceptance can come from.

01:41:26.146 --> 01:41:29.216 align:start size:97% position:3%
- For me, the thing that's
just such a bummer about death

01:41:29.249 --> 01:41:30.550
is that I just I'm done.

01:41:30.583 --> 01:41:32.886 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't get to continue
learning things

01:41:32.919 --> 01:41:34.454
and seeing what happens,

01:41:34.487 --> 01:41:38.225 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I'm just not part
of Earth anymore.

01:41:38.258 --> 01:41:41.228 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Isn't death kind of what
gives you that passion

01:41:41.261 --> 01:41:42.429
when you think about it?

01:41:42.462 --> 01:41:44.764
When you think about like, I
love learning, I love ideas.

01:41:44.797 --> 01:41:46.633
If you didn't have an endpoint,

01:41:46.666 --> 01:41:48.869
Are you gonna come in here
today with all these cameras

01:41:48.902 --> 01:41:51.138
and do the huge
amount of legwork

01:41:51.171 --> 01:41:53.440
that creating a show requires?

01:41:53.473 --> 01:41:54.274
- No.

01:41:54.307 --> 01:41:55.408
- No, right?

01:41:55.442 --> 01:41:56.843 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because you're like, I don't
know, maybe I'll do it,

01:41:56.876 --> 01:41:59.179
200 years from now,
whereas right now

01:41:59.212 --> 01:42:01.214 align:start size:94% position:6%
you're taking in
information left and right,

01:42:01.247 --> 01:42:02.549
because you wanna
produce content,

01:42:02.582 --> 01:42:04.784
you wanna produce
exciting things

01:42:04.817 --> 01:42:06.286
and share with other people.

01:42:06.319 --> 01:42:07.454
- Because this is my
one chance to do that.

01:42:07.487 --> 01:42:10.724
- This is your one chance,
the passion and the realness

01:42:10.757 --> 01:42:13.460
to life comes from it ending.

01:42:13.493 --> 01:42:15.729
That's the great gift
that death gives us.

01:42:15.762 --> 01:42:17.631 align:start size:84% position:16%
- What's an unhealthy
relationship

01:42:17.664 --> 01:42:19.633
to have to your own mortality?

01:42:19.666 --> 01:42:21.368
- The pursuit of immortality,

01:42:21.401 --> 01:42:23.637
and the pursuit of,
"I will stay alive

01:42:23.670 --> 01:42:26.840
"until I can upload my
brain into the cloud."

01:42:26.873 --> 01:42:28.942
That worries me, the
idea that everyone

01:42:28.975 --> 01:42:31.244
is just allowed to live forever,

01:42:31.277 --> 01:42:34.814
from here on out, is not
environmentally sensible.

01:42:34.847 --> 01:42:38.652 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's just not a sensible
position to take.

01:42:38.685 --> 01:42:39.486
(upbeat music_

01:42:42.922 --> 01:42:45.759 align:start size:91% position:9%
- [Narrator] We are seeing
the dawn of a new era,

01:42:45.792 --> 01:42:49.362 align:start size:88% position:13%
of possibilities
unfold on planet Earth.

01:42:49.395 --> 01:42:53.366
What more amazing will
be like in say 80, 100

01:42:53.399 --> 01:42:55.735
or even 200 years from now?

01:42:55.768 --> 01:42:58.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
Wouldn't you like the
possibility of finding out?

01:42:58.338 --> 01:42:59.806
(upbeat music)

01:42:59.839 --> 01:43:02.309
- To understand why some
people feel like death

01:43:02.342 --> 01:43:05.345 align:start size:88% position:13%
shouldn't be inevitable.
I've come to Alcor,

01:43:05.378 --> 01:43:07.914
one of the world's leading
life extension facilities.

01:43:07.947 --> 01:43:08.748
Linda.

01:43:08.781 --> 01:43:10.517
- Hi, how are you?

01:43:10.550 --> 01:43:11.685
- Great to meet you.

01:43:11.718 --> 01:43:13.286 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Nice to meet you too,
welcome down Alcor.

01:43:13.319 --> 01:43:14.387 line:0%
- Thank you for having me here.

01:43:14.420 --> 01:43:15.855 line:0%
I'm meeting Linda Chamberlain,

01:43:15.888 --> 01:43:18.959 line:0%
who co founded Alcor
nearly 46 years ago.

01:43:18.992 --> 01:43:21.528
So this facility that
we are in right now

01:43:21.561 --> 01:43:26.567 align:start position:0%
is where you both cryo
preserve people and store them.

01:43:27.433 --> 01:43:29.803
- Yes, we have 160 patients.

01:43:29.836 --> 01:43:30.637
- Wow.

01:43:30.670 --> 01:43:34.574
- And we have 1190

01:43:34.607 --> 01:43:37.711
something members, it changes.

01:43:37.744 --> 01:43:39.613
- A member is someone
who is alive today.

01:43:39.646 --> 01:43:42.816
- Alive today, they've made
the arrangements for this.

01:43:42.849 --> 01:43:46.620 align:start size:97% position:3%
Once they are cryo preserved,
they become patients.

01:43:46.653 --> 01:43:48.321
- You're using the word patient.

01:43:48.354 --> 01:43:49.589
- Yes.

01:43:49.622 --> 01:43:51.258
- Okay, tell me about
why you use that word.

01:43:51.291 --> 01:43:53.260
- For us, death is not something

01:43:53.293 --> 01:43:55.262
which is like an on off switch.

01:43:55.295 --> 01:43:58.665 align:start size:94% position:6%
One second, you're alive
the next second you're dead.

01:43:58.698 --> 01:44:01.801
What we are trying
to do is to slow down

01:44:01.834 --> 01:44:04.671
and stop the dying process.

01:44:04.704 --> 01:44:06.506
- To become a patient at Alcor.

01:44:06.539 --> 01:44:10.710 align:start size:88% position:13%
First you have to pay
between 80 and $200,000,

01:44:10.743 --> 01:44:13.947
then you have to die
or more specifically

01:44:13.980 --> 01:44:16.283
be pronounced clinically dead.

01:44:16.316 --> 01:44:18.485 align:start size:91% position:9%
This generally means
that your heart and lungs

01:44:18.518 --> 01:44:19.453
have stopped functioning.

01:44:19.486 --> 01:44:22.589
At that point, Alcor
can begin their work.

01:44:22.622 --> 01:44:24.591
- Now there are two
ways that a person

01:44:24.624 --> 01:44:25.959
could sign up for
this procedure.

01:44:25.992 --> 01:44:29.763
There's a whole body
patient or as a neuro

01:44:29.796 --> 01:44:31.598 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Oh, and does neuro
just mean head?

01:44:31.631 --> 01:44:33.867
- It means yes, the
cephalon actually,

01:44:33.900 --> 01:44:37.604
which is all the structures
down to about the clavicle.

01:44:37.637 --> 01:44:40.974 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm a neuro, everybody
in my family,

01:44:41.007 --> 01:44:43.576
who's now in staces is a neuro.

01:44:43.609 --> 01:44:45.979
Most of the people
who really understand

01:44:46.012 --> 01:44:49.316
the technology are neuros.

01:44:49.349 --> 01:44:52.319
The primary reason that
people choose whole body

01:44:52.352 --> 01:44:53.520
is the emotional.

01:44:53.553 --> 01:44:54.754
- Of course.

01:44:54.788 --> 01:44:58.358
- And they're not comfortable
with the idea of their body

01:44:58.391 --> 01:45:00.827
being removed and discarded.

01:45:00.860 --> 01:45:04.964
So let's say that our
patient, is whole body,

01:45:04.997 --> 01:45:07.667 align:start size:84% position:16%
the moment the
patient is pronounced,

01:45:07.700 --> 01:45:09.002
they go into an ice bath.

01:45:09.035 --> 01:45:11.037
And this is just crushed ice,

01:45:11.070 --> 01:45:13.506
and its water in there as well.

01:45:13.539 --> 01:45:16.676
Their heart is started again,
with a mechanical thumper,

01:45:16.709 --> 01:45:19.879
they're intubated and their
lungs are functioning again

01:45:19.912 --> 01:45:23.850 align:start size:94% position:6%
being ventilated circulating
the cooler temperature.

01:45:23.883 --> 01:45:25.585
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:45:25.618 --> 01:45:27.020
So you need the veins, the
arteries, the vasculature,

01:45:27.053 --> 01:45:28.822
the heart, you need all of those

01:45:28.855 --> 01:45:31.991 align:start size:78% position:22%
continuing to pump
and circulate.

01:45:32.024 --> 01:45:33.927
- This is our operating room.

01:45:35.027 --> 01:45:36.696
So basically,

01:45:36.729 --> 01:45:39.766
when the patient comes in
through the door there,

01:45:39.799 --> 01:45:43.970 align:start size:97% position:3%
they'll go into this specially
developed operating table,

01:45:44.003 --> 01:45:49.009 align:start size:97% position:3%
it is going to be circulating
nitrogen gas over them

01:45:49.876 --> 01:45:52.379
to help cool them externally.

01:45:52.412 --> 01:45:54.614
And if its whole body patient,

01:45:54.647 --> 01:45:57.517
then the surgeons open the chest

01:45:57.550 --> 01:45:59.719
and then we begin circulating

01:45:59.752 --> 01:46:01.488
the organ transplants solution.

01:46:02.555 --> 01:46:03.656
- Once in the operating room,

01:46:03.689 --> 01:46:05.091
the patient's blood is replaced

01:46:05.124 --> 01:46:07.627
with cooled organ
transplant fluid

01:46:07.660 --> 01:46:09.696 align:start size:84% position:16%
and circulated through
the vascular system

01:46:09.729 --> 01:46:11.598
to rapidly cool
down the internal

01:46:11.631 --> 01:46:13.733 align:start size:91% position:9%
and external
temperatures of the body.

01:46:13.766 --> 01:46:15.769 align:start size:91% position:9%
Now, just before the water
within the body tissue

01:46:15.802 --> 01:46:17.604
reaches its freezing point.

01:46:17.637 --> 01:46:20.407 align:start size:84% position:16%
Cryo protective
fluids are introduced.

01:46:20.440 --> 01:46:22.909
These act like anti freeze
preventing the formation

01:46:22.942 --> 01:46:26.012 align:start size:91% position:9%
of ice crystals that
could damage soft tissue.

01:46:26.045 --> 01:46:28.882 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is called the
vitrification process.

01:46:30.049 --> 01:46:32.719
- Now let's say that
it is a neuro patient.

01:46:32.752 --> 01:46:34.187
So they come in first here--

01:46:34.220 --> 01:46:35.388
- Yep their whole body.

01:46:35.421 --> 01:46:38.691
- Right the surgeons will do
the neuro separation first.

01:46:38.724 --> 01:46:39.626
- Okay, yeah, that makes sense.

01:46:39.659 --> 01:46:41.127
- To separate the cephalon,

01:46:41.160 --> 01:46:44.764 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is all of the structures
down to about the clavicle,

01:46:44.797 --> 01:46:47.868
bring it over here to

01:46:48.768 --> 01:46:50.871
this operating field.

01:46:52.238 --> 01:46:55.041
Wash the blood out
and we introduce

01:46:55.074 --> 01:46:57.143
the organ transplant solution.

01:46:57.176 --> 01:47:00.513
- I'm imagining a
person's cephalon

01:47:00.546 --> 01:47:02.048
essentially their head in here,

01:47:02.081 --> 01:47:04.651 align:start size:88% position:13%
I can see how it's
going to get clamped in.

01:47:05.651 --> 01:47:08.755
That looks like I'm sure

01:47:08.788 --> 01:47:11.825
a crazy sci fi movie,
but it really happens.

01:47:11.858 --> 01:47:13.726
- It really happens.

01:47:13.759 --> 01:47:15.829 align:start size:81% position:19%
- [Michael] After
the verification
process is complete.

01:47:15.862 --> 01:47:17.997
The patients are
placed inside bags

01:47:18.030 --> 01:47:20.200 align:start size:81% position:19%
that are attached
to open metal cases,

01:47:20.233 --> 01:47:23.002 align:start size:88% position:13%
which are then placed
inside cylindrical tanks

01:47:23.035 --> 01:47:26.072 align:start size:88% position:13%
filled with liquid
nitrogen called dewars.

01:47:26.105 --> 01:47:28.007
- So this is our
patient care bay.

01:47:28.040 --> 01:47:30.710
We have 159 patients

01:47:30.743 --> 01:47:31.911
- In these tanks right here.

01:47:31.944 --> 01:47:32.712
- In these tanks.

01:47:34.280 --> 01:47:37.884 align:start size:88% position:13%
There are approximately
nine patients

01:47:37.917 --> 01:47:41.988
and each one of these four
whole bodies and five neuros.

01:47:43.289 --> 01:47:46.192
This one right here

01:47:46.225 --> 01:47:48.161
is where my husband

01:47:49.529 --> 01:47:50.730
is currently housed.
- This one right here.

01:47:50.763 --> 01:47:53.500
- Right, this is where
Fred is at the moment.

01:47:53.533 --> 01:47:56.570
My mother and my
father in law are

01:47:57.570 --> 01:47:58.205
in this one.

01:47:59.939 --> 01:48:03.543
- Wow, it's so weird
because I am right now,

01:48:04.577 --> 01:48:06.746 align:start size:81% position:19%
not in a graveyard.
- No.

01:48:06.779 --> 01:48:10.116
Alcor is very much
like an ambulance

01:48:10.149 --> 01:48:13.820
taking their loved ones to a
hospital, not down the street.

01:48:13.853 --> 01:48:15.555
that a hospital in the future,

01:48:16.255 --> 01:48:18.825
when technology can help them.

01:48:18.858 --> 01:48:20.593
- They're not being transported

01:48:20.626 --> 01:48:22.662
through space but through time.

01:48:23.663 --> 01:48:24.731
(upbeat music)

01:48:24.764 --> 01:48:27.033
To see what drives this
time traveling ambulance,

01:48:27.066 --> 01:48:29.669
I'm going to sit
down with Max More,

01:48:29.702 --> 01:48:33.673 align:start size:84% position:16%
Alcor's CEO and a
future neuro patient.

01:48:33.706 --> 01:48:36.776
So Max, what's the status
of the technology needed

01:48:36.809 --> 01:48:39.946 align:start size:81% position:19%
to revive cryo
preserve specimens?

01:48:39.979 --> 01:48:40.947
Are we getting closer?

01:48:40.980 --> 01:48:42.582
- We are getting closer,

01:48:42.615 --> 01:48:44.250 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
it's gonna be decades at
least before we can bring back

01:48:44.283 --> 01:48:46.052 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
human beings,
whole human beings.

01:48:46.085 --> 01:48:49.722 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
But we already cryo
preserved eggs, sperm,

01:48:49.755 --> 01:48:51.791
we cryo preserved skin
corneas, heart valves,

01:48:51.824 --> 01:48:52.992
all kinds of things.

01:48:53.025 --> 01:48:55.828
So these are single tissues and
we can reverse that process.

01:48:55.861 --> 01:48:57.630
Now you move from
that to an organ,

01:48:57.663 --> 01:48:59.098
things get more difficult.

01:48:59.131 --> 01:49:02.135 align:start size:91% position:9%
But we actually did an
experiment a few years ago

01:49:02.168 --> 01:49:04.938
took this little tiny worm
we used certain chemicals,

01:49:04.971 --> 01:49:06.139
So it would learn that,

01:49:06.172 --> 01:49:07.807
oh my foods over here
and not over here.

01:49:07.840 --> 01:49:10.677
And we cryo preserved them,
and then we just waited,

01:49:10.710 --> 01:49:12.011
brought them back and
then we tested them,

01:49:12.044 --> 01:49:13.246
we were able to demonstrate

01:49:13.279 --> 01:49:15.582 align:start size:78% position:22%
that the memory
test that the ones

01:49:15.615 --> 01:49:17.784 align:start size:97% position:3%
that had received the
training retained that memory.

01:49:17.817 --> 01:49:19.652
So it was the first
time in an organism

01:49:19.685 --> 01:49:21.287 align:start size:81% position:19%
we've proven
survive with memory.

01:49:21.320 --> 01:49:23.890 align:start size:91% position:9%
So now we're asking, okay,
what's the next step?

01:49:23.923 --> 01:49:26.092 align:start position:0%
Because whole organisms are
difficult to reverse right now,

01:49:26.125 --> 01:49:28.661 align:start size:91% position:9%
but step by step, the
more progress we can make,

01:49:28.694 --> 01:49:29.963
the more convincing this is.

01:49:29.996 --> 01:49:32.665
- Now, when it comes
to extending life,

01:49:32.698 --> 01:49:35.835
some questions come up,
like should people die?

01:49:36.969 --> 01:49:40.173
I know we don't like
the idea of death, but

01:49:40.206 --> 01:49:41.341
do we lose something?

01:49:41.374 --> 01:49:42.275 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I know what
you're getting at--

01:49:42.308 --> 01:49:43.710
- By getting rid of death.

01:49:43.743 --> 01:49:45.078
- Yeah, I think we
will lose something

01:49:45.111 --> 01:49:47.714 align:start size:97% position:3%
like we lost something
when we got rid of slavery or
smallpox,

01:49:47.747 --> 01:49:51.818 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I think people,
people find themselves

01:49:51.851 --> 01:49:53.720
in knots to rationalize death.

01:49:53.753 --> 01:49:54.954
I believe that.

01:49:54.987 --> 01:49:56.689
Right now we're kind of
in this tragic situation

01:49:56.722 --> 01:49:59.158 align:start size:91% position:9%
where over time, hopefully
you kind of learn

01:49:59.191 --> 01:50:00.360
your wisdom grows over time,

01:50:00.393 --> 01:50:01.461
but the same time your cognitive

01:50:01.494 --> 01:50:04.998 align:start position:0%
and physical health is
declining, that's really sucks.

01:50:05.031 --> 01:50:06.666
That's a bad situation.

01:50:06.699 --> 01:50:07.900 align:start size:94% position:6%
What if they both could
keep going up indefinitely,

01:50:07.933 --> 01:50:09.869
so you could live
hundreds of years

01:50:09.902 --> 01:50:12.672
or longer and get smarter and
more knowledgeable and wiser,

01:50:12.705 --> 01:50:15.174
and hopefully more mature,
and have more foresight,

01:50:15.207 --> 01:50:16.342
because you got a much
longer planning horizon.

01:50:16.375 --> 01:50:20.146
What we'll have is a world
of like ultra mature people,

01:50:20.179 --> 01:50:21.781 align:start size:91% position:9%
which I think will
actually be a better world

01:50:21.814 --> 01:50:22.882
than the one we have today.

01:50:22.915 --> 01:50:24.317
And if they say well,

01:50:24.350 --> 01:50:25.918
and it just comes up
all the time, they say,

01:50:25.951 --> 01:50:27.854
well, death is what
gives life meaning.

01:50:27.887 --> 01:50:29.355
Bullshit.

01:50:29.388 --> 01:50:32.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
If that was true, then would
they also advocate people

01:50:32.692 --> 01:50:34.994
who live to 90 should
be killed off at 45,

01:50:35.027 --> 01:50:36.396
will that double the
meaning of their life?

01:50:36.429 --> 01:50:38.798
In fact, I think life
gets more meaning,

01:50:38.831 --> 01:50:40.767
the longer you live,
because you can build

01:50:40.800 --> 01:50:41.801
on what you've done before.

01:50:41.834 --> 01:50:43.836
So if anything it increases

01:50:43.869 --> 01:50:45.705
the meaningfulness
of life, my view.

01:50:45.738 --> 01:50:47.940 align:start size:91% position:9%
- You're making me
realize that in many ways,

01:50:47.973 --> 01:50:48.775
I

01:50:49.742 --> 01:50:52.712
am rationalizing death.

01:50:52.745 --> 01:50:56.082
I'm looking for ways to
excuse it and accept it.

01:50:56.115 --> 01:50:59.318
I don't think it's unhealthy
to accept that you are mortal.

01:50:59.351 --> 01:51:00.820
- Well, I have to accept it

01:51:00.853 --> 01:51:02.355
because I could get
killed at any time.

01:51:02.388 --> 01:51:05.825
One thing I'm have to stress
because every article written

01:51:05.858 --> 01:51:08.761
they always have to use the
word forever, or immortality.

01:51:08.794 --> 01:51:09.862
And that's not on
the table here.

01:51:09.895 --> 01:51:12.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're just offering a
chance people to be revived

01:51:12.398 --> 01:51:14.200
in the time when
we've beaten aging.

01:51:14.233 --> 01:51:15.702 align:start size:91% position:9%
But eventually
something's gonna get you

01:51:15.735 --> 01:51:17.203 align:start size:84% position:16%
so we're not
offering immortality,

01:51:17.236 --> 01:51:20.339 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're offering an unknown
extension of human lifespan.

01:51:20.372 --> 01:51:23.309
(upbeat music)

01:51:23.342 --> 01:51:26.012 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- Okay, so, Elisabeth,
welcome back.

01:51:26.045 --> 01:51:26.846 line:0%
- Thanks.

01:51:26.879 --> 01:51:27.881 line:0%
- Pretty heavy stuff.

01:51:30.916 --> 01:51:33.252
You have a connection to Alcor.

01:51:33.285 --> 01:51:38.291
In fact, you were the one
who got us that interview.

01:51:39.158 --> 01:51:40.359
- Yeah, I set up the visit.

01:51:40.392 --> 01:51:43.396 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So how did you come
into contact with Alcor?

01:51:44.764 --> 01:51:47.167
- Well, I grew up in near San
Francisco in the Bay Area.

01:51:48.834 --> 01:51:52.105
So I think Alcor has always
been in my consciousness,

01:51:53.305 --> 01:51:54.741
and my awareness
since I was a kid.

01:51:54.774 --> 01:51:57.176
I was familiar with
the philosophy of it.

01:51:57.209 --> 01:52:01.814 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I probably have a dozen
friends or so who are members.

01:52:01.847 --> 01:52:03.116
- I think you even are friends

01:52:03.149 --> 01:52:06.953
with the couple on the
brochure about Alcor.

01:52:06.986 --> 01:52:07.954 align:start size:78% position:22%
- That's right.
- That people get.

01:52:07.987 --> 01:52:09.822
- And Max is a friend as well.

01:52:09.855 --> 01:52:11.257 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Right.
- Yeah, the CEO.

01:52:11.290 --> 01:52:14.393 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So I guess the next
question is, are you a member?

01:52:14.426 --> 01:52:17.830
- I'm not a member yet, but
I intend to be at some point.

01:52:17.863 --> 01:52:19.866
- So what's keeping you
from doing it the cost?

01:52:19.899 --> 01:52:21.267
- It's just the cost right now,

01:52:21.300 --> 01:52:24.036
Yeah, that's it, and also
the fact that honestly,

01:52:24.069 --> 01:52:25.270
if I were to die right now,

01:52:25.304 --> 01:52:29.242 align:start size:81% position:19%
the chance of
being cryogenically
frozen is pretty low

01:52:29.275 --> 01:52:31.778 align:start size:88% position:13%
because the way I would
die would probably

01:52:31.811 --> 01:52:35.782
be in a motorbike accident in
India or something like that.

01:52:35.815 --> 01:52:37.817
So it'd be hard to
get this cephalon

01:52:37.850 --> 01:52:39.418
back to the Alcor lab in time.

01:52:39.451 --> 01:52:41.320
- Yep, the best.

01:52:41.353 --> 01:52:43.956
It's weird to talk about
this the best way to die,

01:52:43.989 --> 01:52:48.795
to be cryogenically
frozen is to die,

01:52:48.828 --> 01:52:52.064
right there near their facility.

01:52:52.097 --> 01:52:54.200
I think they've said that
they have a lot of members

01:52:54.233 --> 01:52:59.239
who near the end of their life
move to the Scottsdale area,

01:53:00.005 --> 01:53:01.207
so that when they die,

01:53:01.240 --> 01:53:03.876 align:start size:88% position:13%
they can be taken
right away or actually,

01:53:03.909 --> 01:53:07.413 align:start size:81% position:19%
an Alcor team can
be at your deathbed.

01:53:08.547 --> 01:53:10.583
Now, the best way to be frozen.

01:53:10.616 --> 01:53:12.519
And this is kind of
what makes me feel

01:53:13.886 --> 01:53:15.221
like we have a long way to go,

01:53:15.254 --> 01:53:18.457 align:start size:88% position:13%
is that the best way
for me to be cryo frozen

01:53:18.490 --> 01:53:20.092
and then brought back
later is for someone

01:53:20.125 --> 01:53:23.096
to literally kill me right now.

01:53:24.530 --> 01:53:27.200
Stick me in the ice bath
right now start pumping in

01:53:27.233 --> 01:53:30.537 align:start size:97% position:3%
the fluid to cool my body down
and kill me by cooling me.

01:53:31.503 --> 01:53:33.072
If I die in any other way,

01:53:33.105 --> 01:53:34.941
there will be too
much cell death

01:53:34.974 --> 01:53:36.509
or at least there will be some,

01:53:36.542 --> 01:53:38.511
because for a split second,

01:53:38.544 --> 01:53:43.015 align:start size:88% position:13%
while the law decides
whether I'm dead or not,

01:53:43.048 --> 01:53:44.517
my cells are already dying.

01:53:44.550 --> 01:53:45.852
- Right yeah.

01:53:45.885 --> 01:53:47.854 align:start size:81% position:19%
And also, I suppose
by that logic,

01:53:47.887 --> 01:53:49.055
you would also wanna do it

01:53:49.088 --> 01:53:50.423
as early in your
life as possible,

01:53:50.456 --> 01:53:52.491 align:start size:88% position:13%
when your brain is in
the best possible state.

01:53:52.524 --> 01:53:55.294 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Exactly, I wonder if
they've ever had a member say,

01:53:55.327 --> 01:53:58.631
all right, I have
cognitive decline.

01:53:58.664 --> 01:54:02.268 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it's happening to
the tissues in my brain.

01:54:02.301 --> 01:54:05.605
If you preserve me when I
finally am pronounced dead

01:54:05.638 --> 01:54:09.542
by an authority, what
brain will I have left?

01:54:09.575 --> 01:54:10.944
That's what I'm stuck with.

01:54:12.077 --> 01:54:14.313
They may believe
that in the future,

01:54:14.346 --> 01:54:17.016
we can just reconstruct
an entire consciousness

01:54:17.049 --> 01:54:18.484
by just knowing a little bit

01:54:18.517 --> 01:54:21.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
about some of the brain
connections and structures,

01:54:21.287 --> 01:54:23.189
but I guess all of
this brings us to

01:54:23.222 --> 01:54:24.523
what Caitlin was saying,

01:54:24.556 --> 01:54:26.626 align:start size:94% position:6%
she really was uncomfortable
with that idea.

01:54:28.060 --> 01:54:31.397 align:start size:78% position:22%
That it seems what
were her words?

01:54:31.430 --> 01:54:32.498
Non sensible.

01:54:33.465 --> 01:54:34.567
- Well, I think living forever

01:54:34.600 --> 01:54:37.136 align:start size:81% position:19%
immortality is
pretty nonsensical.

01:54:37.169 --> 01:54:39.505
And even Alcor will
say and even Max says

01:54:39.538 --> 01:54:44.544 align:start size:91% position:9%
that what they're striving
for is not immortality.

01:54:45.711 --> 01:54:46.880 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's just extending
the life that we have.

01:54:47.847 --> 01:54:49.615 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there's many ways
that we do that already.

01:54:49.648 --> 01:54:53.052
We have antibiotics
and vaccinations and
things like that.

01:54:53.085 --> 01:54:55.621
- Right, just wearing a seatbelt

01:54:55.654 --> 01:54:58.024
is a life extension protocol.

01:54:59.458 --> 01:55:01.494
What Alcor does,

01:55:01.527 --> 01:55:04.497
seems much more sci fi.

01:55:04.530 --> 01:55:07.934
And, I also understand
a lot of the criticisms

01:55:07.967 --> 01:55:10.169
around the cost,

01:55:10.202 --> 01:55:12.672
the cost filters out

01:55:12.705 --> 01:55:15.608
only a certain kind of
person to be preserved.

01:55:18.444 --> 01:55:21.580 align:start size:94% position:6%
If we can bring back frozen
people in the future,

01:55:21.613 --> 01:55:25.952
those from now in history
will predominantly be high.

01:55:25.985 --> 01:55:28.621 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm a Silicon Valley
millionaire or billionaire.

01:55:28.654 --> 01:55:31.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm back and now it's
the year 8000, and I'm back.

01:55:31.323 --> 01:55:33.559
- Right and certain
personality traits--

01:55:33.592 --> 01:55:34.727
- [Michael] Only
personality traits?

01:55:34.760 --> 01:55:36.329
- Yeah.

01:55:36.362 --> 01:55:40.299 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, so is that possibly
not gonna represent

01:55:40.332 --> 01:55:42.034
our arrow well in the future,

01:55:42.067 --> 01:55:46.706 align:start size:91% position:9%
if only those with the
means to extend their life

01:55:46.739 --> 01:55:48.975
in that way, do so.

01:55:49.008 --> 01:55:50.576 align:start size:91% position:9%
- And the other thing
that goes along with that

01:55:50.609 --> 01:55:53.412 align:start size:84% position:16%
is I think that people
who have abundance

01:55:53.445 --> 01:55:55.514 align:start size:91% position:9%
are also more likely to
want to extend their life,

01:55:55.547 --> 01:55:57.149
not just because they can,

01:55:57.182 --> 01:55:59.318
but because they're
living a good life.

01:55:59.351 --> 01:56:01.620
And so Alcor is not, I
mean at least for me,

01:56:01.653 --> 01:56:02.989
it's not about denying death,

01:56:03.022 --> 01:56:05.324
it's not because I'm
terrified of dying.

01:56:05.357 --> 01:56:07.526 align:start size:81% position:19%
It's because I
actually love life.

01:56:07.559 --> 01:56:09.695
I enjoy every moment of it.

01:56:09.728 --> 01:56:12.598
And the only tragedy is,
like you said that I won't,

01:56:12.631 --> 01:56:14.633
at some point, I won't
be able to keep learning,

01:56:14.666 --> 01:56:16.168
I won't be able to keep growing,

01:56:16.201 --> 01:56:19.105
I won't be able to keep
discovering new things.

01:56:19.138 --> 01:56:23.242
So people who enjoy life who
have the fortune of being able

01:56:23.275 --> 01:56:26.045 align:start size:94% position:6%
to do all of that naturally
want that to continue.

01:56:28.447 --> 01:56:33.453 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Now I get some of the
reactions people have like,

01:56:34.586 --> 01:56:36.622 align:start size:91% position:9%
well, but it's not
environmentally sensible.

01:56:36.655 --> 01:56:39.759 align:start size:91% position:9%
And we only have a finite
amount of resources,

01:56:39.792 --> 01:56:44.063
I get all of that, I hope that
that ceases to be a problem.

01:56:44.096 --> 01:56:47.533
I think more human
lives is just better.

01:56:47.566 --> 01:56:50.169 align:start size:84% position:16%
And to think any other
way is ridiculous.

01:56:51.603 --> 01:56:56.342
We need to make sure we can
sustain all of those lives

01:56:57.209 --> 01:56:58.378
and give people good lives--

01:56:59.545 --> 01:57:01.714 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And that could take
us about overpopulation.

01:57:01.747 --> 01:57:03.382
- Is what I'm saying, yeah.

01:57:03.415 --> 01:57:07.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
I feel like any negative side
effects of overpopulation

01:57:07.419 --> 01:57:09.588
could be fixed by the time
we're also able to literally

01:57:09.621 --> 01:57:11.490
bring back a frozen brain.

01:57:11.523 --> 01:57:13.626
Okay, we should be
working on both.

01:57:15.160 --> 01:57:16.729 align:start size:91% position:9%
But I'm basically throwing
that off the table,

01:57:16.762 --> 01:57:18.464
because I don't really care.

01:57:18.497 --> 01:57:19.698
I think it's not going to
be an issue in the future,

01:57:19.731 --> 01:57:22.101 align:start size:78% position:22%
so long as we keep
focusing on it,

01:57:22.134 --> 01:57:23.436
problems of overpopulation.

01:57:23.469 --> 01:57:25.304
Well, we need humans to die,

01:57:25.337 --> 01:57:27.273 align:start size:94% position:6%
or else we're not gonna have
enough beef for everyone,

01:57:27.306 --> 01:57:29.375 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's like, all
right, let's fix that.

01:57:29.408 --> 01:57:30.810
But assuming that that's fixed,

01:57:31.677 --> 01:57:33.079
I still feel like there's

01:57:35.247 --> 01:57:36.582
a kind of hubris
that turns me off

01:57:36.615 --> 01:57:39.719 align:start size:88% position:13%
to the idea of extending
your life like this.

01:57:41.186 --> 01:57:45.058
Why do you think that you
get to live longer in this?

01:57:46.525 --> 01:57:50.129 align:start position:0%
I hate to say artificial because
seat belts are artificial,

01:57:50.162 --> 01:57:52.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
they're technology we
invented to make us not die

01:57:52.631 --> 01:57:54.233
so often and quickly.

01:57:54.266 --> 01:57:56.268 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, yeah, I get what
you're saying I mean,

01:57:56.301 --> 01:57:58.804 align:start size:97% position:3%
it does feel kind of self
indulgent, to be able to say,

01:57:58.837 --> 01:58:00.506
Here I am, I'm so special,

01:58:00.539 --> 01:58:03.342
I get to be one of the few
that survived in the future.

01:58:03.375 --> 01:58:05.578 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I guess my counter
argument to that is whenever,

01:58:05.611 --> 01:58:07.513
with any new technology,

01:58:07.546 --> 01:58:11.584
it's always initially only
limited to a subset of people

01:58:11.617 --> 01:58:15.554 align:start size:81% position:19%
who are risk takers,
early adopters,

01:58:15.587 --> 01:58:17.456 align:start size:84% position:16%
who have the financial
means to do it,

01:58:17.489 --> 01:58:18.791
who have the sort of foresight

01:58:18.824 --> 01:58:22.428
or sort of long term perspective

01:58:22.461 --> 01:58:24.463
to wanna do that
in the first place.

01:58:24.496 --> 01:58:27.466
And just because that's a
small set of the population

01:58:27.499 --> 01:58:29.235
doesn't mean that those
people shouldn't do it.

01:58:29.268 --> 01:58:30.736
In fact, on the contrary,

01:58:30.769 --> 01:58:33.673 align:start position:0%
that's the first step to making
it accessible to everyone.

01:58:35.507 --> 01:58:37.409
- I brought up a point to Max

01:58:37.442 --> 01:58:39.612
that I think is in
the bonus footage,

01:58:39.645 --> 01:58:43.516 align:start size:97% position:3%
but didn't make it into the
episode about social progress.

01:58:43.549 --> 01:58:46.452
And how if we wind
up with five, six,

01:58:46.485 --> 01:58:48.588 align:start size:81% position:19%
700 year old, super
mature people,

01:58:49.821 --> 01:58:53.826 align:start size:97% position:3%
what if their ideas about
the way society is organized,

01:58:53.859 --> 01:58:58.164
doesn't change, and we
wind up getting stuck,

01:58:58.197 --> 01:59:00.299
not making progress,

01:59:00.332 --> 01:59:02.568
because the population
wants to keep living

01:59:02.601 --> 01:59:05.604
the way they lived in their 20s,

01:59:05.637 --> 01:59:08.808
which, in 600 years
might not be the way.

01:59:10.676 --> 01:59:12.411
Which I don't think
any system right now

01:59:12.444 --> 01:59:13.646
is the best system.

01:59:13.679 --> 01:59:16.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
But if we start having
people not die out,

01:59:16.415 --> 01:59:19.351
does that mean that we
stopped making progress?

01:59:19.384 --> 01:59:20.686
It's a weird argument.

01:59:20.719 --> 01:59:21.887
Max then points out.

01:59:21.920 --> 01:59:23.589 align:start size:81% position:19%
So what are you
recommending we do,

01:59:23.622 --> 01:59:25.491
genocide people
when they're old?

01:59:25.524 --> 01:59:26.926
I'm like, no, not that.

01:59:28.260 --> 01:59:28.827
What do you think?

01:59:28.860 --> 01:59:29.728
Am I making sense here?

01:59:29.761 --> 01:59:31.230
- Yeah, definitely.

01:59:31.263 --> 01:59:33.265
I mean, I think that the brain

01:59:33.298 --> 01:59:35.834
does tend to become
sort of ossified.

01:59:35.867 --> 01:59:39.271 align:start size:94% position:6%
As we get older, people tend
to be more conservative,

01:59:39.304 --> 01:59:42.708 align:start size:91% position:9%
more traditional, be
more set in the routines.

01:59:42.741 --> 01:59:45.911 align:start size:91% position:9%
But I think by the time
we have the technology to,

01:59:45.944 --> 01:59:49.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
and cryonically free
somebody and revive somebody

01:59:49.681 --> 01:59:54.253 align:start size:97% position:3%
we'll also have age
technologies to make the brain

01:59:54.286 --> 01:59:56.555
more flexible again and reverse

01:59:56.588 --> 01:59:58.291
the aging process in the brain.

01:59:59.424 --> 02:00:03.662 align:start size:88% position:13%
So it might not actually
be an issue anymore.

02:00:03.695 --> 02:00:06.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the other counter
arguments I have to that is,

02:00:06.398 --> 02:00:08.601
okay so maybe that is true.

02:00:08.634 --> 02:00:11.470
But let's see how it plays out.

02:00:11.503 --> 02:00:13.639
We're still gonna be
having children, right.

02:00:13.672 --> 02:00:16.542
And so there are still
gonna be fresh ideas,

02:00:16.575 --> 02:00:18.611
new generations, the
generational dynamic

02:00:18.644 --> 02:00:20.446
is gonna be very different.

02:00:20.479 --> 02:00:23.582
Because it won't just be
boomers versus millennials.

02:00:23.615 --> 02:00:25.284
(laughing)

02:00:25.317 --> 02:00:27.453
- Yeah, there will be like
four or five generations

02:00:27.486 --> 02:00:28.954
around to be battling it out.

02:00:28.987 --> 02:00:31.590
There will be 20,

02:00:31.623 --> 02:00:32.691
30.

02:00:32.724 --> 02:00:36.629
And man, we will learn so much.

02:00:36.662 --> 02:00:41.300
I think a lot of that fear
you have as you get older

02:00:41.333 --> 02:00:45.738 align:start position:0%
about things changing is an
evolutionarily designed process

02:00:45.771 --> 02:00:48.607
of like, Look, I lived
long enough to reproduce,

02:00:48.640 --> 02:00:51.343
therefore, the way I was
raised must be good enough.

02:00:51.376 --> 02:00:53.912 align:start size:97% position:3%
anything different could risk
that not happening again.

02:00:53.945 --> 02:00:56.615 align:start size:94% position:6%
So of course natural
selection will choose people

02:00:56.648 --> 02:00:59.485
who have developed a mind
that doesn't like things

02:00:59.518 --> 02:01:01.754
to change, it doesn't want
the kids be too different,

02:01:01.787 --> 02:01:04.990
but imagine living
to be 600 years old,

02:01:05.023 --> 02:01:08.460
and you see so many
generations of kids

02:01:08.493 --> 02:01:09.795 align:start size:81% position:19%
that like different
kinds of music

02:01:09.828 --> 02:01:11.397
and do different
kinds of hobbies

02:01:11.430 --> 02:01:12.832
and things and they're all okay.

02:01:14.099 --> 02:01:15.901 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah, think about
how much wisdom

02:01:15.934 --> 02:01:18.404
you'd have after 600 years.

02:01:18.437 --> 02:01:20.706
- I think what we should do
now is go back to the episode

02:01:20.739 --> 02:01:22.541 align:start size:84% position:16%
and look at the
decision that I make.

02:01:23.642 --> 02:01:26.445
(upbeat music)

02:01:29.748 --> 02:01:32.318 align:start size:88% position:13%
Do I think that
someday we will be able

02:01:32.351 --> 02:01:37.356
to cryonically freeze an entire
person and then revive them?

02:01:38.623 --> 02:01:39.825
Yes, I do.

02:01:39.858 --> 02:01:41.994
I believe that cryo preservation

02:01:42.027 --> 02:01:45.964 align:start position:0%
will change the meaning of
death, and lead to breakthroughs

02:01:45.997 --> 02:01:49.368 align:start size:97% position:3%
and medical technology that
will improve all of our lives.

02:01:50.035 --> 02:01:52.037
But do I

02:01:52.070 --> 02:01:54.606
want to extend my
life indefinitely?

02:01:54.639 --> 02:01:59.011 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well, on the one hand,
obviously, death is a bummer.

02:01:59.044 --> 02:02:02.414
But on the other, the
universe managed fine

02:02:02.447 --> 02:02:05.718 align:start size:78% position:22%
without me for
billions of years.

02:02:05.751 --> 02:02:07.786
Am I really so important

02:02:07.819 --> 02:02:10.689
that it should never
not have me again?

02:02:10.722 --> 02:02:14.393
Should I be around
as long as possible?

02:02:14.426 --> 02:02:17.963 align:start size:97% position:3%
Or do those who will come
later deserve their own world?

02:02:19.531 --> 02:02:22.401
Should I try to extend my life?

02:02:23.435 --> 02:02:26.472
Or should I decide to die

02:02:26.505 --> 02:02:29.742
when my time comes and
return all this matter

02:02:29.775 --> 02:02:31.377
I'm borrowing back to the world?

02:02:32.477 --> 02:02:34.079 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well I don't think
there's a right answer.

02:02:34.112 --> 02:02:37.616 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's a personal choice
we each get to make

02:02:37.649 --> 02:02:39.518
and should be able to make.

02:02:39.551 --> 02:02:41.721 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I've been thinking
about it a lot.

02:02:42.654 --> 02:02:45.424
(upbeat music)

02:02:46.858 --> 02:02:49.528 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I'm going to speak again
with my friend Caitlin

02:02:49.561 --> 02:02:52.965 align:start size:91% position:9%
the mortician to confront
my own mortality.

02:02:52.998 --> 02:02:55.467
Well, Caitlin, Thanks for
meeting with me again.

02:02:55.500 --> 02:02:58.170 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've been surrounded by
death lately, spoke to you.

02:02:58.203 --> 02:03:00.406
I visited Alcor and,

02:03:01.773 --> 02:03:04.109 align:start position:0%
if we never invented
technology to bring people back

02:03:04.142 --> 02:03:06.612 align:start size:78% position:22%
then the Alcor
patients are dead.

02:03:06.645 --> 02:03:09.181
But they have that hope.

02:03:09.214 --> 02:03:11.984
I worked on Terror
Management Theory.

02:03:12.017 --> 02:03:15.587
And I even had a loved one
pass away just two weeks ago,

02:03:15.620 --> 02:03:16.588
my grandmother.

02:03:16.621 --> 02:03:18.757
- I'm sorry to hear that.

02:03:18.790 --> 02:03:21.693
- She was cremated,
as well as my father.

02:03:21.726 --> 02:03:23.429
And I realized,

02:03:24.796 --> 02:03:26.532
I've never made a clear decision

02:03:26.565 --> 02:03:27.933
about what should happen to me.

02:03:30.235 --> 02:03:32.905 align:start position:0%
Because I just figured I'll
figure that out when I'm older,

02:03:32.938 --> 02:03:34.473
but I could die at any time.

02:03:34.506 --> 02:03:35.741
- You sure could.

02:03:35.774 --> 02:03:38.644
- So I wanna be prepared, and
I want my wishes to be known.

02:03:39.778 --> 02:03:43.115
So I've decided, when
that moment comes,

02:03:43.148 --> 02:03:46.952 align:start size:88% position:13%
I want it to be my final
moment of existence,

02:03:46.985 --> 02:03:49.221
I wanna give all my
atoms and molecules

02:03:49.254 --> 02:03:53.792 align:start size:97% position:3%
back to the universe, and I've
decided that I want to die.

02:03:53.825 --> 02:03:55.661
- I'm so glad you've
made that decision.

02:03:55.694 --> 02:03:56.929
And you've come to
the right place.

02:03:56.962 --> 02:03:59.665
- I want to be

02:03:59.698 --> 02:04:02.568 align:start size:91% position:9%
naturally buried, I wanna
have a green burial,

02:04:02.601 --> 02:04:04.970
become worm food and plant food

02:04:05.003 --> 02:04:07.673 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I want it all
to go back to Earth.

02:04:07.706 --> 02:04:12.711 align:start position:0%
But I kind of want a place where
people can come to be like,

02:04:13.578 --> 02:04:14.746
that's where he was buried.

02:04:14.779 --> 02:04:19.618
- So there's everything from
just little discs in the ground

02:04:20.986 --> 02:04:24.490
where you are, to GPS
that locates you to
natural cemeteries

02:04:24.523 --> 02:04:26.692 align:start size:91% position:9%
that are trying to
reintroduce native plants.

02:04:26.725 --> 02:04:27.860
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

02:04:27.893 --> 02:04:29.728 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So you can have
your own Joshua Tree.

02:04:29.761 --> 02:04:32.998
So the first thing I'm gonna
give you to give a look over

02:04:33.031 --> 02:04:35.501
is what's called an
advanced directive.

02:04:35.534 --> 02:04:38.003
And everybody needs
to have one of these.

02:04:38.036 --> 02:04:42.007 align:start size:91% position:9%
And why it's so important
is that it's you,

02:04:42.040 --> 02:04:45.577
not only designating someone
to be in charge of your body

02:04:45.610 --> 02:04:47.913 align:start size:81% position:19%
as you're dying,
right after you die,

02:04:47.946 --> 02:04:50.682
and then with however you
decide to dispose of it,

02:04:50.715 --> 02:04:52.651
but also who that person is.

02:04:52.684 --> 02:04:55.087 align:start size:91% position:9%
- So this isn't just about
burial, this is dying.

02:04:55.120 --> 02:04:57.723 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Oh, no, it's about
death dying death

02:04:57.756 --> 02:04:59.958
and after death.
- Interesting.

02:04:59.991 --> 02:05:03.161 align:start size:88% position:13%
A choice like this is
extremely new to humans.

02:05:03.194 --> 02:05:05.163
It used to be that
your only options

02:05:05.196 --> 02:05:10.102
upon death were cremation,
embalming or rotting away.

02:05:10.135 --> 02:05:12.804 align:start size:88% position:13%
But today, you can
choose to pause yourself

02:05:12.837 --> 02:05:14.940
at death's door until the door

02:05:14.973 --> 02:05:16.675
has been moved somewhere else.

02:05:17.576 --> 02:05:18.978
But I've decided not to do that.

02:05:20.111 --> 02:05:21.914 align:start size:81% position:19%
So I'm ready to
make this official.

02:05:21.947 --> 02:05:23.916 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Fire in the hole.
- Okay.

02:05:23.949 --> 02:05:26.752
(upbeat music)

02:05:27.752 --> 02:05:29.988
- Whoa, all right.
- How do you feel?

02:05:30.021 --> 02:05:32.224 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Weirdly, I feel
very relaxed and good.

02:05:32.257 --> 02:05:33.959
It was kind of life changing,

02:05:33.992 --> 02:05:36.061 align:start size:84% position:16%
but what it really was
was death changing.

02:05:36.094 --> 02:05:37.262
- Huh, well, thank you,

02:05:37.295 --> 02:05:39.197 align:start size:81% position:19%
and I'm glad you've
decided to die.

02:05:39.230 --> 02:05:40.065
- Thank you.

02:05:40.965 --> 02:05:43.335 align:start size:88% position:13%
Jeff and Sheldon, thank
you for showing me

02:05:43.368 --> 02:05:45.637
the power of death's influence.

02:05:45.670 --> 02:05:48.674
Caitlin, thank you for
helping me accept it.

02:05:48.707 --> 02:05:50.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
Max, thank you for
the work you are doing

02:05:50.709 --> 02:05:52.744
and the opportunities you
are offering humanity.

02:05:52.777 --> 02:05:55.280
And all of you out there,

02:05:55.313 --> 02:05:57.683
as always, thanks for watching.

02:05:58.883 --> 02:06:01.186 line:0%
All right, Elisabeth,

02:06:01.219 --> 02:06:02.054 line:0%
I decided

02:06:03.054 --> 02:06:04.056 line:0%
and still believe

02:06:05.256 --> 02:06:06.091 line:0%
I should die.

02:06:07.826 --> 02:06:09.127
- You're still happy
with that decision?

02:06:09.160 --> 02:06:10.796
- I'm still happy
with that decision.

02:06:10.829 --> 02:06:13.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm really happy for you
to make whatever decision

02:06:14.032 --> 02:06:15.834
you want about yourself,

02:06:17.769 --> 02:06:18.971
you're you, you own you.

02:06:19.004 --> 02:06:22.040
So I don't care what
people decide to do.

02:06:22.073 --> 02:06:25.644
I just I don't know,

02:06:25.677 --> 02:06:29.781 align:start size:84% position:16%
the main part was that
I just feel like I

02:06:29.814 --> 02:06:31.850
want one turn

02:06:31.883 --> 02:06:32.718
at this game.

02:06:33.918 --> 02:06:36.054
And I don't wanna
be around forever,

02:06:36.087 --> 02:06:38.123
being like, well, I'm
older than you, so.

02:06:39.290 --> 02:06:42.260
But I also don't feel
like I can criticize

02:06:42.293 --> 02:06:44.630 align:start size:84% position:16%
other people's
decisions about this.

02:06:48.066 --> 02:06:49.134
I don't know, what do you think?

02:06:49.167 --> 02:06:50.369
- I think it's interesting.

02:06:50.402 --> 02:06:52.204
When people talk
about immortality,

02:06:52.237 --> 02:06:53.839
they always think
of it in terms of

02:06:53.872 --> 02:06:56.074 align:start size:91% position:9%
there's some sort of
costs that comes with it.

02:06:56.107 --> 02:06:57.876
Like, there's no way that
you could have something

02:06:57.909 --> 02:06:59.645
so wonderful as life extension

02:06:59.678 --> 02:07:01.313
without some sort of drawback.

02:07:01.346 --> 02:07:05.150 align:start size:94% position:6%
And all this sort of
fantasy and sci fi about it

02:07:05.183 --> 02:07:06.718
even stories about vampires.

02:07:06.751 --> 02:07:07.919
There's always like,

02:07:07.952 --> 02:07:11.256 align:start size:88% position:13%
it comes with some sort
of terrible burden.

02:07:11.289 --> 02:07:12.691
And

02:07:12.724 --> 02:07:14.292
why?

02:07:14.325 --> 02:07:17.162
That's just something that
we culturally have created,

02:07:17.195 --> 02:07:19.031
doesn't necessarily
have to be the case.

02:07:19.064 --> 02:07:21.734
And I

02:07:23.268 --> 02:07:25.370
I'm perfectly inclined to think

02:07:25.403 --> 02:07:28.006
that we can extend our
lives it probably be,

02:07:28.039 --> 02:07:29.808
won't be like an all
of a sudden we find

02:07:29.841 --> 02:07:32.044 align:start size:97% position:3%
some sort of magic drink that
we're gonna live forever.

02:07:32.077 --> 02:07:34.680
Instead, it's gonna be a
very incremental process

02:07:34.713 --> 02:07:38.150
of first it's gonna be
normal to live 100 years

02:07:38.183 --> 02:07:41.420
and then 110 years, and 120
years and hundred like that.

02:07:42.787 --> 02:07:46.191 align:start size:91% position:9%
And, yeah, probably
without some kind of crazy

02:07:46.224 --> 02:07:48.026
cause for not gonna
be some abomination

02:07:48.059 --> 02:07:50.896
or freak of nature or
something like that.

02:07:50.929 --> 02:07:53.865
- Yeah, I think it
is really important

02:07:53.898 --> 02:07:57.135 align:start size:78% position:22%
the points that
you've made about,

02:07:57.168 --> 02:07:59.971 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're not talking about a
drink that makes you immortal,

02:08:00.004 --> 02:08:03.842
like a vampire in
that sense of whoo,

02:08:03.875 --> 02:08:07.879 align:start size:97% position:3%
but to strive for immortality
is something against God.

02:08:07.912 --> 02:08:10.816 align:start size:78% position:22%
talking more about
extending life,

02:08:10.849 --> 02:08:12.050
you could still get hit by a bus

02:08:12.083 --> 02:08:14.086 align:start size:78% position:22%
at any time and
literally be dead.

02:08:15.220 --> 02:08:17.389
Your brains connections
are completely lost,

02:08:17.422 --> 02:08:18.957
and we can never recreate them.

02:08:20.091 --> 02:08:22.227
That's why I think that
what Alcor is doing,

02:08:22.260 --> 02:08:24.730
to help us understand
how to preserve tissues

02:08:24.763 --> 02:08:26.865 align:start size:84% position:16%
and extend people's
lives is so important.

02:08:28.466 --> 02:08:32.871
If a drink was invented,
that allowed one to stay

02:08:32.904 --> 02:08:36.208 align:start size:88% position:13%
exactly as healthy
mentally, and physically

02:08:36.241 --> 02:08:39.878
as they were that moment they
drank it, I wouldn't drink it.

02:08:41.946 --> 02:08:42.781
Because again,

02:08:44.983 --> 02:08:48.754
I want my song to
have a final note.

02:08:49.554 --> 02:08:50.956
I don't like the end being

02:08:50.989 --> 02:08:53.792 align:start size:81% position:19%
kind of like punted
down the road.

02:08:53.825 --> 02:08:55.794
- I wonder if people would
have said the same thing

02:08:55.827 --> 02:08:58.263
back, before antibiotics,

02:08:58.296 --> 02:08:59.765 align:start size:81% position:19%
if they were looking
at the horizon,

02:08:59.798 --> 02:09:03.101
and somebody described
to them, antibiotics

02:09:03.134 --> 02:09:05.403
and this could extend
your life by 40 years

02:09:05.436 --> 02:09:06.805
and this ear infection you have

02:09:06.838 --> 02:09:09.174 align:start size:88% position:13%
doesn't have to cripple
you and kill you.

02:09:09.207 --> 02:09:10.809
- Right, right.

02:09:10.842 --> 02:09:12.844 align:start position:0%
Well, I mean, but I would
still say the same thing today.

02:09:12.877 --> 02:09:15.847 align:start size:97% position:3%
If someone said, okay, you're
gonna die like in a week,

02:09:15.880 --> 02:09:18.517 align:start size:97% position:3%
unless you take this
medicine, I'll be like, sure.

02:09:18.550 --> 02:09:20.519
I don't know if I'm
just feeling like

02:09:20.552 --> 02:09:24.156 align:start size:78% position:22%
there is like a
right time to die.

02:09:24.189 --> 02:09:27.126
Like, the way it's sort of been.

02:09:28.526 --> 02:09:32.898 align:start size:91% position:9%
When it's time your family
can move on and you've

02:09:34.532 --> 02:09:37.803 align:start size:97% position:3%
led the way and now it's time
for them to lead the next

02:09:37.836 --> 02:09:39.571
and everyone gets a
turn as the leader.

02:09:39.604 --> 02:09:42.841
- Yeah, I mean, I'm one
of my goals right now.

02:09:42.874 --> 02:09:45.210
And I guess another reason
that I haven't done Alcor yet

02:09:45.243 --> 02:09:48.814
is I think there's other
ways of extending life

02:09:48.847 --> 02:09:50.549
that are a little
more accessible.

02:09:50.582 --> 02:09:52.517
I mean, Alcor is
a pretty big risk,

02:09:52.550 --> 02:09:56.555
the chances of actually being
revived are extremely low,

02:09:56.588 --> 02:09:58.256
but it's sort of
a Pascal's Wager,

02:09:58.289 --> 02:09:59.958
it's like, what's
the alternative?

02:09:59.991 --> 02:10:01.159
- Yeah, what do I lose?

02:10:01.192 --> 02:10:02.360
I may as well.

02:10:02.393 --> 02:10:03.829
- Well, one of the
things I'm doing now,

02:10:03.862 --> 02:10:04.797
actually, next week,

02:10:04.830 --> 02:10:07.199
is getting my stem cells
cryonically preserved.

02:10:08.066 --> 02:10:09.835
So yeah, I'm going to a company

02:10:09.868 --> 02:10:11.870
called Forever Labs
in San Francisco.

02:10:13.238 --> 02:10:14.406 align:start size:91% position:9%
They actually think
they're based in Michigan,

02:10:14.439 --> 02:10:16.875 align:start size:84% position:16%
but they have a clinic
in San Francisco,

02:10:16.908 --> 02:10:18.376 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're gonna extract
the stem cells,

02:10:18.409 --> 02:10:21.112
and then bank them for
the rest of my life.

02:10:21.145 --> 02:10:23.882 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So you can use those
for therapies or?

02:10:23.915 --> 02:10:25.550
- Exactly, yeah, yeah.

02:10:25.583 --> 02:10:29.555
So say, I have an organ down
the line that needs repair.

02:10:30.555 --> 02:10:33.391
Now I have stem cells
from my younger self,

02:10:33.424 --> 02:10:35.560
that we could use, or
maybe they'll develop

02:10:35.593 --> 02:10:38.563 align:start size:91% position:9%
age reversal technologies
down the line.

02:10:38.596 --> 02:10:41.433 align:start size:81% position:19%
And then I could use
those stem cells,

02:10:41.466 --> 02:10:43.268 align:start size:81% position:19%
they've already done
that in rodents.

02:10:44.569 --> 02:10:48.540 align:start size:97% position:3%
And done some very basic,
clinical studies with humans,

02:10:48.573 --> 02:10:52.644 align:start size:97% position:3%
where they've given human stem
cells from younger donors.

02:10:52.677 --> 02:10:55.447
And actually, older
adults are able to be

02:10:55.480 --> 02:10:59.618 align:start position:0%
they show more physical fitness,
more cognitive sharpness,

02:10:59.651 --> 02:11:03.221
after the stem cell injection.

02:11:03.254 --> 02:11:06.591
But also, there's, with
that particular study,

02:11:06.624 --> 02:11:08.493
when they receive too
much of the stem cells,

02:11:08.526 --> 02:11:11.129 align:start size:84% position:16%
they actually have
negative side effects.

02:11:11.162 --> 02:11:12.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
But that's probably
because their immune system

02:11:12.997 --> 02:11:15.533 align:start size:88% position:13%
is reacting to somebody
else's stem cells,

02:11:15.566 --> 02:11:18.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is why there's a
benefit in banking your own.

02:11:18.169 --> 02:11:19.671 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Wow, banking your
own stem cells.

02:11:19.704 --> 02:11:22.440 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that sounds brilliant,
I would love to do it--

02:11:22.473 --> 02:11:24.476 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And they do with
kids, with babies now.

02:11:24.509 --> 02:11:25.577
- Take the baby stem cells,

02:11:25.610 --> 02:11:28.246
and you've got like the
freshest best stem cells.

02:11:28.279 --> 02:11:30.048 align:start size:91% position:9%
And then when you're super
old, and you're like,

02:11:30.081 --> 02:11:31.449 align:start size:78% position:22%
Oh, my liver could
use some help.

02:11:31.482 --> 02:11:32.450 align:start size:84% position:16%
They'll be like,
when you were a baby,

02:11:32.483 --> 02:11:35.320
we took these things out
and they've been frozen.

02:11:35.353 --> 02:11:37.155
So they're still fresh.

02:11:37.188 --> 02:11:42.194 align:start position:0%
Well, it seems to me like I
should go, when I naturally go.

02:11:43.561 --> 02:11:46.031 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I think maybe you're
feeling a sense of fairness,

02:11:46.064 --> 02:11:49.467
or just what's socially,
the right thing to do.

02:11:49.500 --> 02:11:51.703
Because if everybody around you,

02:11:51.736 --> 02:11:53.605
if the technology were
available to everybody,

02:11:53.638 --> 02:11:57.442
and everybody you knew
was living to 100 or 120,

02:11:57.475 --> 02:12:00.312
I think it would be it
would feel more okay.

02:12:01.579 --> 02:12:04.249
But it feels sort
of unfair to say,

02:12:04.282 --> 02:12:08.586
Okay, I'm entitled to
this new technology,

02:12:08.619 --> 02:12:11.623
or I'm entitled to more
life and other people,

02:12:11.656 --> 02:12:13.425
the rest of the world isn't--

02:12:13.458 --> 02:12:14.626
- I'm so good.
- And I find that

02:12:14.659 --> 02:12:17.028
really admirable actually.
- The world needs me forever,

02:12:17.061 --> 02:12:19.998
not forever, but the
world just needs me.

02:12:20.031 --> 02:12:23.234
If I would left the world,
oh, that'd be such a tragedy.

02:12:23.267 --> 02:12:25.403
to the world, wow!

02:12:25.436 --> 02:12:26.538
- But it kind of feels like

02:12:26.571 --> 02:12:28.473 align:start size:78% position:22%
you're breaking
some sort of like,

02:12:29.640 --> 02:12:32.577
agreement that the whole
human race has accepted,

02:12:34.178 --> 02:12:36.181
like, I'm going
to defy something

02:12:37.482 --> 02:12:39.651
that we've all sort
of mutually agreed

02:12:39.684 --> 02:12:41.320
is the way things are.

02:12:42.587 --> 02:12:45.056
But if we all mutually
agree something else,

02:12:45.089 --> 02:12:48.026 align:start size:91% position:9%
because it becomes so
widespread or ubiquitous,

02:12:48.059 --> 02:12:49.427
then it doesn't feel like

02:12:49.460 --> 02:12:50.662
you've sort of transgressed

02:12:50.695 --> 02:12:54.399
or is nothing to
feel, weird about.

02:12:54.432 --> 02:12:56.701 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right, it's a really
interesting way to frame it.

02:12:56.734 --> 02:12:58.169
I wanna point out, though,

02:12:58.202 --> 02:13:00.672
that although Max
really emphasized

02:13:00.705 --> 02:13:03.108 align:start size:81% position:19%
that he's not
selling immortality,

02:13:03.141 --> 02:13:05.744
they're very much is a
belief in immortality

02:13:05.777 --> 02:13:08.413
that you'll hear
from their members,

02:13:08.446 --> 02:13:10.448 align:start size:81% position:19%
especially in a
sort of digital way

02:13:10.481 --> 02:13:15.053
where their consciousness can
be recreated in a computer.

02:13:15.086 --> 02:13:16.388 align:start size:78% position:22%
And at that point,
it doesn't seem

02:13:16.421 --> 02:13:18.156
like there's still
this inevitable,

02:13:18.189 --> 02:13:21.259 align:start size:88% position:13%
but you could get hit
by a bus, but you could,

02:13:21.292 --> 02:13:23.228
be in an accident, at
that point it's like,

02:13:23.261 --> 02:13:25.263
No, but I never will
be because I will exist

02:13:25.296 --> 02:13:27.365
and be replicated as
digital conscious.

02:13:27.398 --> 02:13:29.467
And that I don't wanna do,

02:13:29.500 --> 02:13:30.435 align:start size:81% position:19%
I don't care if
other people do it.

02:13:30.468 --> 02:13:32.437
I just feel like I'm not
gonna burden the world

02:13:32.470 --> 02:13:34.139
with more of me than it needs.

02:13:35.573 --> 02:13:37.575
- I have trouble wrapping
my mind around that one,

02:13:37.608 --> 02:13:41.046 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the area I wanna say yes,
I'll do it, like why not?

02:13:41.079 --> 02:13:42.614
I wanna try everything.

02:13:42.647 --> 02:13:44.682 align:start size:81% position:19%
But to me somehow
there's a disconnect

02:13:44.715 --> 02:13:46.251
where that doesn't
feel like me anymore.

02:13:46.284 --> 02:13:49.687 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maybe I'm so embodied, that
feels a little bit like

02:13:49.720 --> 02:13:53.525
he was describing people want
to preserve their entire body

02:13:53.558 --> 02:13:55.727
because it's hard to
conceive of yourself

02:13:55.760 --> 02:13:57.696 align:start size:84% position:16%
as just a head or
just a consciousness.

02:13:59.163 --> 02:14:03.334 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it's just hard to imagine,
if I can't experience

02:14:03.367 --> 02:14:05.637
if I don't have
a physical, like,

02:14:05.670 --> 02:14:07.739 align:start size:81% position:19%
visceral experience
of the world.

02:14:09.240 --> 02:14:12.677 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that constant feedback,
what is life even?

02:14:12.710 --> 02:14:14.679 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It's kind of
lonely in a weird way

02:14:14.712 --> 02:14:16.348 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Yeah.
- Right, lonely.

02:14:18.649 --> 02:14:20.218
I guess you could
have a robot body.

02:14:22.420 --> 02:14:24.089 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It's like, how do
you even have a thought

02:14:24.122 --> 02:14:26.458
really without this input?

02:14:26.491 --> 02:14:28.393
- Well, I think the idea is
that you would have inputs

02:14:28.426 --> 02:14:32.363 align:start size:97% position:3%
from some kind of like, Cyborg
body that they built you

02:14:32.396 --> 02:14:33.731
that they injected
your consciousness

02:14:33.764 --> 02:14:35.400
into through the wire right?

02:14:36.601 --> 02:14:38.670 align:start position:0%
But at that point, you were
talking about true immortality.

02:14:38.703 --> 02:14:41.739
Because you've come up with
a way to solve any accident

02:14:41.772 --> 02:14:44.409 align:start size:88% position:13%
that ever happens to
you, you will never die.

02:14:44.442 --> 02:14:46.811 align:start size:91% position:9%
And that's very different
than a bring me back

02:14:46.844 --> 02:14:50.682
when you have a cure for
this cognitive problem

02:14:50.715 --> 02:14:52.283
that I'm developing.

02:14:52.316 --> 02:14:53.785
Bring me back, cure it,
and then I get to live

02:14:53.818 --> 02:14:56.688
for another like, I don't know,
a couple of decades or what?

02:14:56.721 --> 02:14:57.889
I don't know.

02:14:57.922 --> 02:15:01.192 align:start size:97% position:3%
Wow, this is all fascinating,
and very important stuff.

02:15:01.225 --> 02:15:02.894 align:start size:94% position:6%
Lisbeth Thank you very much.
- Thanks Michael.

02:15:02.927 --> 02:15:04.529
- Thank you.

02:15:04.562 --> 02:15:06.397 align:start size:97% position:3%
And without further ado,
we're gonna keep bulldozing on

02:15:06.430 --> 02:15:10.401 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
through here we are
with your Brain on Tech,

02:15:10.434 --> 02:15:13.538 line:0%
not your brain made out of
tech, but your brain on tech.

02:15:14.438 --> 02:15:16.341 line:0%
(upbeat music)

02:15:16.374 --> 02:15:17.575
Oh, hello.

02:15:17.608 --> 02:15:20.612
technology isn't just
changing our lives.

02:15:20.645 --> 02:15:23.414
It's changing our brains.

02:15:23.447 --> 02:15:26.584 align:start size:88% position:13%
Not just how they think,
but how they look.

02:15:26.617 --> 02:15:29.821
It's been shown that playing
certain video games for hours

02:15:29.854 --> 02:15:32.257
can improve your
memory for details,

02:15:32.290 --> 02:15:35.693
your ability to navigate
space, in video games,

02:15:35.726 --> 02:15:40.298
and can make your brain well
certain parts of it bigger.

02:15:40.331 --> 02:15:43.201
But scientists want to know
if exploring digital worlds

02:15:43.234 --> 02:15:46.404 align:start size:97% position:3%
can change our brains in
ways that improve our ability

02:15:46.437 --> 02:15:48.573
to navigate the real world.

02:15:48.606 --> 02:15:53.311 align:start position:0%
To find out, we've built a giant
maze to test their theories

02:15:53.344 --> 02:15:57.182 align:start position:0%
for the first time ever,
outside the world of computers.

02:15:57.215 --> 02:15:57.849
Now my job.

02:15:59.417 --> 02:16:00.952
I'm the lab rat.

02:16:00.985 --> 02:16:03.722
(upbeat music)

02:16:19.570 --> 02:16:21.539
(vacuum cleaner humming)

02:16:21.572 --> 02:16:24.442
Our brains have been
profoundly transformed

02:16:24.475 --> 02:16:26.611
by our interactions
with technology.

02:16:26.644 --> 02:16:28.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
A lot of the information
that I used to have to store

02:16:28.779 --> 02:16:32.283
in my brain is now
stored in my phone,

02:16:32.316 --> 02:16:34.552
my contacts, my schedule.

02:16:34.585 --> 02:16:35.887
In many ways, I've delegated

02:16:35.920 --> 02:16:38.489 align:start size:81% position:19%
what used to be
done by this organ,

02:16:38.522 --> 02:16:41.793
to this new external organ.

02:16:41.826 --> 02:16:43.795
Doing that frees up
my brains resources

02:16:43.828 --> 02:16:44.996
for other things that matter,

02:16:45.029 --> 02:16:48.299 align:start size:88% position:13%
or that technology can't
quite do for us yet.

02:16:48.332 --> 02:16:52.270
So while we all don't have
implants in our brains, yet,

02:16:52.303 --> 02:16:55.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
technology has already
found a way into our heads,

02:16:55.640 --> 02:16:58.509 align:start size:91% position:9%
which is why you may
find it deeply disturbing

02:16:58.542 --> 02:17:01.413 align:start size:81% position:19%
to see me do
something like this.

02:17:10.388 --> 02:17:12.690
Studies show we can
improve our brains

02:17:12.723 --> 02:17:14.759
by having enriching experiences,

02:17:14.792 --> 02:17:17.629 align:start size:88% position:13%
like playing a new
challenging video game.

02:17:17.662 --> 02:17:20.665
To learn more about this, I
came to UC Irvine Stark lab

02:17:20.698 --> 02:17:23.901
to speak with experts in the
field of learning and memory.

02:17:23.934 --> 02:17:27.472 line:0%
So Dane and Craig, you guys
work on learning and memory,

02:17:27.505 --> 02:17:28.640 line:0%
What about them?

02:17:28.673 --> 02:17:31.542 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- So the lab is trying to
figure out how memory works,

02:17:31.575 --> 02:17:34.712 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
how it works in the brain,
and one brain structure

02:17:34.745 --> 02:17:36.414
and the temporal lobe

02:17:36.447 --> 02:17:39.884
that we know is important to
memory is the hippocampus.

02:17:39.917 --> 02:17:42.654 align:start size:78% position:22%
- So what does the
hippocampus do?

02:17:42.687 --> 02:17:44.455
- We know it has
a role in memory

02:17:44.488 --> 02:17:46.791 align:start size:81% position:19%
and really a certain
kind of memory.

02:17:46.824 --> 02:17:48.860
The hippocampus is really
involved when you need

02:17:48.893 --> 02:17:52.730 align:start size:84% position:16%
to rapidly form new
arbitrary associations

02:17:52.763 --> 02:17:55.500
in remembering what
you did yesterday,

02:17:55.533 --> 02:17:56.934
definitely needs
the hippocampus.

02:17:56.967 --> 02:17:59.604
Maybe we'll go to the store,
we park our car in the lot.

02:17:59.637 --> 02:18:01.072
And we need to be
able to remember,

02:18:01.105 --> 02:18:02.840
not just I parked
my car in the lot,

02:18:02.873 --> 02:18:06.811
I parked my car in this
exact spot in the lot.

02:18:06.844 --> 02:18:09.447 align:start size:91% position:9%
And those details, that's
what the hippocampus

02:18:09.480 --> 02:18:11.649
seems to really be
helping us out on.

02:18:11.682 --> 02:18:13.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And you keep looking
down at this piece

02:18:13.517 --> 02:18:16.321
of chewed bubblegum on the
book, is that a hippocampus?

02:18:16.354 --> 02:18:18.790 align:start size:94% position:6%
- [Dr. Dane] Yes, this
actually is my hippocampus.

02:18:18.823 --> 02:18:20.525
- Is this the whole thing?

02:18:20.558 --> 02:18:21.759
- That's it.

02:18:21.792 --> 02:18:22.694
- Oh, there's one
on the other side--

02:18:22.727 --> 02:18:23.628
- That looks just like this.

02:18:23.661 --> 02:18:24.629
- Yeah, mirror image of it.

02:18:24.662 --> 02:18:27.699
- [Michael] In 2015, Dr.
Stark and Dr. Clemenson

02:18:27.732 --> 02:18:31.002
conducted a study to show how
video games affect the brain.

02:18:31.035 --> 02:18:32.403
They gathered participants

02:18:32.436 --> 02:18:33.805
who normally didn't
play video games

02:18:33.838 --> 02:18:35.740
and split them up
into three groups,

02:18:35.773 --> 02:18:37.508 line:85%
a control group who didn't play

02:18:37.541 --> 02:18:39.110 line:85%
any video games for two weeks,

02:18:39.143 --> 02:18:40.712 line:83%
and active control
group who played

02:18:40.745 --> 02:18:42.747 align:start size:81% position:19% line:83%
two dimensional
games for two weeks,

02:18:42.780 --> 02:18:44.449 line:83%
and an experimental
group who played

02:18:44.482 --> 02:18:46.551 line:85%
3D games for two weeks.

02:18:46.584 --> 02:18:48.619 line:83%
Beforehand, they had
all the participants

02:18:48.652 --> 02:18:49.887 line:85%
perform two virtual tasks

02:18:49.920 --> 02:18:52.790
on computers to measure
their spatial memory.

02:18:52.823 --> 02:18:53.624
- As soon as they came back,

02:18:53.657 --> 02:18:55.560
we re administered
those two tasks.

02:18:55.593 --> 02:18:57.762
And what we found was that the
people who played the 3D game

02:18:57.795 --> 02:18:59.831
saw an improvement in
their test scores,

02:18:59.864 --> 02:19:03.701
whereas the control group and
active control group did not.

02:19:03.734 --> 02:19:05.636 align:start size:91% position:9%
We didn't do brain scans,
but we can speculate

02:19:05.669 --> 02:19:06.904
that there were changes

02:19:06.937 --> 02:19:08.573
to the experimental
groups hippocampus.

02:19:09.840 --> 02:19:13.111
- So what are we gonna
be doing to me here?

02:19:13.144 --> 02:19:14.879 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So we're gonna do everything
that we've done before

02:19:14.912 --> 02:19:17.815 align:start position:0%
in our past studies, except
we're gonna add two new things.

02:19:17.848 --> 02:19:20.485 align:start size:88% position:13%
The first is we're gonna
add some brain scans

02:19:20.518 --> 02:19:21.453
to see if we see a change

02:19:21.486 --> 02:19:23.421
in the structural side
of your hippocampus.

02:19:23.454 --> 02:19:24.655 align:start size:81% position:19%
I mean, we've never
actually looked

02:19:24.688 --> 02:19:25.589
at somebody's brain scans

02:19:25.623 --> 02:19:27.625 align:start size:84% position:16%
before and after they
play video games.

02:19:27.658 --> 02:19:29.060
And the second thing
we're actually gonna do

02:19:29.093 --> 02:19:31.729 align:start size:94% position:6%
is we're gonna put you
through a real world space.

02:19:31.762 --> 02:19:34.766
- You're gonna be
the rat in a maze.

02:19:34.799 --> 02:19:37.034 align:start size:91% position:9%
- [Michael] This is
truly untested territory.

02:19:37.067 --> 02:19:39.604 align:start size:88% position:13%
The effect of video
gaming on spatial memory

02:19:39.637 --> 02:19:41.739
has never been studied in
a physical environment.

02:19:41.772 --> 02:19:44.542
On a scale this big
and comprehensive,

02:19:44.575 --> 02:19:46.511 align:start size:88% position:13%
I will have to
navigate my way through

02:19:46.544 --> 02:19:49.581 align:start size:78% position:22%
a 3600 square foot
physical maze,

02:19:50.781 --> 02:19:53.050
will playing video games
improve my mental skills

02:19:53.083 --> 02:19:54.685
in the real world?

02:19:54.718 --> 02:19:58.123 align:start position:0%
If so, society will have a
whole new way to look at gaming.

02:19:59.723 --> 02:20:02.660 align:start size:97% position:3%
First, we had to get baseline
measurements of my brain.

02:20:02.693 --> 02:20:03.794
- Welcome to the MRI center,

02:20:03.828 --> 02:20:06.697 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're gonna be taking a
whole series of scans of you

02:20:06.730 --> 02:20:09.634
as the before scan to then
see what's gonna be happening

02:20:09.667 --> 02:20:11.803
to your brain as a function
of actually doing the gaming

02:20:11.836 --> 02:20:13.704 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Cool, what kind of
things are you looking for?

02:20:13.737 --> 02:20:16.140
- Changes in the size and
shape of your hippocampus

02:20:16.173 --> 02:20:19.644 align:start size:84% position:16%
and also changes
in the connectivity
between brain regions.

02:20:19.677 --> 02:20:22.079 align:start size:84% position:16%
- My brain was scanned
using diffusion MRI

02:20:22.112 --> 02:20:25.583
with a special emphasis on
my all important hippocampus.

02:20:26.717 --> 02:20:29.921
Diffusion MRIs, I'd
never had one before.

02:20:29.954 --> 02:20:31.222
I had a bunch for this episode.

02:20:31.255 --> 02:20:33.658 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
By the way, Daniel,
welcome back.

02:20:33.691 --> 02:20:35.026 line:0%
- I'm glad to be here.

02:20:35.059 --> 02:20:36.727 line:0%
- I'm glad you're here too,

02:20:36.760 --> 02:20:40.131 line:0%
what the heck is a diffusion
MRI, I had like five of them.

02:20:40.164 --> 02:20:43.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're different, they
would make my chest twitch.

02:20:43.701 --> 02:20:46.571 align:start position:0%
Yeah, interesting. I'm actually
not sure why that happened.

02:20:46.604 --> 02:20:48.606 align:start size:88% position:13%
And actually had my
first ever diffusion MRI

02:20:48.639 --> 02:20:50.041
done a few weeks ago.

02:20:50.074 --> 02:20:53.044
- Was it for a health
reason or for a study?

02:20:53.077 --> 02:20:56.547 align:start size:94% position:6%
- My lab mate wanted to
test his scanning protocol.

02:20:56.580 --> 02:20:58.783 align:start size:91% position:9%
And he actually just asked
if I could sit in there

02:20:58.816 --> 02:21:00.952
