02:29:54.618 --> 02:29:57.721 align:start size:81% position:19%
the bottle, the
crayon and the boot.

02:29:57.754 --> 02:29:58.522
Go.

02:29:58.555 --> 02:29:59.623
- Book.

02:29:59.656 --> 02:30:00.657
I think,

02:30:00.690 --> 02:30:02.059
that's the ball.

02:30:02.092 --> 02:30:05.329
(upbeat music)

02:30:05.362 --> 02:30:06.597
Got it.

02:30:06.630 --> 02:30:08.065
Yeah, bottle was that hard one.

02:30:08.098 --> 02:30:11.702 align:start size:88% position:13%
But now, I remember
which alley to go down.

02:30:11.735 --> 02:30:13.137
Perfect.

02:30:13.170 --> 02:30:14.005
Crayon

02:30:15.138 --> 02:30:17.374
Boot, don't want that.

02:30:17.407 --> 02:30:18.475
There it is.

02:30:18.508 --> 02:30:20.444
Okay, now I need the boot.

02:30:20.477 --> 02:30:22.413
Oh I just saw the boot.

02:30:22.446 --> 02:30:23.280
But how did I?

02:30:24.581 --> 02:30:25.482
Got it.

02:30:25.515 --> 02:30:26.517
I'm done, I'm coming back.

02:30:27.651 --> 02:30:28.652
Got them.

02:30:28.685 --> 02:30:29.920
- All right.

02:30:29.953 --> 02:30:31.722
- [Dr. Clemenson] So then the
next for the pump, the pillow,

02:30:31.755 --> 02:30:33.657
the basketball and the cat.

02:30:33.690 --> 02:30:34.792 align:start size:66% position:34%
- [Dr. Stark] All
right, excellent.

02:30:34.825 --> 02:30:37.094
- [Dr. Clemenson] Good job.

02:30:37.127 --> 02:30:38.362
- Okay, so that was really fun,

02:30:38.395 --> 02:30:40.464
but I can't be the only subject.

02:30:40.497 --> 02:30:42.466 align:start size:81% position:19%
This experiment
could use a control.

02:30:42.499 --> 02:30:44.635 align:start size:91% position:9%
How else will we know
that me enriching my life

02:30:44.668 --> 02:30:46.670
with daily video game playing

02:30:46.703 --> 02:30:48.705
really causes a change
in my spatial memory?

02:30:48.738 --> 02:30:49.740
Right?

02:30:49.773 --> 02:30:50.874
Well, luckily for that,

02:30:50.908 --> 02:30:54.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
we've got a nice matched
control, guys similar to me.

02:30:54.444 --> 02:30:55.646
Okay, one of them
has too much hair.

02:30:55.679 --> 02:30:57.381
But you guys look good.

02:30:57.414 --> 02:30:58.348
You ready?

02:30:58.381 --> 02:30:59.249
- [All] Ready.

02:30:59.282 --> 02:31:00.517 line:0%
- In this experiment,

02:31:00.550 --> 02:31:02.486 line:0%
it was important to
have a control group,

02:31:02.519 --> 02:31:04.688 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
my look alikes had to go
through the exact same tests

02:31:04.721 --> 02:31:07.624 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
that I did to establish
their individual baselines,

02:31:07.657 --> 02:31:09.493 line:0%
the difference would
be that they would play

02:31:09.526 --> 02:31:12.496 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
absolutely no video games
for the next 10 days,

02:31:12.529 --> 02:31:14.298 line:0%
then any change
in my performance

02:31:14.331 --> 02:31:17.367 line:0%
would be compared against
any changes in theirs.

02:31:17.400 --> 02:31:19.770
(upbeat music)

02:31:20.637 --> 02:31:23.107
Next, I began my gaming regimen,

02:31:23.140 --> 02:31:24.842
starting from an ideal baseline

02:31:24.875 --> 02:31:27.745
since I hadn't played
video games in years.

02:31:29.379 --> 02:31:31.682
Would 10 days of gaming
really make a difference?

02:31:31.715 --> 02:31:36.253
Now Daniel, on YouTube, I'm
known as a pro gamer, right?

02:31:37.621 --> 02:31:40.724
Every day, I'm like, Zelda!

02:31:40.757 --> 02:31:42.159
You know what I
mean, you get it.

02:31:42.192 --> 02:31:43.327
Any Who?

02:31:43.360 --> 02:31:44.761 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think a lot of people
might be wondering

02:31:44.794 --> 02:31:48.799
what video game was Michael
playing during this test?

02:31:48.832 --> 02:31:50.667
Well we couldn't tell
you during the episode

02:31:50.700 --> 02:31:54.171 align:start size:97% position:3%
because the makers of the
game didn't give us permission

02:31:54.204 --> 02:31:55.739 align:start size:91% position:9%
to ever mention or show
any footage from the game.

02:31:55.772 --> 02:31:58.308
So we had to replace my screen

02:31:58.341 --> 02:32:00.577
with like generic stock footage.

02:32:00.610 --> 02:32:02.212 align:start size:88% position:13%
I don't know outerspace
looking stuff,

02:32:02.245 --> 02:32:04.781
which was very annoying,
because a lot of commenters

02:32:04.814 --> 02:32:06.583
are like, that's not a game.

02:32:06.616 --> 02:32:09.520
Here's the secret,
I'll tell you now.

02:32:09.553 --> 02:32:10.421
I played

02:32:12.389 --> 02:32:13.457
League of Legends.

02:32:15.325 --> 02:32:16.493
(laughing)

02:32:16.526 --> 02:32:19.763
And Daniel, let me tell
you, I was terrible.

02:32:19.796 --> 02:32:23.167 align:start size:81% position:19%
And people made that
very clear to me.

02:32:23.200 --> 02:32:26.403 align:start size:88% position:13%
I've never been bullied
so much in my life,

02:32:26.436 --> 02:32:27.638
- By people online or?

02:32:27.671 --> 02:32:28.839
- By people online.

02:32:28.872 --> 02:32:30.774 align:start size:81% position:19%
Because I would
join a game, right?

02:32:30.807 --> 02:32:32.743 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I play and I'm
still kind of learning

02:32:32.776 --> 02:32:33.877
how the game works.

02:32:33.911 --> 02:32:38.348
And then when it was over,
people could like comment,

02:32:38.381 --> 02:32:40.584
and they'd be like, I hate you.

02:32:40.617 --> 02:32:44.354 align:start size:91% position:9%
And like you, I'm gonna
flag you for being so bad.

02:32:44.387 --> 02:32:45.555
- Yeah,

02:32:45.588 --> 02:32:48.392
I mean the point here wasn't
really good at the game so much

02:32:48.425 --> 02:32:50.627 align:start size:88% position:13%
as it was to explore
three dimensional space.

02:32:50.660 --> 02:32:53.597
- Exactly, I did not
get good at the game.

02:32:53.630 --> 02:32:58.602
But I did explore 3D spaces
I normally wasn't exploring.

02:32:58.635 --> 02:33:00.504
- Right, because the game
involves a lot of strategy.

02:33:00.537 --> 02:33:01.605
I'm assuming, I haven't played

02:33:01.638 --> 02:33:02.940
League of Legends
so I don't know.

02:33:02.973 --> 02:33:04.641
- Well, luckily,
I'm a pro gamer.

02:33:04.674 --> 02:33:05.508
So let me just tell you--

02:33:05.542 --> 02:33:07.945
- As established.
- As a pro gamer,

02:33:07.978 --> 02:33:10.681 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would probably say,
League of Legends

02:33:10.714 --> 02:33:15.385 align:start size:84% position:16%
is basically like
Mario meets checkers.

02:33:15.418 --> 02:33:16.052
And

02:33:18.521 --> 02:33:20.624
I didn't get better at the game.

02:33:20.657 --> 02:33:21.892
But by playing it every day,

02:33:21.925 --> 02:33:25.762
I really was investigating 3D
spaces in a new way every day.

02:33:25.795 --> 02:33:26.930
- Which is what matters,

02:33:26.963 --> 02:33:28.865 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Which is what
matters for this test.

02:33:28.898 --> 02:33:31.668 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Right, I mean, I guess,
if they were testing

02:33:31.701 --> 02:33:35.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
something like visual
working memory, for example,

02:33:35.672 --> 02:33:36.940 align:start size:94% position:6%
then maybe your performance
wouldn't matter.

02:33:36.973 --> 02:33:38.642
Because it's your ability to,

02:33:39.876 --> 02:33:42.012
hold various things in the
map in your head at once

02:33:42.045 --> 02:33:44.781
or your attention, for example,

02:33:44.814 --> 02:33:46.617
but all they really cared
about was your ability

02:33:46.650 --> 02:33:49.319
to learn a new space.

02:33:49.352 --> 02:33:50.487
- Exactly.

02:33:50.520 --> 02:33:52.022
So let's get back to the episode

02:33:52.055 --> 02:33:55.359 align:start size:81% position:19%
and explore some new
spaces ourselves.

02:33:55.392 --> 02:33:58.528 align:start size:94% position:6%
For many of you out there
because some of this footage

02:33:58.561 --> 02:34:00.964 align:start size:84% position:16%
is from my house
where I used to live.

02:34:00.997 --> 02:34:05.502
And you'll get to see my
kitties, corn and pickle.

02:34:05.535 --> 02:34:07.504
All right, here we go.

02:34:07.537 --> 02:34:10.741
(upbeat music)

02:34:10.774 --> 02:34:14.344 align:start position:0%
Technology isn't just affecting
the way we remember things.

02:34:14.377 --> 02:34:16.013
It's also playing
with the empathy

02:34:16.046 --> 02:34:17.915 align:start size:81% position:19%
and social circuits
of our brains.

02:34:19.316 --> 02:34:21.618
In fact, in many cases,
we are more comfortable

02:34:21.651 --> 02:34:24.588
relating to machines
than we are to people.

02:34:24.621 --> 02:34:26.923
Just think about how much
we care about our phones.

02:34:26.956 --> 02:34:29.593 align:start size:81% position:19%
roboticist, and
MIT Media Lab alum,

02:34:29.626 --> 02:34:31.995 align:start size:81% position:19%
Alex Reben invented
the blab droid,

02:34:32.028 --> 02:34:34.398 align:start size:84% position:16%
a miniature robot
equipped with a camera

02:34:34.431 --> 02:34:35.699
and an innocent little voice

02:34:35.732 --> 02:34:37.501 align:start size:78% position:22%
that asked very
personal questions

02:34:37.534 --> 02:34:39.569
of unsuspecting pedestrians.

02:34:39.602 --> 02:34:42.372
- [Robot] If you could
take back one mistake,

02:34:42.405 --> 02:34:43.774
what would it be?

02:34:43.807 --> 02:34:46.376
- Oh, gosh, I only
get to take back one?

02:34:46.409 --> 02:34:47.678 align:start size:78% position:22%
- [Michael] The
majority of people

02:34:47.711 --> 02:34:49.646
instantly shared
intimate details

02:34:49.679 --> 02:34:50.580
- [Robot] Tell me something

02:34:50.614 --> 02:34:53.350 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you've never told
a stranger before.

02:34:53.383 --> 02:34:55.385 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I'm scared I won't
be able to love

02:34:55.418 --> 02:34:58.722
and to let myself go
in a love relationship.

02:34:59.856 --> 02:35:01.525
- In many ways, we
are more comfortable

02:35:01.558 --> 02:35:04.061 align:start size:81% position:19%
talking to a machine
than a human.

02:35:05.395 --> 02:35:07.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
But what about talking
through a machine?

02:35:07.697 --> 02:35:10.734
I mean, it's often easier
to say difficult things

02:35:10.767 --> 02:35:13.670
to a person by a text instead
of in real life, isn't it?

02:35:13.703 --> 02:35:15.939 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, what if the
person on the other end

02:35:15.972 --> 02:35:20.944 align:start position:0%
wasn't a friend or a significant
other, but was a therapist?

02:35:20.977 --> 02:35:23.580 align:start size:88% position:13%
A mental health care
startup called Talkspace

02:35:23.613 --> 02:35:25.615
allows adult users
who pay a weekly fee

02:35:25.648 --> 02:35:27.751
to text therapists for advice.

02:35:27.784 --> 02:35:28.952
- [Advertiser] At Talkspace.

02:35:28.985 --> 02:35:31.722
We believe that therapy
should be anonymous.

02:35:31.755 --> 02:35:35.425 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
Stigma free, simple,
affordable and comfortable.

02:35:35.458 --> 02:35:37.461
- Texting can give
users the distance

02:35:37.494 --> 02:35:39.529
they need to be open and honest.

02:35:39.562 --> 02:35:42.432 align:start size:88% position:13%
And messages can be sent
when the user wants,

02:35:42.465 --> 02:35:45.135
not during an appointment
or business hours only

02:35:45.168 --> 02:35:48.806 align:start size:97% position:3%
- [Advertiser] Talkspace,
therapy for how we live today.

02:35:49.973 --> 02:35:51.408
- How am I?

02:35:51.441 --> 02:35:55.779
Better now that my
phone is working?

02:35:57.147 --> 02:35:58.548
(laughing)

02:35:58.581 --> 02:35:59.750
Sometimes, however,
we aren't looking

02:35:59.783 --> 02:36:01.818
for technology to comfort us.

02:36:01.851 --> 02:36:04.855 align:start size:97% position:3%
We're finding ourselves
wanting to comfort technology.

02:36:06.589 --> 02:36:09.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is a ROBOTIS OP2, cute
little fella, isn't he?

02:36:11.728 --> 02:36:13.797
So how did that make you feel?

02:36:13.830 --> 02:36:14.698
Bad?

02:36:14.731 --> 02:36:15.866
Well why?

02:36:15.899 --> 02:36:17.868
Robots are just machines,

02:36:17.901 --> 02:36:20.103 align:start size:81% position:19%
metal and wires
and computer chips,

02:36:20.136 --> 02:36:22.506
but we spend a lot of
time with technology.

02:36:23.740 --> 02:36:26.710
We depend on technology
and we care about it.

02:36:26.743 --> 02:36:27.978
But the degree to which

02:36:28.011 --> 02:36:30.414
we empathize with it
depends on context.

02:36:30.447 --> 02:36:32.816
(upbeat music)

02:36:34.584 --> 02:36:37.988
Recently, my Vsauce
was invaded by bugs,

02:36:38.021 --> 02:36:40.190
robot hexbugs that is.

02:36:40.223 --> 02:36:41.958
These bugs are made of plastic,

02:36:41.991 --> 02:36:45.695 align:start size:94% position:6%
metal electronic circuitry,
they aren't alive,

02:36:45.728 --> 02:36:46.897
but could certain conditions

02:36:46.930 --> 02:36:50.500
cause them to inspire
empathy in humans?

02:36:50.533 --> 02:36:54.671
A 2015 MIT study found that
giving a robot movement,

02:36:54.704 --> 02:36:56.673
a name and a personal backstory

02:36:56.706 --> 02:36:59.176
tends to increase its
anthropomorphic effect,

02:36:59.209 --> 02:37:02.112 align:start size:91% position:9%
which can lead to an
emotional can with humans.

02:37:02.145 --> 02:37:03.680
We decided to see
this in action.

02:37:03.713 --> 02:37:05.015 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm Michael.
- Nice to meet you.

02:37:05.048 --> 02:37:05.916
- Thank you for your help today.

02:37:05.949 --> 02:37:07.517
- Of course pleasure.

02:37:07.550 --> 02:37:09.052 align:start size:91% position:9%
- [Michael] In our
demonstration our subjects

02:37:09.085 --> 02:37:10.587
think they're focused testing

02:37:10.620 --> 02:37:12.722
a new user friendly technology.

02:37:12.755 --> 02:37:15.759
In this case, they're
given a lifeless hexbug

02:37:15.792 --> 02:37:17.928
and then asked to describe it.

02:37:17.961 --> 02:37:19.996
- This thing kind
of looks like a bug

02:37:20.029 --> 02:37:21.198
only I don't know what it does.

02:37:21.231 --> 02:37:22.966
It has a switch on the bottom.

02:37:22.999 --> 02:37:24.668
- It's light.

02:37:24.701 --> 02:37:27.237 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It's sort of a rectangle,
but the ends are like hexagon,

02:37:27.270 --> 02:37:29.773
- Then it was time
to test their empathy

02:37:29.806 --> 02:37:31.007 align:start size:81% position:19%
Akrino, what I
would like to do now

02:37:31.040 --> 02:37:34.077 align:start size:88% position:13%
is place the item in the
middle of that block,

02:37:34.110 --> 02:37:35.846
there's a magnet
that will hold it

02:37:35.879 --> 02:37:37.948 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I would like for
you to take this mallet.

02:37:39.249 --> 02:37:40.451
And please smash it.

02:37:41.150 --> 02:37:41.952
- Yeah, really?

02:37:41.985 --> 02:37:42.619
Okay, that's cool.

02:37:44.587 --> 02:37:45.622
- Our participants demonstrated

02:37:45.655 --> 02:37:48.825 align:start size:91% position:9%
no resistance to smashing
this lifeless object.

02:37:48.858 --> 02:37:50.928
Many of them even
seemed to enjoy it.

02:37:52.161 --> 02:37:54.698
Do you feel bad for breaking it?

02:37:54.731 --> 02:37:57.267
- Not really, I felt
indifferent to it.

02:37:57.300 --> 02:37:59.035 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Not really, because
it wasn't real.

02:37:59.068 --> 02:38:00.270
- Not really.

02:38:00.303 --> 02:38:01.905
- While these subjects exhibited

02:38:01.938 --> 02:38:04.508
no empathy to the
inanimate bugs.

02:38:04.541 --> 02:38:05.809
Look what happened when we gave

02:38:05.842 --> 02:38:08.845
the exact same bugs
names and movement.

02:38:08.878 --> 02:38:10.046
This is Margaret.

02:38:10.079 --> 02:38:11.248
Okay.

02:38:11.281 --> 02:38:12.382 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm gonna place
Margaret down here.

02:38:12.416 --> 02:38:15.285
I just want you to take a
moment to watch Margaret.

02:38:15.318 --> 02:38:16.686
All right.

02:38:16.719 --> 02:38:17.988 align:start size:81% position:19%
And you can feel
free to pick her up.

02:38:18.021 --> 02:38:19.823
She's really well behaved.

02:38:19.856 --> 02:38:21.625
She's honestly one
of our favorites.

02:38:21.658 --> 02:38:22.759
- [Woman] Okay.

02:38:22.793 --> 02:38:24.561 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So how would you describe
Margaret's personality?

02:38:25.828 --> 02:38:28.732
- A little erratic right now.

02:38:28.765 --> 02:38:30.667
But I think if I pick
her up, she calms down.

02:38:30.700 --> 02:38:31.935 align:start size:78% position:22%
- [Michael] Notice
how the subject

02:38:31.968 --> 02:38:34.104 align:start size:94% position:6%
has already
anthropomorphized the object

02:38:34.137 --> 02:38:35.772
referring to it as she.

02:38:35.805 --> 02:38:37.674
- Maybe she feeds off my energy.

02:38:37.707 --> 02:38:38.742
- Could be.

02:38:39.709 --> 02:38:42.012
- Go towards the light,
go towards the middle.

02:38:42.045 --> 02:38:43.780 align:start size:88% position:13%
- [Michael] Do you think
Margaret likes you?

02:38:43.813 --> 02:38:44.614
- Yeah.

02:38:44.647 --> 02:38:46.550
Maybe that's why
she's doing this.

02:38:46.583 --> 02:38:49.553
And maybe when I go like that
she doesn't act or all eratic.

02:38:50.219 --> 02:38:51.187
- That's Aaron.

02:38:51.220 --> 02:38:52.722
- Hi, Aaron.

02:38:52.755 --> 02:38:54.224
- He can be a bit of a pistol.

02:38:54.257 --> 02:38:55.659
- No way.

02:38:55.692 --> 02:38:57.761 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yes, it really depends
on who's holding him.

02:38:59.796 --> 02:39:01.331
(laughing)

02:39:01.364 --> 02:39:03.333
Oh, yeah, he's got
a lot of energy.

02:39:03.366 --> 02:39:04.834
Aaron.

02:39:04.867 --> 02:39:06.670
Hi.

02:39:06.703 --> 02:39:08.204 align:start position:0%
- [Michael] Now that you've
interacted with Eli a bit more,

02:39:08.237 --> 02:39:10.740 align:start size:84% position:16%
how would you describe
his personality?

02:39:10.773 --> 02:39:11.708
- Oh, he's just nervous.

02:39:11.741 --> 02:39:13.777
He's scared, he doesn't
know what's going on.

02:39:13.810 --> 02:39:14.711
- Hey, Joe.

02:39:14.745 --> 02:39:16.179
- Will these subjects
be just as willing

02:39:16.212 --> 02:39:17.714
to smash their bugs?

02:39:17.747 --> 02:39:20.784
Amy, I'm gonna place
Margaret right here.

02:39:22.218 --> 02:39:24.888
And then I would like for
you to take this mallet.

02:39:25.755 --> 02:39:27.224
And I'd like you to smash it.

02:39:28.825 --> 02:39:29.826
- No.

02:39:29.859 --> 02:39:32.129
I don't wanna hurt it.

02:39:33.196 --> 02:39:35.599 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Just take this
mallet and smash Aaron.

02:39:40.870 --> 02:39:42.872 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm gonna ask you
to take this mallet,

02:39:42.905 --> 02:39:47.777
