02:42:21.964 --> 02:42:23.933
all the objects hidden here.

02:42:23.966 --> 02:42:25.535
- [Dr. Stark] And time.

02:42:25.568 --> 02:42:27.137
- [Michael] Then my test began.

02:42:27.170 --> 02:42:28.905
- First object is
a rubber duck, go.

02:42:28.938 --> 02:42:31.441
- The rubber duck
was way over here.

02:42:31.474 --> 02:42:32.809
(laughing)

02:42:32.842 --> 02:42:34.077
How you like that?

02:42:34.110 --> 02:42:35.011
Got a duck.

02:42:35.044 --> 02:42:36.980
- Second item, is the hat go.

02:42:37.013 --> 02:42:39.182 align:start size:91% position:9%
- With this maze, I found
myself instinctively

02:42:39.215 --> 02:42:40.850
using a different approach.

02:42:40.883 --> 02:42:42.318
Top hat

02:42:42.351 --> 02:42:44.320 align:start size:97% position:3%
instead of thinking of the
overall geography of the maze,

02:42:44.353 --> 02:42:45.354
like I did last time.

02:42:45.388 --> 02:42:48.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
This time I was remembering
specific details.

02:42:48.457 --> 02:42:51.861
Second, right of
the turn, got it.

02:42:51.894 --> 02:42:54.497 align:start size:84% position:16%
Literally recalling
certain corners, turns

02:42:54.530 --> 02:42:55.565
and straightaways.

02:42:55.598 --> 02:42:56.833
Bonsai.

02:42:56.866 --> 02:42:58.034
- [Dr. Clemenson]
Now, blue base.

02:42:58.067 --> 02:43:00.370 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh, wow, it's
actually a cool vase.

02:43:00.403 --> 02:43:03.306
But would this improves
my overall performance?

02:43:03.339 --> 02:43:05.141 align:start size:78% position:22%
Got your backpack.
- All right.

02:43:05.174 --> 02:43:06.075
(laughing)

02:43:06.108 --> 02:43:07.310
So we've gotten all the objects.

02:43:07.343 --> 02:43:08.812 align:start size:84% position:16%
But of course, we have
another memory test

02:43:08.845 --> 02:43:10.547
that we're gonna do here.

02:43:10.580 --> 02:43:12.949
We're gonna go around to
the other side of the maze

02:43:12.982 --> 02:43:14.117
and test your memory from there.

02:43:14.150 --> 02:43:15.451 align:start size:88% position:13%
- All right.
- So your first sequence

02:43:15.484 --> 02:43:17.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
is the blue seahorse,
the flashlight,

02:43:17.920 --> 02:43:20.290 line:91%
the rubber duck and
the bonsai tree, go.

02:43:20.323 --> 02:43:21.558
(upbeat music)

02:43:21.591 --> 02:43:22.726
- [Michael] With the
multiple item tasks.

02:43:22.759 --> 02:43:25.528
Even though I was working
from the opposite entrance,

02:43:25.561 --> 02:43:28.231 align:start size:94% position:6%
I continued to recall
various details of the maze,

02:43:28.264 --> 02:43:30.233
which seemed to serve me well.

02:43:30.266 --> 02:43:33.403 align:start size:84% position:16%
From there, it's
just a little spiral.

02:43:33.436 --> 02:43:34.271
Nice.

02:43:36.572 --> 02:43:37.473 align:start size:91% position:9%
- [Dr. Clemenson] All
right, your next sequence

02:43:37.507 --> 02:43:40.109 align:start size:84% position:16%
is the blue vase,
the hat, the backpack

02:43:40.142 --> 02:43:41.544
and the baseball glove.

02:43:41.577 --> 02:43:42.478
- Did it.
- And time.

02:43:42.511 --> 02:43:43.913
- [Dr. Clemenson] Awesome.

02:43:43.946 --> 02:43:45.148
- So how was it?

02:43:45.181 --> 02:43:47.250
- That was not as
hard as I expected,

02:43:47.283 --> 02:43:49.185
It was about details.

02:43:49.218 --> 02:43:53.857 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was literally thinking
Oh, okay, there's that turn,

02:43:53.890 --> 02:43:54.925 align:start size:78% position:22%
and I could do
one or two things,

02:43:54.958 --> 02:43:58.094
the gloves the first one,
the bonsai even before

02:43:58.127 --> 02:43:59.929
I didn't even plan that at all,

02:43:59.962 --> 02:44:01.264
it just kind of happened.

02:44:01.297 --> 02:44:03.466
My look alikes were also
tested in the new maze.

02:44:03.499 --> 02:44:05.068 align:start size:81% position:19%
Have you been
playing video games?

02:44:05.101 --> 02:44:06.469
- [All] No, sir.

02:44:06.502 --> 02:44:08.571 align:start size:88% position:13%
- [Michael] Again, their
non gaming condition

02:44:08.604 --> 02:44:10.039
would be the control

02:44:10.072 --> 02:44:12.976 align:start size:88% position:13%
with my performance
measured against theirs.

02:44:14.110 --> 02:44:16.579
- All right, we're here
for scan number two.

02:44:16.612 --> 02:44:18.915 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Finally, my brain
was scanned once again,

02:44:18.948 --> 02:44:21.551 align:start size:97% position:3%
to determine whether any
physical changes had occurred.

02:44:21.584 --> 02:44:24.587 align:start size:94% position:6%
Dr. Stark and Dr. Clemenson
would analyze the MRI

02:44:24.620 --> 02:44:27.891
along with all the other data
and report their findings.

02:44:28.591 --> 02:44:30.493
Did you see my cats?

02:44:30.526 --> 02:44:32.295 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I did there's corn and
what's the other one?

02:44:32.328 --> 02:44:33.129
- Pickle.

02:44:33.162 --> 02:44:33.963
- Corn and Pickle.

02:44:33.996 --> 02:44:35.164
- Pickle, pickle.

02:44:35.197 --> 02:44:37.333
Pickle's the younger one.

02:44:37.366 --> 02:44:38.601
But we're not here to
talk about my cats.

02:44:38.634 --> 02:44:41.137
We're here to talk
about my hippocampus.

02:44:41.170 --> 02:44:44.474
Memory, comes in lots
of different forms.

02:44:44.507 --> 02:44:48.612 align:start position:0%
The hippocampus seems to be
very involved in spatial memory.

02:44:50.146 --> 02:44:53.950 align:start position:0%
- So the hippocampus is
involved in the spatial memory.

02:44:53.983 --> 02:44:57.120
And more broadly, what we
call short term memory.

02:44:57.153 --> 02:44:59.355
So it's not things that you're
keeping in your head at one.

02:44:59.388 --> 02:45:01.224
So if for example, you're
rehearsing phone number

02:45:01.257 --> 02:45:02.458
in your head over
and over again,

02:45:02.491 --> 02:45:04.060 align:start size:81% position:19%
that's what's called
working memory.

02:45:04.093 --> 02:45:06.095
So it's stuff that it's
consciously in your head,

02:45:06.128 --> 02:45:07.363
whereas short term memory

02:45:07.396 --> 02:45:09.098
is stuff that you
just encountered.

02:45:09.131 --> 02:45:10.633
So if you're thinking about

02:45:10.666 --> 02:45:12.402 align:start size:88% position:13%
what you had for
breakfast this morning,

02:45:12.435 --> 02:45:14.437
that's the hippocampus that
store that new information.

02:45:14.470 --> 02:45:16.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Where I parked my car,
where I put my water bottle,

02:45:16.739 --> 02:45:18.308
but like three weeks from now,

02:45:18.341 --> 02:45:20.043 align:start size:91% position:9%
I might not even remember
that I drink water

02:45:20.076 --> 02:45:21.010
in a bottle today.

02:45:21.043 --> 02:45:22.512
- Exactly.

02:45:22.545 --> 02:45:24.047
So what happens actually is
that while you're asleep,

02:45:24.080 --> 02:45:27.650
the hippocampus over the
course of days and weeks

02:45:27.683 --> 02:45:31.421
is actually offloading its
new memories into your cortex

02:45:31.454 --> 02:45:33.723
for long term storage
for long term memory,

02:45:33.756 --> 02:45:36.593
and actually kind of erases

02:45:37.727 --> 02:45:40.463
the recent memories that
you can store new ones.

02:45:40.496 --> 02:45:42.131 align:start size:81% position:19%
- How does it
decide what to store

02:45:42.164 --> 02:45:44.134
in long term memory
and what to delete?

02:45:45.334 --> 02:45:49.005
- Well, it's not so
understood, actually.

02:45:49.038 --> 02:45:51.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's not well understood
how it even does this,

02:45:51.540 --> 02:45:53.710
what we know is that
when you're asleep,

02:45:53.743 --> 02:45:55.678
there's hippocampal
replay what we call.

02:45:55.711 --> 02:45:58.681 align:start size:97% position:3%
So let's say for example,
you have some sort of pattern

02:45:58.714 --> 02:46:00.116
of activation in hippocampus

02:46:00.149 --> 02:46:02.118
that we know course
bonds to your memory,

02:46:02.151 --> 02:46:04.387
we can literally
see those neurons

02:46:04.420 --> 02:46:07.056 align:start size:91% position:9%
firing in the same pattern
when you're asleep.

02:46:07.089 --> 02:46:09.225
- So does that kind of

02:46:09.258 --> 02:46:12.495
explain what dreams might
be at least partially?

02:46:12.528 --> 02:46:14.597
- That is one theory
for what dreams are.

02:46:14.630 --> 02:46:18.634
So one worry if, for example,
your hippocampus is playing

02:46:18.667 --> 02:46:23.239 align:start size:97% position:3%
your new memories to your
cortex is that you might erase

02:46:23.272 --> 02:46:25.375
similar memories that had
been there for a long time.

02:46:25.408 --> 02:46:28.211
And again, this is not
well established science.

02:46:28.244 --> 02:46:29.445
This is just one theory

02:46:29.478 --> 02:46:33.049 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's emerged largely
from modeling work.

02:46:33.082 --> 02:46:36.452 align:start size:88% position:13%
But one idea is like
let's say, two days ago,

02:46:36.485 --> 02:46:40.456 align:start size:81% position:19%
you went to the
park with your wife.

02:46:40.489 --> 02:46:42.225 align:start size:88% position:13%
But you also went to
the park with your wife,

02:46:42.258 --> 02:46:43.726
let's say, a year ago,

02:46:43.759 --> 02:46:45.728
and there are some
similarities there.

02:46:45.761 --> 02:46:49.265 align:start position:0%
And so one idea is that dreaming
could be the reactivation

02:46:49.298 --> 02:46:50.433 align:start size:84% position:16%
of those old memories
in your cortex

02:46:50.466 --> 02:46:54.270 align:start size:94% position:6%
so as to basically keep them
from getting overwritten.

02:46:54.303 --> 02:46:55.705
- Wow, interesting.

02:46:55.738 --> 02:46:57.340
- [Daniel] Which is one theory.

02:46:57.373 --> 02:46:59.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
- It's just a theory,
sure, but I like that idea.

02:46:59.508 --> 02:47:01.311
And if you don't
get enough sleep,

02:47:02.178 --> 02:47:03.713
your memory will be affected.

02:47:03.746 --> 02:47:05.782
- Yeah, long term
memory for sure.

02:47:05.815 --> 02:47:06.749
So if you're sitting for a test,

02:47:06.782 --> 02:47:08.718
you really do need to sleep.

02:47:08.751 --> 02:47:10.520
So that those memories can be

02:47:10.553 --> 02:47:13.256 align:start size:91% position:9%
what we call consolidated
in your cortex.

02:47:13.289 --> 02:47:15.625
And also, this is why you
don't remember your dreams,

02:47:15.658 --> 02:47:18.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the thing that would
be doing the short term

02:47:18.627 --> 02:47:20.596
remembering is your hippocampus.

02:47:20.629 --> 02:47:22.665
But if it's playing your
memories to your cortex,

02:47:22.698 --> 02:47:24.167
while your dreaming,
it's not recording

02:47:24.200 --> 02:47:26.469
what's happening in your cortex.

02:47:26.502 --> 02:47:29.105 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Right, because it's
playing it for the cortex.

02:47:29.138 --> 02:47:30.840
- Exactly, so that the direction

02:47:30.873 --> 02:47:32.508
of information flow
has been reversed.

02:47:32.541 --> 02:47:35.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
So normally, it's the cortex
getting sensory inputs,

02:47:35.544 --> 02:47:38.281 align:start position:0%
and it goes to the hippocampus,
and that's storing it.

02:47:38.314 --> 02:47:40.683 align:start size:88% position:13%
But when you're dreaming
that information flow

02:47:40.716 --> 02:47:42.185
goes the other direction.

02:47:43.252 --> 02:47:44.420
- Fascinating.

02:47:44.453 --> 02:47:46.289
- Yeah. I mean, it's
still very theoretical.

02:47:46.322 --> 02:47:49.592
because dreaming, it's a
pretty hard thing to study

02:47:49.625 --> 02:47:52.195
because people aren't awake,
and you can't really test it.

02:47:52.228 --> 02:47:55.498
So, a lot of this comes
from making inferences

02:47:55.531 --> 02:47:57.734 align:start size:84% position:16%
from fMRI scans of
people who are asleep.

02:47:59.802 --> 02:48:02.405
And also studying the effects
asleep and dreaming on memory.

02:48:02.438 --> 02:48:03.639
- Just for clarity sake.

02:48:03.672 --> 02:48:06.175
Are you saying that
this theory posits

02:48:06.208 --> 02:48:09.212
that dreams might be
the hippocampus plane

02:48:09.245 --> 02:48:11.247 align:start size:81% position:19%
memories from short
term to cortex?

02:48:11.280 --> 02:48:12.448
- Yes, yeah.

02:48:12.481 --> 02:48:14.617
- Not cortex playing
long term memories back

02:48:14.650 --> 02:48:15.685
to the hippocampus.

02:48:15.718 --> 02:48:17.787
- Right, or maybe
even the cortex

02:48:17.820 --> 02:48:18.821
playing old memories to itself

02:48:18.855 --> 02:48:21.190 align:start size:91% position:9%
so it doesn't forget
them or get over written.

02:48:21.223 --> 02:48:22.692
- Okay, could be either, right.

02:48:22.725 --> 02:48:23.759
- [Daniel] Yeah, it's not
really well understood.

02:48:23.792 --> 02:48:25.595
- Of course, wow, how exciting.

02:48:25.628 --> 02:48:27.763 align:start size:81% position:19%
Maybe one of you out
there will become

02:48:27.796 --> 02:48:30.399
the final dream conqueror

02:48:30.432 --> 02:48:31.767 align:start size:88% position:13%
to answer all
questions about dreams.

02:48:31.800 --> 02:48:33.536
Probably not, though,
just to be honest,

02:48:33.569 --> 02:48:35.772 align:start size:91% position:9%
like, it's probably a
tougher nut to crack then.

02:48:36.739 --> 02:48:38.374
Or is it?

02:48:38.407 --> 02:48:39.909
- I mean, neuroscience
is advancing pretty fast.

02:48:39.942 --> 02:48:40.743
- Yeah.

02:48:40.776 --> 02:48:42.712
- So maybe one day soon.

02:48:42.745 --> 02:48:44.280
- Do you imagine
someday being like,

02:48:44.313 --> 02:48:45.781
dreams used to like, be weird.

02:48:45.814 --> 02:48:47.851 align:start size:84% position:16%
And people be like,
Wow, that's so crazy--

02:48:49.752 --> 02:48:51.154 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I mean, there's
so much in the brain

02:48:51.187 --> 02:48:53.222
that people used to
think of as mystical.

02:48:53.255 --> 02:48:54.624
So I think one great example,

02:48:54.657 --> 02:48:57.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
which isn't totally related
to this is epilepsy.

02:48:57.259 --> 02:48:59.929
People used to think that was
being possessed by a demon

02:48:59.962 --> 02:49:02.231
Whereas now we're like, how
do people ever think that?

02:49:02.264 --> 02:49:03.699
- Well, I've got a dream,

02:49:03.732 --> 02:49:05.301 align:start position:0%
and that dream is for us to
continue watching this episode.

02:49:05.334 --> 02:49:06.235
Are you ready?
- Let's do it.

02:49:06.268 --> 02:49:06.903
- Here we go.

02:49:13.709 --> 02:49:16.812 align:start size:91% position:9%
I feel like my hippocampus
is a little bit bigger.

02:49:16.845 --> 02:49:18.881
Yeah, actually,
no, I don't know.

02:49:18.914 --> 02:49:20.283
(laughing)

02:49:20.316 --> 02:49:22.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm anxious to see
what your results are.

02:49:22.718 --> 02:49:23.519
- I guess let's start off first

02:49:23.552 --> 02:49:25.888
with the object
recognition task.

02:49:25.921 --> 02:49:29.458
And it's important to know
that in our control tests,

02:49:29.491 --> 02:49:32.762
without video gaming, people
do not improve on this task.

02:49:32.795 --> 02:49:34.730
But your memory got better.

02:49:34.763 --> 02:49:36.632
You went up by 10 points.

02:49:36.665 --> 02:49:38.968 align:start size:84% position:16%
10 points is actually
20 years worth

02:49:39.001 --> 02:49:41.938
of what happens to us as we age.

02:49:41.971 --> 02:49:44.473 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Oh, wow, that's about
what you might see

02:49:44.506 --> 02:49:46.742
in someone who's
getting really old,

02:49:46.775 --> 02:49:48.344
but they might go
down by 10 point.

02:49:48.377 --> 02:49:49.445
- Exactly.

02:49:49.478 --> 02:49:50.613 align:start size:91% position:9%
- So the second one we did
was the virtual version

02:49:50.646 --> 02:49:51.981
of the water maze task,

02:49:52.014 --> 02:49:54.817 align:start size:81% position:19%
and you actually
performed 30% better

02:49:54.850 --> 02:49:56.419
the second time that you did it.

02:49:56.452 --> 02:49:57.853
- Aye, not bad, I could tell

02:49:57.886 --> 02:50:01.224
that I was using
better strategies.

02:50:01.257 --> 02:50:02.725
- Yeah.

02:50:02.758 --> 02:50:06.362
- We also had the real maze,
as you know we made two mazes.

02:50:06.395 --> 02:50:08.297 align:start size:84% position:16%
Despite our efforts
to try to equate them.

02:50:08.330 --> 02:50:09.732
the second maze was a little bit

02:50:09.765 --> 02:50:11.901 align:start size:81% position:19%
more difficult than
the first maze,

02:50:11.934 --> 02:50:15.304
if we took a look at
things like how quickly

02:50:15.337 --> 02:50:18.441 align:start size:88% position:13%
you got the objects,
how many errors you made

02:50:18.474 --> 02:50:20.443 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we looked at the
control subject performance,

02:50:20.476 --> 02:50:21.577
and pre versus post.

02:50:21.611 --> 02:50:24.714 align:start position:0%
So on all of them, they
actually got a little bit slower

02:50:24.747 --> 02:50:27.883 align:start size:97% position:3% line:91%
in maze two, and all that
one of them made more errors.

02:50:27.916 --> 02:50:30.820 align:start size:84% position:16% line:88%
We took a look at
your performance, you
didn't get slower,

02:50:30.853 --> 02:50:33.289 align:start size:88% position:13% line:91%
from maze one to two,
you actually got faster.

02:50:33.322 --> 02:50:34.557 line:92%
- [Michael] Really.

02:50:34.590 --> 02:50:36.559 line:91%
- And you made the same
exact number of errors,

02:50:36.592 --> 02:50:39.729 align:start size:84% position:16%
so they don't
improve, and you did.

02:50:39.762 --> 02:50:41.764
And even though this experiment

02:50:41.797 --> 02:50:43.733
had a small number of subjects,

02:50:43.766 --> 02:50:45.668
the results are
really consistent

02:50:45.701 --> 02:50:48.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
with our virtual maze,
study with 70 test subjects.

02:50:48.904 --> 02:50:51.941
- Thank you video games,
what about inside my brain?

02:50:51.974 --> 02:50:54.410 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Inside your brain, it's a
little tougher to really tell,

02:50:54.443 --> 02:50:57.780
we would expect that
any effect of this

02:50:57.813 --> 02:50:59.415
is going to be small.

02:50:59.448 --> 02:51:00.816
I mean, we couldn't
take your hippocampus

02:51:00.849 --> 02:51:02.551
and make it twice as big,

02:51:02.584 --> 02:51:04.754
because then it would have to
be pushing something else out.

02:51:04.787 --> 02:51:08.457
So it's just not going
to be a large change.

02:51:08.490 --> 02:51:09.892
So where we did
find a difference

02:51:09.925 --> 02:51:12.328 align:start size:88% position:13%
is actually in the shape
of the hippocampus,

02:51:12.361 --> 02:51:15.765 align:start size:97% position:3%
what we saw is there were some
regions in the hippocampus

02:51:15.798 --> 02:51:18.768 align:start size:94% position:6% line:76%
on both sides that appeared
to have changed shape

02:51:18.801 --> 02:51:22.438 line:76%
from day one before gaming
to day 10, after gaming.

02:51:22.471 --> 02:51:25.408
- What's really surprising
to me is that as an adult,

02:51:25.441 --> 02:51:27.310
my brain is still changing,

02:51:27.343 --> 02:51:29.745
that makes me wanna take
better care of my brain.

02:51:29.778 --> 02:51:30.980
Exercise it more,

02:51:31.013 --> 02:51:32.982
because it is a thing
that can change it.

02:51:33.015 --> 02:51:37.753 align:start size:91% position:9%
So I'm not just stuck with
what I have now, today.

02:51:37.786 --> 02:51:41.057
- I mean, in all of this, I
think that the big takeaway

02:51:41.090 --> 02:51:45.061
is that doing things, giving
your brain something to learn,

02:51:45.094 --> 02:51:48.831 align:start size:88% position:13%
something to do,
something to figure out,

02:51:48.864 --> 02:51:52.535 align:start size:84% position:16%
this is what we think
is actually keeping
your brain sharp.

02:51:52.568 --> 02:51:54.370
- One way to do that is to
keep watching Mind Field.

02:51:54.403 --> 02:51:55.104
- [Dr. Stark] Exactly.

02:51:55.137 --> 02:51:55.938
(laughing)

02:51:55.971 --> 02:51:58.708
(upbeat music)

02:52:01.744 --> 02:52:05.114 align:start size:84% position:16%
- As our relationship
with technology
becomes ever stronger,

02:52:05.147 --> 02:52:06.582
people are bound to worry

02:52:06.615 --> 02:52:08.818
about what it will
do to our brains,

02:52:08.851 --> 02:52:11.120 align:start size:81% position:19%
will offloading
memory and computing

02:52:11.153 --> 02:52:13.956
to our machines make us dumber?

02:52:13.989 --> 02:52:18.060
Will our empathy for machines
have negative consequences

02:52:18.093 --> 02:52:20.530
for how we interact
with each other?

02:52:21.630 --> 02:52:24.100 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, let's look
back to another time,

02:52:24.133 --> 02:52:26.135 align:start size:84% position:16%
a new kind of
technology threatened

02:52:26.168 --> 02:52:28.704
to fundamentally
change our brains.

02:52:28.737 --> 02:52:30.573 align:start size:81% position:19%
Two and a half
thousand years ago,

02:52:30.606 --> 02:52:32.708 align:start size:84% position:16%
the Greek philosopher
Socrates worried

02:52:32.741 --> 02:52:34.710
that the wide use of writing

02:52:34.743 --> 02:52:37.680 align:start size:91% position:9%
would have a negative
impact on people's minds.

02:52:37.713 --> 02:52:40.816
He said that writing would
to quote his student Plato,

02:52:40.849 --> 02:52:42.551
create forgetfulness,

02:52:42.584 --> 02:52:44.787 align:start size:88% position:13%
because people will
not use their memories,

02:52:44.820 --> 02:52:47.723 align:start size:94% position:6%
they will trust the
external written characters

02:52:47.756 --> 02:52:50.693
and not remember themselves.

02:52:50.726 --> 02:52:52.027
Socrates was right.

02:52:52.060 --> 02:52:55.598 align:start position:0%
Written language did
fundamentally change our brains.

02:52:55.631 --> 02:52:57.700
But it's also one
of the cornerstones

02:52:57.733 --> 02:53:00.469 align:start size:97% position:3%
of everything modern
civilization has accomplished.

02:53:00.502 --> 02:53:03.172 align:start size:97% position:3%
One of the defining
characteristics of being human

02:53:03.205 --> 02:53:06.509
is that this is not the
boundary of my body.

02:53:06.542 --> 02:53:08.911
And this is not the
boundary of my mind.

02:53:10.179 --> 02:53:12.615 align:start size:81% position:19%
And as always,
thanks for watching.

02:53:16.084 --> 02:53:17.820
There you go.

02:53:17.853 --> 02:53:19.989
Episode is over,
how do you feel?

02:53:21.089 --> 02:53:22.525 line:0%
- I feel good about it.

02:53:23.659 --> 02:53:26.028 line:0%
I liked what we covered
about the hippocampus

02:53:26.061 --> 02:53:29.698 align:start position:0% line:0%
and kind of getting at that
slightly counter intuitive idea

02:53:29.731 --> 02:53:31.867 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the hippocampus is
not just short term memory,

02:53:31.900 --> 02:53:35.070
but also spatial memory
and spatial navigation

02:53:35.103 --> 02:53:36.705
and how in our own cognition,

02:53:36.738 --> 02:53:38.507 align:start size:78% position:22%
those two are very
tied together.

02:53:38.540 --> 02:53:39.575
- Whoo, right.

02:53:39.608 --> 02:53:41.844 align:start size:91% position:9%
You had mentioned earlier
about memory palaces?

02:53:41.877 --> 02:53:43.078
- Yeah, yeah.

02:53:43.111 --> 02:53:44.780
So have you ever
watched Sherlock?

02:53:44.813 --> 02:53:46.215
- I love Sherlock,

02:53:46.248 --> 02:53:47.883 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've written plenty of
fanfiction about that show.

02:53:47.916 --> 02:53:50.719 align:start position:0%
- There is this recurring
trope in the show where he goes

02:53:50.752 --> 02:53:52.955
into I think he calls it
his memory palace, right?

02:53:52.988 --> 02:53:53.789
- Right. Right, right.

02:53:53.822 --> 02:53:55.191
- Memory palaces comes

02:53:55.224 --> 02:53:58.994
from this very ancient
memorization technique,

02:53:59.027 --> 02:54:03.098
that's actually it's been
used since ancient Greece,

02:54:03.131 --> 02:54:06.635 align:start size:91% position:9%
where I think they call
it the method of low side.

02:54:06.668 --> 02:54:08.737
And the reason it works.

02:54:08.770 --> 02:54:11.140
So I should explain the
method is basically,

02:54:11.173 --> 02:54:15.144 align:start position:0%
you imagine some two dimensional
three dimensional space

02:54:15.177 --> 02:54:17.746
that you're familiar
with, like your house,

02:54:17.779 --> 02:54:20.683 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you place an
object to be remembered

02:54:20.716 --> 02:54:22.785 align:start size:84% position:16%
in different locations
in the house.

02:54:22.818 --> 02:54:24.987
So for example, let's say
you walk into the kitchen.

02:54:25.020 --> 02:54:28.524 align:start size:91% position:9%
So in the kitchen, there's
these set of facts,

02:54:28.557 --> 02:54:29.258
and you go into your bedroom.

02:54:29.291 --> 02:54:30.426
And there's these set of facts.

02:54:30.459 --> 02:54:33.529
And the reason this works
is because your hippocampus

02:54:33.562 --> 02:54:37.700 align:start size:97% position:3%
is very spatial, it organizes
information spatially.

02:54:37.733 --> 02:54:41.804
And this actually has an
impact on our cognition.

02:54:41.837 --> 02:54:45.741
So it's also involved in, for
example, our sense of time,

02:54:45.774 --> 02:54:47.209
which is tied to
our sense of space.

02:54:47.242 --> 02:54:50.546
So do you ever like
walk into a new room

02:54:50.579 --> 02:54:51.881
and then you forget
why you're there?

02:54:51.914 --> 02:54:53.115
- Oh, totally, yeah.

02:54:53.148 --> 02:54:54.550
- That's because of
your hippocampus,

02:54:54.583 --> 02:54:57.152 align:start size:84% position:16%
your hippocampus, tied
that mental context

02:54:57.185 --> 02:55:00.589 align:start size:91% position:9%
and certain memories to
the room you're in before,

02:55:00.622 --> 02:55:01.857
now you walk into a new room,

02:55:01.890 --> 02:55:03.959
you're in a new spatial context,

02:55:03.992 --> 02:55:07.196
and that context isn't
attached to this memory.

02:55:07.229 --> 02:55:08.064
- Wow!

02:55:09.298 --> 02:55:10.466
Fascinating, yeah.

02:55:10.499 --> 02:55:13.802 align:start position:0%
Because it's always kind of
you realize that you don't know

02:55:13.835 --> 02:55:15.237
why you're there
at the same moment

02:55:15.270 --> 02:55:16.839
that you realize you're there.

02:55:16.872 --> 02:55:18.774 align:start size:84% position:16%
Then you're like,
all right, new place,

02:55:18.807 --> 02:55:20.910 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we lost everything
that was associated

02:55:20.943 --> 02:55:23.245
with the last place, didn't
we shoot, why am I here?

02:55:23.278 --> 02:55:24.947 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Yep, and there's
also research,

02:55:26.114 --> 02:55:27.983 align:start size:88% position:13%
showing that this can
warp your sense of time.

02:55:28.016 --> 02:55:30.786
So this actually research I did

02:55:30.819 --> 02:55:32.788
back when I was an undergrad,

02:55:32.821 --> 02:55:35.724 align:start size:91% position:9%
on how the hippocampus
and other related regions,

02:55:35.757 --> 02:55:38.827
they encode our sense
of the passage of time,

02:55:38.860 --> 02:55:41.063
or at least our memory for
how much time has passed.

02:55:41.096 --> 02:55:43.733 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so if you constantly
switch context,

02:55:45.100 --> 02:55:47.903 align:start position:0%
for example, by changing rooms,
or even changing the kinds

02:55:47.936 --> 02:55:49.572
of things you're thinking about,

02:55:49.605 --> 02:55:51.874 align:start size:91% position:9%
you're gonna remember more
time as having passed.

02:55:51.907 --> 02:55:53.776
- Huh, interesting.

02:55:53.809 --> 02:55:54.643
Really?

02:55:54.677 --> 02:55:55.644 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah, and you
can manipulate this

02:55:55.678 --> 02:55:57.646
by even having someone
walk into a new room,

02:55:57.679 --> 02:55:59.615
which is a way of
changing context.

02:55:59.648 --> 02:56:02.351 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right, and so they will
think that it's been longer.

02:56:02.384 --> 02:56:04.119 align:start size:88% position:13%
- They'll remember, more
time as having past.

02:56:04.152 --> 02:56:06.655
So your memory of time passing

02:56:06.688 --> 02:56:09.158
isn't exactly the same as
your current experience

02:56:09.191 --> 02:56:10.359
of time passing.

02:56:10.392 --> 02:56:11.627
- Of course, yeah, I
couldn't feel like man,

02:56:11.660 --> 02:56:13.996
this is so boring, later on.

02:56:14.029 --> 02:56:15.698 align:start size:84% position:16%
I basically feel
like it took a second

02:56:15.731 --> 02:56:18.000
because I've got so few
memories from there.

02:56:18.033 --> 02:56:20.336 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's what it was like
being in isolation,

02:56:20.369 --> 02:56:22.838
in season one episode
one of Mind Field.

02:56:22.871 --> 02:56:26.375
I have like, no memories
from those three days,

02:56:26.408 --> 02:56:27.910
because nothing happened,

02:56:27.943 --> 02:56:30.012 align:start size:81% position:19%
I just stared at
white walls all day.

02:56:30.045 --> 02:56:32.181 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And how do you
remember time passing

02:56:32.214 --> 02:56:34.116
when you were in there?

02:56:34.149 --> 02:56:36.885 align:start size:91% position:9%
- It didn't pass, that
was the other weird thing.

02:56:36.918 --> 02:56:39.855 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's this whole soliloquy
I gave halfway through,

02:56:39.888 --> 02:56:42.825 align:start size:97% position:3%
I believe my stay in that room
that isn't in the episode.

02:56:42.858 --> 02:56:45.060
But it was something like, wow,

02:56:45.093 --> 02:56:46.762
I'm not even scared
of death anymore.

02:56:46.795 --> 02:56:48.130 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because there's nothing
to look forward to

02:56:48.163 --> 02:56:49.331
every moment is the same.

02:56:49.364 --> 02:56:53.002
I wonder if this is how a bug
feels that you just exist,

02:56:53.035 --> 02:56:54.870
and there's now and
that's all there is.

02:56:54.903 --> 02:56:56.973
And so death isn't scary.

02:56:58.674 --> 02:56:59.975
It was a crazy experiment.

02:57:00.008 --> 02:57:01.877
- A data bug would be
having those though.

02:57:01.910 --> 02:57:03.912
- I know, I know but
I'm just thinking,

02:57:03.945 --> 02:57:05.414 align:start size:88% position:13%
what is the
consciousness of a bug?

02:57:05.447 --> 02:57:08.717
Like if it exists, and it might
not plan for the future much

02:57:08.750 --> 02:57:10.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
It might not
remember how long ago

02:57:10.886 --> 02:57:12.988
it did that other
thing in the past.

02:57:13.021 --> 02:57:16.859
So it's just kind of the
world exists right now.

02:57:16.892 --> 02:57:19.395
But yeah, because I have
no memories from that.

02:57:19.428 --> 02:57:20.563
It just felt like it
happened like this.

02:57:20.596 --> 02:57:23.732
But if I look at what I've
done in the last three days,

02:57:23.765 --> 02:57:25.334
especially now as a new dad,

02:57:25.367 --> 02:57:29.071
it feels like that's
so much time passed

02:57:29.104 --> 02:57:31.407
forever has passed,
since three days ago.

02:57:31.440 --> 02:57:32.875
- Yep.

02:57:32.908 --> 02:57:35.444
So we actually did, this
wasn't the hippocampus,

02:57:35.477 --> 02:57:37.246
but it was a region
right next to it

02:57:37.279 --> 02:57:39.848 align:start size:94% position:6%
called the internal cortex,
which is kind of the,

02:57:39.881 --> 02:57:40.716
that's

02:57:42.150 --> 02:57:43.385
the part of the cortex

02:57:43.418 --> 02:57:45.921
that feeds information
into the hippocampus.

02:57:45.954 --> 02:57:49.058 align:start size:88% position:13%
So we looked at how
activity there predicts

02:57:49.091 --> 02:57:50.993 align:start size:84% position:16%
people sense or memory
for time passing.

02:57:51.026 --> 02:57:53.862
And so what we did is we
put people in the scanner,

02:57:53.895 --> 02:57:56.298
and they listened
to a short story.

02:57:56.331 --> 02:57:58.867
And after they left the scanner,

02:57:58.900 --> 02:58:00.869
we then would play two
clips from the story

02:58:00.902 --> 02:58:02.237
and we asked them how
much time do remember

02:58:02.270 --> 02:58:05.074 align:start size:78% position:22%
passing between
clip A and clip B?

02:58:05.107 --> 02:58:07.309 align:start size:91% position:9%
And what we found was
the more activity changed

02:58:07.342 --> 02:58:10.112 align:start size:84% position:16%
in their internal
cortex in that period,

02:58:10.145 --> 02:58:13.449
the more time they remembered
passing between those two.

02:58:13.482 --> 02:58:14.883
- Wow, fascinating.

02:58:14.916 --> 02:58:16.752
- The idea being literally

02:58:16.785 --> 02:58:20.723
there was more stuff changing
in their head, in that period.

02:58:20.756 --> 02:58:22.357
- So the perception
of time passing,

02:58:22.390 --> 02:58:24.927
the memory of time
passing, dreams,

02:58:24.960 --> 02:58:27.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
we've got a lot of
material for a season four,

02:58:27.996 --> 02:58:29.732
what do you say YouTube?

02:58:29.765 --> 02:58:30.866
(laughing)

02:58:30.899 --> 02:58:32.034
- All about the hippocampus,

02:58:32.067 --> 02:58:33.836
- All about them, I would
love to do a themed season.

02:58:33.869 --> 02:58:35.771 align:start size:78% position:22%
Just every episode
is about fear,

02:58:35.804 --> 02:58:38.207 align:start size:84% position:16%
or every episode is
about the hippocampus.

02:58:38.240 --> 02:58:39.909
Or the cerebellum, I don't care.

02:58:41.076 --> 02:58:42.277
But there's a lot of
cool stuff out there.

02:58:42.310 --> 02:58:44.213
And Daniel, it's always
a pleasure to have you

02:58:44.246 --> 02:58:45.781 align:start size:78% position:22%
come and share
all of your ideas.

